{"surah": 23, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "قَدْ أَفْلَحَ ٱلْمُؤْمِنُونَ ", "translation": "Certainly will the believers have succeeded:", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَدْ", "translit": "ْdَq", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَفْلَحَ", "translit": "َḥَlْfَaʾ", "root": "فلح"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْمُؤْمِنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnِmْwʾُmْlٱ", "root": "امن"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "ٱلَّذِينَ هُمْ فِى صَلَاتِهِمْ خَٰشِعُونَ ", "translation": "They who are during their prayer humbly intent", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هُمْ", "translit": "ْmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "صَلَاتِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِtāَlَṣ", "root": "صلو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "خَٰشِعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعِhsَٰhk", "root": "خشع"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "وَٱلَّذِينَ هُمْ عَنِ ٱللَّغْوِ مُعْرِضُونَ ", "translation": "And they who turn away from ill speech", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هُمْ", "translit": "ْmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَنِ", "translit": "ِnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّغْوِ", "translit": "ِwْغَّllٱ", "root": "لغو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مُعْرِضُونَ", "translit": "َnwُḍِrْʿعُm", "root": "عرض"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "وَٱلَّذِينَ هُمْ لِلزَّكَوٰةِ فَٰعِلُونَ ", "translation": "And they who are observant of zakāh", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هُمْ", "translit": "ْmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لِلزَّكَوٰةِ", "translit": "ِẗٰwَkَّzlِl", "root": "زكو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَٰعِلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlِʿعَٰf", "root": "فعل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 5, "text": "وَٱلَّذِينَ هُمْ لِفُرُوجِهِمْ حَٰفِظُونَ ", "translation": "And they who guard their private parts", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هُمْ", "translit": "ْmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لِفُرُوجِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِjgwُrُfِl", "root": "فرج"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "حَٰفِظُونَ", "translit": "َnwُẓِfَٰḥ", "root": "حفظ"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 6, "text": "إِلَّا عَلَىٰٓ أَزْوَٰجِهِمْ أَوْ مَا مَلَكَتْ أَيْمَٰنُهُمْ فَإِنَّهُمْ غَيْرُ مَلُومِينَ ", "translation": "Except from their wives or those their right hands possess,[1] for indeed, they will not be blamed -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَلَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَزْوَٰجِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِjgَٰwْzَaʾ", "root": "زوج"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مَلَكَتْ", "translit": "ْtَkَlَm", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَيْمَٰنُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُnَٰmْyَaʾ", "root": "يمن"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَإِنَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّnِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "غَيْرُ", "translit": "ُrْyَغ", "root": "غير"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مَلُومِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmwُlَm", "root": "لوم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 7, "text": "فَمَنِ ٱبْتَغَىٰ وَرَآءَ ذَٰلِكَ فَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ هُمُ ٱلْعَادُونَ ", "translation": "But whoever seeks beyond that, then those are the transgressors -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَمَنِ", "translit": "ِnَmَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱبْتَغَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَغَtْbٱ", "root": "بغي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَرَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَrَw", "root": "وري"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "هُمُ", "translit": "ُmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْعَادُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdāَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عدو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 8, "text": "وَٱلَّذِينَ هُمْ لِأَمَٰنَٰتِهِمْ وَعَهْدِهِمْ رَٰعُونَ ", "translation": "And they who are to their trusts and their promises attentive", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هُمْ", "translit": "ْmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لِأَمَٰنَٰتِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِtَٰnَٰmَaʾِl", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَعَهْدِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِdْhَʿعَw", "root": "عهد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "رَٰعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعَٰr", "root": "رعي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 9, "text": "وَٱلَّذِينَ هُمْ عَلَىٰ صَلَوَٰتِهِمْ يُحَافِظُونَ ", "translation": "And they who carefully maintain their prayers -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هُمْ", "translit": "ْmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "صَلَوَٰتِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِtَٰwَlَṣ", "root": "صلو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يُحَافِظُونَ", "translit": "َnwُẓِfāَḥُy", "root": "حفظ"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 10, "text": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ هُمُ ٱلْوَٰرِثُونَ ", "translation": "Those are the inheritors", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هُمُ", "translit": "ُmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْوَٰرِثُونَ", "translit": "َnwُhtِrَٰwْlٱ", "root": "ورث"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 11, "text": "ٱلَّذِينَ يَرِثُونَ ٱلْفِرْدَوْسَ هُمْ فِيهَا خَٰلِدُونَ ", "translation": "Who will inherit al-Firdaus.[1] They will abide therein eternally.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَرِثُونَ", "translit": "َnwُhtِrَy", "root": "ورث"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْفِرْدَوْسَ", "translit": "َsْwَdْrِfْlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "هُمْ", "translit": "ْmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "خَٰلِدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdِlَٰhk", "root": "خلد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 12, "text": "وَلَقَدْ خَلَقْنَا ٱلْإِنسَٰنَ مِن سُلَٰلَةٍ مِّن طِينٍ ", "translation": "And certainly did We create man from an extract of clay.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "خَلَقْنَا", "translit": "āَnْqَlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْإِنسَٰنَ", "translit": "َnَٰsnِiʾْlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "سُلَٰلَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَlَٰlُs", "root": "سلل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "طِينٍ", "translit": "ٍnyِṭ", "root": "طين"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 13, "text": "ثُمَّ جَعَلْنَٰهُ نُطْفَةً فِى قَرَارٍ مَّكِينٍ ", "translation": "Then We placed him as a sperm-drop[1] in a firm lodging [i.e., the womb].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "جَعَلْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْlَʿعَjg", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "نُطْفَةً", "translit": "ًẗَfْṭُn", "root": "نطف"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَرَارٍ", "translit": "ٍrāَrَq", "root": "قرر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مَّكِينٍ", "translit": "ٍnyِkَّm", "root": "مكن"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 14, "text": "ثُمَّ خَلَقْنَا ٱلنُّطْفَةَ عَلَقَةً فَخَلَقْنَا ٱلْعَلَقَةَ مُضْغَةً فَخَلَقْنَا ٱلْمُضْغَةَ عِظَٰمًا فَكَسَوْنَا ٱلْعِظَٰمَ لَحْمًا ثُمَّ أَنشَأْنَٰهُ خَلْقًا ءَاخَرَۚ فَتَبَارَكَ ٱللَّهُ أَحْسَنُ ٱلْخَٰلِقِينَ ", "translation": "Then We made the sperm-drop into a clinging clot, and We made the clot into a lump [of flesh], and We made [from] the lump, bones, and We covered the bones with flesh; then We developed him into another creation. So blessed is Allāh, the best of creators.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "خَلَقْنَا", "translit": "āَnْqَlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلنُّطْفَةَ", "translit": "َẗَfْṭُّnlٱ", "root": "نطف"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَلَقَةً", "translit": "ًẗَqَlَʿع", "root": "علق"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَخَلَقْنَا", "translit": "āَnْqَlَhkَf", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْعَلَقَةَ", "translit": "َẗَqَlَʿعْlٱ", "root": "علق"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مُضْغَةً", "translit": "ًẗَغْḍُm", "root": "مضغ"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَخَلَقْنَا", "translit": "āَnْqَlَhkَf", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْمُضْغَةَ", "translit": "َẗَغْḍُmْlٱ", "root": "مضغ"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "عِظَٰمًا", "translit": "āًmَٰẓِʿع", "root": "عظم"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "فَكَسَوْنَا", "translit": "āَnْwَsَkَf", "root": "كسو"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْعِظَٰمَ", "translit": "َmَٰẓِʿعْlٱ", "root": "عظم"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "لَحْمًا", "translit": "āًmْḥَl", "root": "لحم"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "أَنشَأْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْaʾَhsnَaʾ", "root": "نشا"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "خَلْقًا", "translit": "āًqْlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ءَاخَرَ", "translit": "َrَhkāَء", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "فَتَبَارَكَ", "translit": "َkَrāَbَtَf", "root": "برك"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "أَحْسَنُ", "translit": "ُnَsْḥَaʾ", "root": "حسن"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "ٱلْخَٰلِقِينَ", "translit": "َnyِqِlَٰhkْlٱ", "root": "خلق"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 15, "text": "ثُمَّ إِنَّكُم بَعْدَ ذَٰلِكَ لَمَيِّتُونَ ", "translation": "Then indeed, after that you are to die.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِنَّكُم", "translit": "mُkَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بَعْدَ", "translit": "َdْʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَمَيِّتُونَ", "translit": "َnwُtِّyَmَl", "root": "موت"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 16, "text": "ثُمَّ إِنَّكُمْ يَوْمَ ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ تُبْعَثُونَ ", "translation": "Then indeed you, on the Day of Resurrection, will be resurrected.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِنَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَmَٰyِqْlٱ", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "تُبْعَثُونَ", "translit": "َnwُhtَʿعْbُt", "root": "بعث"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 17, "text": "وَلَقَدْ خَلَقْنَا فَوْقَكُمْ سَبْعَ طَرَآئِقَ وَمَا كُنَّا عَنِ ٱلْخَلْقِ غَٰفِلِينَ ", "translation": "And We have created above you seven layered heavens, and never have We been of [Our] creation unaware.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "خَلَقْنَا", "translit": "āَnْqَlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَوْقَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَqْwَf", "root": "فوق"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "سَبْعَ", "translit": "َʿعْbَs", "root": "سبع"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "طَرَآئِقَ", "translit": "َqِyʾٓāَrَṭ", "root": "طرق"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "كُنَّا", "translit": "āَّnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَنِ", "translit": "ِnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْخَلْقِ", "translit": "ِqْlَhkْlٱ", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "غَٰفِلِينَ", "translit": "َnyِlِfَٰغ", "root": "غفل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 18, "text": "وَأَنزَلْنَا مِنَ ٱلسَّمَآءِ مَآءًۢ بِقَدَرٍ فَأَسْكَنَّٰهُ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِۖ وَإِنَّا عَلَىٰ ذَهَابٍۭ بِهِۦ لَقَٰدِرُونَ ", "translation": "And We have sent down rain from the sky in a measured amount and settled it in the earth. And indeed, We are Able to take it away.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَنزَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَznَaʾَw", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَآءًۢ", "translit": "ًۢءٓāَm", "root": "موه"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِقَدَرٍ", "translit": "ٍrَdَqِb", "root": "قدر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَأَسْكَنَّٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَّٰnَkْsَaʾَf", "root": "سكن"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَإِنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ذَهَابٍۭ", "translit": "ٍۭbāَhَhd", "root": "ذهب"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "لَقَٰدِرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrِdَٰqَl", "root": "قدر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 19, "text": "فَأَنشَأْنَا لَكُم بِهِۦ جَنَّٰتٍ مِّن نَّخِيلٍ وَأَعْنَٰبٍ لَّكُمْ فِيهَا فَوَٰكِهُ كَثِيرَةٌ وَمِنْهَا تَأْكُلُونَ ", "translation": "And We brought forth for you thereby gardens of palm trees and grapevines in which for you are abundant fruits and from which you eat.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَأَنشَأْنَا", "translit": "āَnْaʾَhsnَaʾَf", "root": "نشا"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَكُم", "translit": "mُkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "جَنَّٰتٍ", "translit": "ٍtَّٰnَjg", "root": "جنن"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "نَّخِيلٍ", "translit": "ٍlyِhkَّn", "root": "نخل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَأَعْنَٰبٍ", "translit": "ٍbَٰnْʿعَaʾَw", "root": "عنب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَوَٰكِهُ", "translit": "ُhِkَٰwَf", "root": "فكه"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "كَثِيرَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَryِhtَk", "root": "كثر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَمِنْهَا", "translit": "āَhْnِmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "تَأْكُلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlُkْaʾَt", "root": "اكل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 20, "text": "وَشَجَرَةً تَخْرُجُ مِن طُورِ سَيْنَآءَ تَنۢبُتُ بِٱلدُّهْنِ وَصِبْغٍ لِّلْءَاكِلِينَ ", "translation": "And [We brought forth] a tree issuing from Mount Sinai which produces oil and food [i.e., olives] for those who eat.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَشَجَرَةً", "translit": "ًẗَrَjgَhsَw", "root": "شجر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَخْرُجُ", "translit": "ُjgُrْhkَt", "root": "خرج"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "طُورِ", "translit": "ِrwُṭ", "root": "طور"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "سَيْنَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَnْyَs", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "تَنۢبُتُ", "translit": "ُtُbۢnَt", "root": "نبت"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بِٱلدُّهْنِ", "translit": "ِnْhُّdlٱِb", "root": "دهن"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَصِبْغٍ", "translit": "ٍغْbِṣَw", "root": "صبغ"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لِّلْءَاكِلِينَ", "translit": "َnyِlِkāَءْlِّl", "root": "اكل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 21, "text": "وَإِنَّ لَكُمْ فِى ٱلْأَنْعَٰمِ لَعِبْرَةًۖ نُّسْقِيكُم مِّمَّا فِى بُطُونِهَا وَلَكُمْ فِيهَا مَنَٰفِعُ كَثِيرَةٌ وَمِنْهَا تَأْكُلُونَ ", "translation": "And indeed, for you in livestock is a lesson. We give you drink from that which is in their bellies, and for you in them are numerous benefits, and from them you eat.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْأَنْعَٰمِ", "translit": "ِmَٰʿعْnَaʾْlٱ", "root": "نعم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَعِبْرَةً", "translit": "ًẗَrْbِʿعَl", "root": "عبر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "نُّسْقِيكُم", "translit": "mُkyِqْsُّn", "root": "سقي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِّمَّا", "translit": "āَّmِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بُطُونِهَا", "translit": "āَhِnwُṭُb", "root": "بطن"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَلَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مَنَٰفِعُ", "translit": "ُʿعِfَٰnَm", "root": "نفع"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "كَثِيرَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَryِhtَk", "root": "كثر"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَمِنْهَا", "translit": "āَhْnِmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "تَأْكُلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlُkْaʾَt", "root": "اكل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 22, "text": "وَعَلَيْهَا وَعَلَى ٱلْفُلْكِ تُحْمَلُونَ ", "translation": "And upon them and on ships you are carried.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَعَلَيْهَا", "translit": "āَhْyَlَʿعَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَعَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿعَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْفُلْكِ", "translit": "ِkْlُfْlٱ", "root": "فلك"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تُحْمَلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlَmْḥُt", "root": "حمل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 23, "text": "وَلَقَدْ أَرْسَلْنَا نُوحًا إِلَىٰ قَوْمِهِۦ فَقَالَ يَٰقَوْمِ ٱعْبُدُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ مَا لَكُم مِّنْ إِلَٰهٍ غَيْرُهُۥٓۖ أَفَلَا تَتَّقُونَ ", "translation": "And We had certainly sent Noah to his people, and he said, \"O my people, worship Allāh; you have no deity other than Him; then will you not fear Him?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَرْسَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَsْrَaʾ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "نُوحًا", "translit": "āًḥwُn", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَوْمِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِmْwَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَقَالَ", "translit": "َlāَqَf", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَٰقَوْمِ", "translit": "ِmْwَqَٰy", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱعْبُدُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdُbْʿعٱ", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لَكُم", "translit": "mُkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مِّنْ", "translit": "ْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "إِلَٰهٍ", "translit": "ٍhَٰlِiʾ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "غَيْرُهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhُrْyَغ", "root": "غير"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "أَفَلَا", "translit": "āَlَfَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "تَتَّقُونَ", "translit": "َnwُqَّtَt", "root": "وقي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 24, "text": "فَقَالَ ٱلْمَلَؤُا۟ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ مِن قَوْمِهِۦ مَا هَٰذَآ إِلَّا بَشَرٌ مِّثْلُكُمْ يُرِيدُ أَن يَتَفَضَّلَ عَلَيْكُمْ وَلَوْ شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ لَأَنزَلَ مَلَٰٓئِكَةً مَّا سَمِعْنَا بِهَٰذَا فِىٓ ءَابَآئِنَا ٱلْأَوَّلِينَ ", "translation": "But the eminent among those who disbelieved from his people said, \"This is not but a man like yourselves who wishes to take precedence over you; and if Allāh had willed [to send a messenger], He would have sent down angels. We have not heard of this among our forefathers.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَقَالَ", "translit": "َlāَqَf", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْمَلَؤُا۟", "translit": "۟āُwʾَlَmْlٱ", "root": "ملا"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "قَوْمِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِmْwَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "هَٰذَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بَشَرٌ", "translit": "ٌrَhsَb", "root": "بشر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مِّثْلُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُlْhtِّm", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "يُرِيدُ", "translit": "ُdyِrُy", "root": "رود"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "يَتَفَضَّلَ", "translit": "َlَّḍَfَtَy", "root": "فضل"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "عَلَيْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَلَوْ", "translit": "ْwَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "شَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "لَأَنزَلَ", "translit": "َlَznَaʾَl", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "مَلَٰٓئِكَةً", "translit": "ًẗَkِyʾَٰٓlَm", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "مَّا", "translit": "āَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "سَمِعْنَا", "translit": "āَnْʿعِmَs", "root": "سمع"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "بِهَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "فِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "ءَابَآئِنَا", "translit": "āَnِyʾٓāَbāَء", "root": "ابو"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "ٱلْأَوَّلِينَ", "translit": "َnyِlَّwَaʾْlٱ", "root": "اول"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 25, "text": "إِنْ هُوَ إِلَّا رَجُلٌۢ بِهِۦ جِنَّةٌ فَتَرَبَّصُوا۟ بِهِۦ حَتَّىٰ حِينٍ ", "translation": "He is not but a man possessed with madness, so wait concerning him for a time.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنْ", "translit": "ْnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "رَجُلٌۢ", "translit": "ٌۢlُjgَr", "root": "رجل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "جِنَّةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَّnِjg", "root": "جنن"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَتَرَبَّصُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُṣَّbَrَtَf", "root": "ربص"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "حَتَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّtَḥ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "حِينٍ", "translit": "ٍnyِḥ", "root": "حين"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 26, "text": "قَالَ رَبِّ ٱنصُرْنِى بِمَا كَذَّبُونِ ", "translation": "[Noah] said, \"My Lord, support me because they have denied me.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَبِّ", "translit": "ِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱنصُرْنِى", "translit": "àِnْrُṣnٱ", "root": "نصر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "كَذَّبُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُbَّhdَk", "root": "كذب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 27, "text": "فَأَوْحَيْنَآ إِلَيْهِ أَنِ ٱصْنَعِ ٱلْفُلْكَ بِأَعْيُنِنَا وَوَحْيِنَا فَإِذَا جَآءَ أَمْرُنَا وَفَارَ ٱلتَّنُّورُۙ فَٱسْلُكْ فِيهَا مِن كُلٍّ زَوْجَيْنِ ٱثْنَيْنِ وَأَهْلَكَ إِلَّا مَن سَبَقَ عَلَيْهِ ٱلْقَوْلُ مِنْهُمْۖ وَلَا تُخَٰطِبْنِى فِى ٱلَّذِينَ ظَلَمُوٓا۟ۖ إِنَّهُم مُّغْرَقُونَ ", "translation": "So We inspired to him, \"Construct the ship under Our observation and Our inspiration, and when Our command comes and the oven overflows,[1] put into it [i.e., the ship] from each [creature] two mates and your family, except him for whom the decree [of destruction] has proceeded. And do not address Me concerning those who have wronged; indeed, they are to be drowned.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَأَوْحَيْنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnْyَḥْwَaʾَf", "root": "وحي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَنِ", "translit": "ِnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱصْنَعِ", "translit": "ِʿعَnْṣٱ", "root": "صنع"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْفُلْكَ", "translit": "َkْlُfْlٱ", "root": "فلك"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بِأَعْيُنِنَا", "translit": "āَnِnُyْʿعَaʾِb", "root": "عين"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَوَحْيِنَا", "translit": "āَnِyْḥَwَw", "root": "وحي"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "جَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَمْرُنَا", "translit": "āَnُrْmَaʾ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَفَارَ", "translit": "َrāَfَw", "root": "فور"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلتَّنُّورُ", "translit": "ُrwُّnَّtlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "فَٱسْلُكْ", "translit": "ْkُlْsٱَf", "root": "سلك"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "كُلٍّ", "translit": "ٍّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "زَوْجَيْنِ", "translit": "ِnْyَjgْwَz", "root": "زوج"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱثْنَيْنِ", "translit": "ِnْyَnْhtٱ", "root": "ثني"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "وَأَهْلَكَ", "translit": "َkَlْhَaʾَw", "root": "اهل"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "سَبَقَ", "translit": "َqَbَs", "root": "سبق"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "ٱلْقَوْلُ", "translit": "ُlْwَqْlٱ", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "مِنْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "تُخَٰطِبْنِى", "translit": "àِnْbِṭَٰhkُt", "root": "خطب"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "ظَلَمُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُmَlَẓ", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "إِنَّهُم", "translit": "mُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 32, "arabic": "مُّغْرَقُونَ", "translit": "َnwُqَrْغُّm", "root": "غرق"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 28, "text": "فَإِذَا ٱسْتَوَيْتَ أَنتَ وَمَن مَّعَكَ عَلَى ٱلْفُلْكِ فَقُلِ ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ ٱلَّذِى نَجَّىٰنَا مِنَ ٱلْقَوْمِ ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ ", "translation": "And when you have boarded the ship, you and those with you, then say, 'Praise to Allāh who has saved us from the wrongdoing people.'", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱسْتَوَيْتَ", "translit": "َtْyَwَtْsٱ", "root": "سوي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَنتَ", "translit": "َtnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مَّعَكَ", "translit": "َkَʿعَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْفُلْكِ", "translit": "ِkْlُfْlٱ", "root": "فلك"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَقُلِ", "translit": "ِlُqَf", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْحَمْدُ", "translit": "ُdْmَḥْlٱ", "root": "حمد"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِl", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "نَجَّىٰنَا", "translit": "āَnٰàَّjgَn", "root": "نجو"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلْقَوْمِ", "translit": "ِmْwَqْlٱ", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِlَّٰẓlٱ", "root": "ظلم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 29, "text": "وَقُل رَّبِّ أَنزِلْنِى مُنزَلًا مُّبَارَكًا وَأَنتَ خَيْرُ ٱلْمُنزِلِينَ ", "translation": "And say, 'My Lord, let me land at a blessed landing place, and You are the best to accommodate [us].'\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقُل", "translit": "lُqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَّبِّ", "translit": "ِّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَنزِلْنِى", "translit": "àِnْlِznَaʾ", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مُنزَلًا", "translit": "āًlَznُm", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مُّبَارَكًا", "translit": "āًkَrāَbُّm", "root": "برك"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَأَنتَ", "translit": "َtnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "خَيْرُ", "translit": "ُrْyَhk", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْمُنزِلِينَ", "translit": "َnyِlِznُmْlٱ", "root": "نزل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 30, "text": "إِنَّ فِى ذَٰلِكَ لَءَايَٰتٍ وَإِن كُنَّا لَمُبْتَلِينَ ", "translation": "Indeed in that are signs, and indeed, We are ever testing [Our servants].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَءَايَٰتٍ", "translit": "ٍtَٰyāَءَl", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "كُنَّا", "translit": "āَّnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَمُبْتَلِينَ", "translit": "َnyِlَtْbُmَl", "root": "بلو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 31, "text": "ثُمَّ أَنشَأْنَا مِنۢ بَعْدِهِمْ قَرْنًا ءَاخَرِينَ ", "translation": "Then We produced after them a generation of others.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَنشَأْنَا", "translit": "āَnْaʾَhsnَaʾ", "root": "نشا"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بَعْدِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِdْʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَرْنًا", "translit": "āًnْrَq", "root": "قرن"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ءَاخَرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrَhkāَء", "root": "اخر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 32, "text": "فَأَرْسَلْنَا فِيهِمْ رَسُولًا مِّنْهُمْ أَنِ ٱعْبُدُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ مَا لَكُم مِّنْ إِلَٰهٍ غَيْرُهُۥٓۖ أَفَلَا تَتَّقُونَ ", "translation": "And We sent among them a messenger[1] from themselves, [saying], \"Worship Allāh; you have no deity other than Him; then will you not fear Him?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَأَرْسَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَsْrَaʾَf", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فِيهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "رَسُولًا", "translit": "āًlwُsَr", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِّنْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَنِ", "translit": "ِnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱعْبُدُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdُbْʿعٱ", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَكُم", "translit": "mُkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مِّنْ", "translit": "ْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "إِلَٰهٍ", "translit": "ٍhَٰlِiʾ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "غَيْرُهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhُrْyَغ", "root": "غير"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أَفَلَا", "translit": "āَlَfَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "تَتَّقُونَ", "translit": "َnwُqَّtَt", "root": "وقي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 33, "text": "وَقَالَ ٱلْمَلَأُ مِن قَوْمِهِ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ وَكَذَّبُوا۟ بِلِقَآءِ ٱلْءَاخِرَةِ وَأَتْرَفْنَٰهُمْ فِى ٱلْحَيَوٰةِ ٱلدُّنْيَا مَا هَٰذَآ إِلَّا بَشَرٌ مِّثْلُكُمْ يَأْكُلُ مِمَّا تَأْكُلُونَ مِنْهُ وَيَشْرَبُ مِمَّا تَشْرَبُونَ ", "translation": "And the eminent among his people who disbelieved and denied the meeting of the Hereafter while We had given them luxury in the worldly life said, \"This is not but a man like yourselves. He eats of that from which you eat and drinks of what you drink.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقَالَ", "translit": "َlāَqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْمَلَأُ", "translit": "ُaʾَlَmْlٱ", "root": "ملا"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "قَوْمِهِ", "translit": "ِhِmْwَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَكَذَّبُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُbَّhdَkَw", "root": "كذب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بِلِقَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَqِlِb", "root": "لقي"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْءَاخِرَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَrِhkāَءْlٱ", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَأَتْرَفْنَٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَٰnْfَrْtَaʾَw", "root": "ترف"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْحَيَوٰةِ", "translit": "ِẗٰwَyَḥْlٱ", "root": "حيي"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلدُّنْيَا", "translit": "āَyْnُّdlٱ", "root": "دنو"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "هَٰذَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "بَشَرٌ", "translit": "ٌrَhsَb", "root": "بشر"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "مِّثْلُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُlْhtِّm", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "يَأْكُلُ", "translit": "ُlُkْaʾَy", "root": "اكل"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "مِمَّا", "translit": "āَّmِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "تَأْكُلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlُkْaʾَt", "root": "اكل"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "مِنْهُ", "translit": "ُhْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "وَيَشْرَبُ", "translit": "ُbَrْhsَyَw", "root": "شرب"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "مِمَّا", "translit": "āَّmِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "تَشْرَبُونَ", "translit": "َnwُbَrْhsَt", "root": "شرب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 34, "text": "وَلَئِنْ أَطَعْتُم بَشَرًا مِّثْلَكُمْ إِنَّكُمْ إِذًا لَّخَٰسِرُونَ ", "translation": "And if you should obey a man like yourselves, indeed, you would then be losers.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَئِنْ", "translit": "ْnِyʾَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَطَعْتُم", "translit": "mُtْʿعَṭَaʾ", "root": "طوع"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بَشَرًا", "translit": "āًrَhsَb", "root": "بشر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِّثْلَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَlْhtِّm", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِنَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِذًا", "translit": "āًhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَّخَٰسِرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrِsَٰhkَّl", "root": "خسر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 35, "text": "أَيَعِدُكُمْ أَنَّكُمْ إِذَا مِتُّمْ وَكُنتُمْ تُرَابًا وَعِظَٰمًا أَنَّكُم مُّخْرَجُونَ ", "translation": "Does he promise you that when you have died and become dust and bones that you will be brought forth [once more]?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَيَعِدُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُdِʿعَyَaʾ", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَنَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِتُّمْ", "translit": "ْmُّtِm", "root": "موت"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَكُنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnُkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "تُرَابًا", "translit": "āًbāَrُt", "root": "ترب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَعِظَٰمًا", "translit": "āًmَٰẓِʿعَw", "root": "عظم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَنَّكُم", "translit": "mُkَّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مُّخْرَجُونَ", "translit": "َnwُjgَrْhkُّm", "root": "خرج"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 36, "text": "هَيْهَاتَ هَيْهَاتَ لِمَا تُوعَدُونَ ", "translation": "How far, how far, is that which you are promised.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "هَيْهَاتَ", "translit": "َtāَhْyَh", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هَيْهَاتَ", "translit": "َtāَhْyَh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لِمَا", "translit": "āَmِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تُوعَدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdَʿعwُt", "root": "وعد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 37, "text": "إِنْ هِىَ إِلَّا حَيَاتُنَا ٱلدُّنْيَا نَمُوتُ وَنَحْيَا وَمَا نَحْنُ بِمَبْعُوثِينَ ", "translation": "It [i.e., life] is not but our worldly life - we die and live, but we will not be resurrected.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنْ", "translit": "ْnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هِىَ", "translit": "َàِh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "حَيَاتُنَا", "translit": "āَnُtāَyَḥ", "root": "حيي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلدُّنْيَا", "translit": "āَyْnُّdlٱ", "root": "دنو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "نَمُوتُ", "translit": "ُtwُmَn", "root": "موت"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَنَحْيَا", "translit": "āَyْḥَnَw", "root": "حيي"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "نَحْنُ", "translit": "ُnْḥَn", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بِمَبْعُوثِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhtwُʿعْbَmِb", "root": "بعث"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 38, "text": "إِنْ هُوَ إِلَّا رَجُلٌ ٱفْتَرَىٰ عَلَى ٱللَّهِ كَذِبًا وَمَا نَحْنُ لَهُۥ بِمُؤْمِنِينَ ", "translation": "He is not but a man who has invented a lie about Allāh, and we will not believe him.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنْ", "translit": "ْnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "رَجُلٌ", "translit": "ٌlُjgَr", "root": "رجل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱفْتَرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrَtْfٱ", "root": "فري"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كَذِبًا", "translit": "āًbِhdَk", "root": "كذب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "نَحْنُ", "translit": "ُnْḥَn", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بِمُؤْمِنِينَ", "translit": "َnyِnِmْwʾُmِb", "root": "امن"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 39, "text": "قَالَ رَبِّ ٱنصُرْنِى بِمَا كَذَّبُونِ ", "translation": "He said, \"My Lord, support me because they have denied me.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَبِّ", "translit": "ِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱنصُرْنِى", "translit": "àِnْrُṣnٱ", "root": "نصر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "كَذَّبُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُbَّhdَk", "root": "كذب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 40, "text": "قَالَ عَمَّا قَلِيلٍ لَّيُصْبِحُنَّ نَٰدِمِينَ ", "translation": "[Allāh] said, \"After a little, they will surely become regretful.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَلِيلٍ", "translit": "ٍlyِlَq", "root": "قلل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَّيُصْبِحُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُḥِbْṣُyَّl", "root": "صبح"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "نَٰدِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِdَٰn", "root": "ندم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 41, "text": "فَأَخَذَتْهُمُ ٱلصَّيْحَةُ بِٱلْحَقِّ فَجَعَلْنَٰهُمْ غُثَآءًۚ فَبُعْدًا لِّلْقَوْمِ ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ ", "translation": "So the shriek[1] seized them in truth,[2] and We made them as [plant] stubble. Then away with the wrongdoing people.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَأَخَذَتْهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhْtَhdَhkَaʾَf", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلصَّيْحَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَḥْyَّṣlٱ", "root": "صيح"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِٱلْحَقِّ", "translit": "ِّqَḥْlٱِb", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَجَعَلْنَٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَٰnْlَʿعَjgَf", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "غُثَآءً", "translit": "ًءٓāَhtُغ", "root": "غثو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَبُعْدًا", "translit": "āًdْʿعُbَf", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لِّلْقَوْمِ", "translit": "ِmْwَqْlِّl", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِlَّٰẓlٱ", "root": "ظلم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 42, "text": "ثُمَّ أَنشَأْنَا مِنۢ بَعْدِهِمْ قُرُونًا ءَاخَرِينَ ", "translation": "Then We produced after them other generations.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَنشَأْنَا", "translit": "āَnْaʾَhsnَaʾ", "root": "نشا"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بَعْدِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِdْʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قُرُونًا", "translit": "āًnwُrُq", "root": "قرن"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ءَاخَرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrَhkāَء", "root": "اخر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 43, "text": "مَا تَسْبِقُ مِنْ أُمَّةٍ أَجَلَهَا وَمَا يَسْتَـْٔخِرُونَ ", "translation": "No nation will precede its time [of termination], nor will they remain [thereafter].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَسْبِقُ", "translit": "ُqِbْsَt", "root": "سبق"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أُمَّةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَّmُaʾ", "root": "امم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَجَلَهَا", "translit": "āَhَlَjgَaʾ", "root": "اجل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَسْتَـْٔخِرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrِhkْٔـَtْsَy", "root": "اخر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 44, "text": "ثُمَّ أَرْسَلْنَا رُسُلَنَا تَتْرَاۖ كُلَّ مَا جَآءَ أُمَّةً رَّسُولُهَا كَذَّبُوهُۚ فَأَتْبَعْنَا بَعْضَهُم بَعْضًا وَجَعَلْنَٰهُمْ أَحَادِيثَۚ فَبُعْدًا لِّقَوْمٍ لَّا يُؤْمِنُونَ ", "translation": "Then We sent Our messengers in succession. Every time there came to a nation its messenger, they denied him, so We made them follow one another [to destruction], and We made them narrations.[1] So away with a people who do not believe.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَرْسَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَsْrَaʾ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "رُسُلَنَا", "translit": "āَnَlُsُr", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تَتْرَا", "translit": "āَrْtَt", "root": "وتر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "كُلَّ", "translit": "َّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "جَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أُمَّةً", "translit": "ًẗَّmُaʾ", "root": "امم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "رَّسُولُهَا", "translit": "āَhُlwُsَّr", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "كَذَّبُوهُ", "translit": "ُhwُbَّhdَk", "root": "كذب"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "فَأَتْبَعْنَا", "translit": "āَnْʿعَbْtَaʾَf", "root": "تبع"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بَعْضَهُم", "translit": "mُhَḍْʿعَb", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "بَعْضًا", "translit": "āًḍْʿعَb", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَجَعَلْنَٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَٰnْlَʿعَjgَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "أَحَادِيثَ", "translit": "َhtyِdāَḥَaʾ", "root": "حدث"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "فَبُعْدًا", "translit": "āًdْʿعُbَf", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "لِّقَوْمٍ", "translit": "ٍmْwَqِّl", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "لَّا", "translit": "āَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "يُؤْمِنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnِmْwʾُy", "root": "امن"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 45, "text": "ثُمَّ أَرْسَلْنَا مُوسَىٰ وَأَخَاهُ هَٰرُونَ بِـَٔايَٰتِنَا وَسُلْطَٰنٍ مُّبِينٍ ", "translation": "Then We sent Moses and his brother Aaron with Our signs and a clear authority[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَرْسَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَsْrَaʾ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مُوسَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَswُm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَأَخَاهُ", "translit": "ُhāَhkَaʾَw", "root": "اخو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "هَٰرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بِـَٔايَٰتِنَا", "translit": "āَnِtَٰyāَٔـِb", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَسُلْطَٰنٍ", "translit": "ٍnَٰṭْlُsَw", "root": "سلط"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مُّبِينٍ", "translit": "ٍnyِbُّm", "root": "بين"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 46, "text": "إِلَىٰ فِرْعَوْنَ وَمَلَإِي۟هِۦ فَٱسْتَكْبَرُوا۟ وَكَانُوا۟ قَوْمًا عَالِينَ ", "translation": "To Pharaoh and his establishment, but they were arrogant and were a haughty people.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فِرْعَوْنَ", "translit": "َnْwَʿعْrِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَمَلَإِي۟هِۦ", "translit": "ۦِh۟yِiʾَlَmَw", "root": "ملا"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَٱسْتَكْبَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَbْkَtْsٱَf", "root": "كبر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَكَانُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnāَkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "قَوْمًا", "translit": "āًmْwَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "عَالِينَ", "translit": "َnyِlāَʿع", "root": "علو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 47, "text": "فَقَالُوٓا۟ أَنُؤْمِنُ لِبَشَرَيْنِ مِثْلِنَا وَقَوْمُهُمَا لَنَا عَٰبِدُونَ ", "translation": "They said, \"Should we believe two men like ourselves while their people are for us in servitude?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَقَالُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُlāَqَf", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَنُؤْمِنُ", "translit": "ُnِmْwʾُnَaʾ", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لِبَشَرَيْنِ", "translit": "ِnْyَrَhsَbِl", "root": "بشر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِثْلِنَا", "translit": "āَnِlْhtِm", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَقَوْمُهُمَا", "translit": "āَmُhُmْwَqَw", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَنَا", "translit": "āَnَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "عَٰبِدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdِbَٰʿع", "root": "عبد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 48, "text": "فَكَذَّبُوهُمَا فَكَانُوا۟ مِنَ ٱلْمُهْلَكِينَ ", "translation": "So they denied them and were of those destroyed.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَكَذَّبُوهُمَا", "translit": "āَmُhwُbَّhdَkَf", "root": "كذب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فَكَانُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnāَkَf", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْمُهْلَكِينَ", "translit": "َnyِkَlْhُmْlٱ", "root": "هلك"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 49, "text": "وَلَقَدْ ءَاتَيْنَا مُوسَى ٱلْكِتَٰبَ لَعَلَّهُمْ يَهْتَدُونَ ", "translation": "And We certainly gave Moses the Scripture that perhaps they[1] would be guided.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ءَاتَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَtāَء", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مُوسَى", "translit": "àَswُm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْكِتَٰبَ", "translit": "َbَٰtِkْlٱ", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَعَلَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّlَʿعَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَهْتَدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdَtْhَy", "root": "هدي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 50, "text": "وَجَعَلْنَا ٱبْنَ مَرْيَمَ وَأُمَّهُۥٓ ءَايَةً وَءَاوَيْنَٰهُمَآ إِلَىٰ رَبْوَةٍ ذَاتِ قَرَارٍ وَمَعِينٍ ", "translation": "And We made the son of Mary and his mother a sign and sheltered them within a high ground having level [areas] and flowing water.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَجَعَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَʿعَjgَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱبْنَ", "translit": "َnْbٱ", "root": "بني"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَرْيَمَ", "translit": "َmَyْrَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَأُمَّهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَّmُaʾَw", "root": "امم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ءَايَةً", "translit": "ًẗَyāَء", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَءَاوَيْنَٰهُمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmُhَٰnْyَwāَءَw", "root": "اوي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "رَبْوَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَwْbَr", "root": "ربو"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ذَاتِ", "translit": "ِtāَhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "قَرَارٍ", "translit": "ٍrāَrَq", "root": "قرر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَمَعِينٍ", "translit": "ٍnyِʿعَmَw", "root": "عين"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 51, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلرُّسُلُ كُلُوا۟ مِنَ ٱلطَّيِّبَٰتِ وَٱعْمَلُوا۟ صَٰلِحًاۖ إِنِّى بِمَا تَعْمَلُونَ عَلِيمٌ ", "translation": "[Allāh said], \"O messengers, eat from the good foods and work righteousness. Indeed I, of what you do, am Knowing.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلرُّسُلُ", "translit": "ُlُsُّrlٱ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كُلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlُk", "root": "اكل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلطَّيِّبَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰbِّyَّṭlٱ", "root": "طيب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَٱعْمَلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlَmْʿعٱَw", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "صَٰلِحًا", "translit": "āًḥِlَٰṣ", "root": "صلح"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِنِّى", "translit": "àِّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "تَعْمَلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlَmْʿعَt", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "عَلِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِlَʿع", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 52, "text": "وَإِنَّ هَٰذِهِۦٓ أُمَّتُكُمْ أُمَّةً وَٰحِدَةً وَأَنَا۠ رَبُّكُمْ فَٱتَّقُونِ ", "translation": "And indeed this, your religion, is one religion,[1] and I am your Lord, so fear Me.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هَٰذِهِۦٓ", "translit": "ٓۦِhِhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أُمَّتُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُtَّmُaʾ", "root": "امم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أُمَّةً", "translit": "ًẗَّmُaʾ", "root": "امم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَٰحِدَةً", "translit": "ًẗَdِḥَٰw", "root": "وحد"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَأَنَا۠", "translit": "۠āَnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "رَبُّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَٱتَّقُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُqَّtٱَf", "root": "وقي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 53, "text": "فَتَقَطَّعُوٓا۟ أَمْرَهُم بَيْنَهُمْ زُبُرًاۖ كُلُّ حِزْبٍۭ بِمَا لَدَيْهِمْ فَرِحُونَ ", "translation": "But they [i.e., the people] divided their religion among them into portions [i.e., sects] - each faction, in what it has,[1] rejoicing.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَتَقَطَّعُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُʿعَّṭَqَtَf", "root": "قطع"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَمْرَهُم", "translit": "mُhَrْmَaʾ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بَيْنَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "زُبُرًا", "translit": "āًrُbُz", "root": "زبر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "كُلُّ", "translit": "ُّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "حِزْبٍۭ", "translit": "ٍۭbْzِḥ", "root": "حزب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَدَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَdَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فَرِحُونَ", "translit": "َnwُḥِrَf", "root": "فرح"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 54, "text": "فَذَرْهُمْ فِى غَمْرَتِهِمْ حَتَّىٰ حِينٍ ", "translation": "So leave them in their confusion for a time.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَذَرْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْrَhdَf", "root": "وذر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "غَمْرَتِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِtَrْmَغ", "root": "غمر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "حَتَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّtَḥ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "حِينٍ", "translit": "ٍnyِḥ", "root": "حين"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 55, "text": "أَيَحْسَبُونَ أَنَّمَا نُمِدُّهُم بِهِۦ مِن مَّالٍ وَبَنِينَ ", "translation": "Do they think that what We extend to them of wealth and children", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَيَحْسَبُونَ", "translit": "َnwُbَsْḥَyَaʾ", "root": "حسب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَنَّمَا", "translit": "āَmَّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "نُمِدُّهُم", "translit": "mُhُّdِmُn", "root": "مدد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مَّالٍ", "translit": "ٍlāَّm", "root": "مول"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَبَنِينَ", "translit": "َnyِnَbَw", "root": "بني"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 56, "text": "نُسَارِعُ لَهُمْ فِى ٱلْخَيْرَٰتِۚ بَل لَّا يَشْعُرُونَ ", "translation": "Is [because] We hasten for them good things? Rather, they do not perceive.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "نُسَارِعُ", "translit": "ُʿعِrāَsُn", "root": "سرع"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْخَيْرَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰrْyَhkْlٱ", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بَل", "translit": "lَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَّا", "translit": "āَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَشْعُرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrُʿعْhsَy", "root": "شعر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 57, "text": "إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ هُم مِّنْ خَشْيَةِ رَبِّهِم مُّشْفِقُونَ ", "translation": "Indeed, they who are apprehensive from fear of their Lord", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "هُم", "translit": "mُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِّنْ", "translit": "ْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "خَشْيَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَyْhsَhk", "root": "خشي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "رَبِّهِم", "translit": "mِhِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مُّشْفِقُونَ", "translit": "َnwُqِfْhsُّm", "root": "شفق"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 58, "text": "وَٱلَّذِينَ هُم بِـَٔايَٰتِ رَبِّهِمْ يُؤْمِنُونَ ", "translation": "And they who believe in the signs of their Lord", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هُم", "translit": "mُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِـَٔايَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰyāَٔـِb", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "رَبِّهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يُؤْمِنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnِmْwʾُy", "root": "امن"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 59, "text": "وَٱلَّذِينَ هُم بِرَبِّهِمْ لَا يُشْرِكُونَ ", "translation": "And they who do not associate anything with their Lord", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هُم", "translit": "mُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِرَبِّهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِّbَrِb", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يُشْرِكُونَ", "translit": "َnwُkِrْhsُy", "root": "شرك"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 60, "text": "وَٱلَّذِينَ يُؤْتُونَ مَآ ءَاتَوا۟ وَّقُلُوبُهُمْ وَجِلَةٌ أَنَّهُمْ إِلَىٰ رَبِّهِمْ رَٰجِعُونَ ", "translation": "And they who give what they give while their hearts are fearful[1] because they will be returning to their Lord -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يُؤْتُونَ", "translit": "َnwُtْwʾُy", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَآ", "translit": "ٓāَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ءَاتَوا۟", "translit": "۟āwَtāَء", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَّقُلُوبُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُbwُlُqَّw", "root": "قلب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَجِلَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَlِjgَw", "root": "وجل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَنَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "رَبِّهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "رَٰجِعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعِjgَٰr", "root": "رجع"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 61, "text": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ يُسَٰرِعُونَ فِى ٱلْخَيْرَٰتِ وَهُمْ لَهَا سَٰبِقُونَ ", "translation": "It is those who hasten to good deeds, and they outstrip [others] therein.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يُسَٰرِعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعِrَٰsُy", "root": "سرع"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْخَيْرَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰrْyَhkْlٱ", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَهَا", "translit": "āَhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "سَٰبِقُونَ", "translit": "َnwُqِbَٰs", "root": "سبق"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 62, "text": "وَلَا نُكَلِّفُ نَفْسًا إِلَّا وُسْعَهَاۖ وَلَدَيْنَا كِتَٰبٌ يَنطِقُ بِٱلْحَقِّۚ وَهُمْ لَا يُظْلَمُونَ ", "translation": "And We charge no soul except [with that within] its capacity, and with Us is a record which speaks with truth; and they will not be wronged.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "نُكَلِّفُ", "translit": "ُfِّlَkُn", "root": "كلف"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "نَفْسًا", "translit": "āًsْfَn", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وُسْعَهَا", "translit": "āَhَʿعْsُw", "root": "وسع"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَلَدَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَdَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "كِتَٰبٌ", "translit": "ٌbَٰtِk", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَنطِقُ", "translit": "ُqِṭnَy", "root": "نطق"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بِٱلْحَقِّ", "translit": "ِّqَḥْlٱِb", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "يُظْلَمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmَlْẓُy", "root": "ظلم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 63, "text": "بَلْ قُلُوبُهُمْ فِى غَمْرَةٍ مِّنْ هَٰذَا وَلَهُمْ أَعْمَٰلٌ مِّن دُونِ ذَٰلِكَ هُمْ لَهَا عَٰمِلُونَ ", "translation": "But their hearts are covered with confusion over this, and they have [evil] deeds besides that [i.e., disbelief] which they are doing,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "بَلْ", "translit": "ْlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قُلُوبُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُbwُlُq", "root": "قلب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "غَمْرَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَrْmَغ", "root": "غمر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِّنْ", "translit": "ْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَلَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَعْمَٰلٌ", "translit": "ٌlَٰmْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "دُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "هُمْ", "translit": "ْmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "لَهَا", "translit": "āَhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "عَٰمِلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlِmَٰʿع", "root": "عمل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 64, "text": "حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَآ أَخَذْنَا مُتْرَفِيهِم بِٱلْعَذَابِ إِذَا هُمْ يَجْـَٔرُونَ ", "translation": "Until when We seize their affluent ones with punishment,[1] at once they are crying [to Allāh] for help.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "حَتَّىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَّtَḥ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِذَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَخَذْنَا", "translit": "āَnْhdَhkَaʾ", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مُتْرَفِيهِم", "translit": "mِhyِfَrْtُm", "root": "ترف"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِٱلْعَذَابِ", "translit": "ِbāَhdَʿعْlٱِb", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "هُمْ", "translit": "ْmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَجْـَٔرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrَٔـْjgَy", "root": "جار"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 65, "text": "لَا تَجْـَٔرُوا۟ ٱلْيَوْمَۖ إِنَّكُم مِّنَّا لَا تُنصَرُونَ ", "translation": "Do not cry out today. Indeed, by Us you will not be helped.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَجْـَٔرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَٔـْjgَt", "root": "جار"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْيَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَyْlٱ", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِنَّكُم", "translit": "mُkَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِّنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "تُنصَرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrَṣnُt", "root": "نصر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 66, "text": "قَدْ كَانَتْ ءَايَٰتِى تُتْلَىٰ عَلَيْكُمْ فَكُنتُمْ عَلَىٰٓ أَعْقَٰبِكُمْ تَنكِصُونَ ", "translation": "My verses had already been recited to you, but you were turning back on your heels", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَدْ", "translit": "ْdَq", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَانَتْ", "translit": "ْtَnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَايَٰتِى", "translit": "àِtَٰyāَء", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تُتْلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlْtُt", "root": "تلو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَلَيْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَكُنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnُkَf", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "عَلَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَعْقَٰبِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِbَٰqْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عقب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَنكِصُونَ", "translit": "َnwُṣِknَt", "root": "نكص"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 67, "text": "مُسْتَكْبِرِينَ بِهِۦ سَٰمِرًا تَهْجُرُونَ ", "translation": "In arrogance regarding it,[1] conversing by night, speaking evil.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مُسْتَكْبِرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِbْkَtْsُm", "root": "كبر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "سَٰمِرًا", "translit": "āًrِmَٰs", "root": "سمر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تَهْجُرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrُjgْhَt", "root": "هجر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 68, "text": "أَفَلَمْ يَدَّبَّرُوا۟ ٱلْقَوْلَ أَمْ جَآءَهُم مَّا لَمْ يَأْتِ ءَابَآءَهُمُ ٱلْأَوَّلِينَ ", "translation": "Then have they not reflected over the word [i.e., the Qur’ān], or has there come to them that which had not come to their forefathers?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَفَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَfَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَدَّبَّرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَّbَّdَy", "root": "دبر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْقَوْلَ", "translit": "َlْwَqْlٱ", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَمْ", "translit": "ْmَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "جَآءَهُم", "translit": "mُhَءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مَّا", "translit": "āَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَمْ", "translit": "ْmَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَأْتِ", "translit": "ِtْaʾَy", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ءَابَآءَهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhَءٓāَbāَء", "root": "ابو"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْأَوَّلِينَ", "translit": "َnyِlَّwَaʾْlٱ", "root": "اول"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 69, "text": "أَمْ لَمْ يَعْرِفُوا۟ رَسُولَهُمْ فَهُمْ لَهُۥ مُنكِرُونَ ", "translation": "Or did they not know their Messenger, so they are toward him disacknowledging?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَمْ", "translit": "ْmَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَمْ", "translit": "ْmَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَعْرِفُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُfِrْʿعَy", "root": "عرف"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "رَسُولَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَlwُsَr", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مُنكِرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrِknُm", "root": "نكر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 70, "text": "أَمْ يَقُولُونَ بِهِۦ جِنَّةٌۢۚ بَلْ جَآءَهُم بِٱلْحَقِّ وَأَكْثَرُهُمْ لِلْحَقِّ كَٰرِهُونَ ", "translation": "Or do they say, \"In him is madness\"? Rather, he brought them the truth, but most of them, to the truth, are averse.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَمْ", "translit": "ْmَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَقُولُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlwُqَy", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "جِنَّةٌۢ", "translit": "ٌۢẗَّnِjg", "root": "جنن"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بَلْ", "translit": "ْlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "جَآءَهُم", "translit": "mُhَءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بِٱلْحَقِّ", "translit": "ِّqَḥْlٱِb", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَأَكْثَرُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُrَhtْkَaʾَw", "root": "كثر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لِلْحَقِّ", "translit": "ِّqَḥْlِl", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "كَٰرِهُونَ", "translit": "َnwُhِrَٰk", "root": "كره"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 71, "text": "وَلَوِ ٱتَّبَعَ ٱلْحَقُّ أَهْوَآءَهُمْ لَفَسَدَتِ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتُ وَٱلْأَرْضُ وَمَن فِيهِنَّۚ بَلْ أَتَيْنَٰهُم بِذِكْرِهِمْ فَهُمْ عَن ذِكْرِهِم مُّعْرِضُونَ ", "translation": "But if the Truth [i.e., Allāh] had followed their inclinations, the heavens and the earth and whoever is in them would have been ruined. Rather, We have brought them their message,[1] but they, from their message, are turning away.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَوِ", "translit": "ِwَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱتَّبَعَ", "translit": "َʿعَbَّtٱ", "root": "تبع"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْحَقُّ", "translit": "ُّqَḥْlٱ", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَهْوَآءَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَءٓāَwْhَaʾ", "root": "هوي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَفَسَدَتِ", "translit": "ِtَdَsَfَl", "root": "فسد"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتُ", "translit": "ُtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَرْضُ", "translit": "ُḍْrَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فِيهِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بَلْ", "translit": "ْlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَتَيْنَٰهُم", "translit": "mُhَٰnْyَtَaʾ", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بِذِكْرِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِrْkِhdِb", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "فَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "عَن", "translit": "nَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ذِكْرِهِم", "translit": "mِhِrْkِhd", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "مُّعْرِضُونَ", "translit": "َnwُḍِrْʿعُّm", "root": "عرض"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 72, "text": "أَمْ تَسْـَٔلُهُمْ خَرْجًا فَخَرَاجُ رَبِّكَ خَيْرٌۖ وَهُوَ خَيْرُ ٱلرَّٰزِقِينَ ", "translation": "Or do you, [O Muḥammad], ask them for payment? But the reward of your Lord is best, and He is the best of providers.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَمْ", "translit": "ْmَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَسْـَٔلُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُlَٔـْsَt", "root": "سال"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "خَرْجًا", "translit": "āًjgْrَhk", "root": "خرج"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَخَرَاجُ", "translit": "ُjgāَrَhkَf", "root": "خرج"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "رَبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "خَيْرٌ", "translit": "ٌrْyَhk", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "خَيْرُ", "translit": "ُrْyَhk", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلرَّٰزِقِينَ", "translit": "َnyِqِzَّٰrlٱ", "root": "رزق"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 73, "text": "وَإِنَّكَ لَتَدْعُوهُمْ إِلَىٰ صِرَٰطٍ مُّسْتَقِيمٍ ", "translation": "And indeed, you invite them to a straight path.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِنَّكَ", "translit": "َkَّnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَتَدْعُوهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhwُʿعْdَtَl", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "صِرَٰطٍ", "translit": "ٍṭَٰrِṣ", "root": "صرط"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مُّسْتَقِيمٍ", "translit": "ٍmyِqَtْsُّm", "root": "قوم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 74, "text": "وَإِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ لَا يُؤْمِنُونَ بِٱلْءَاخِرَةِ عَنِ ٱلصِّرَٰطِ لَنَٰكِبُونَ ", "translation": "But indeed, those who do not believe in the Hereafter are deviating from the path.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يُؤْمِنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnِmْwʾُy", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِٱلْءَاخِرَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَrِhkāَءْlٱِb", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَنِ", "translit": "ِnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلصِّرَٰطِ", "translit": "ِṭَٰrِّṣlٱ", "root": "صرط"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَنَٰكِبُونَ", "translit": "َnwُbِkَٰnَl", "root": "نكب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 75, "text": "وَلَوْ رَحِمْنَٰهُمْ وَكَشَفْنَا مَا بِهِم مِّن ضُرٍّ لَّلَجُّوا۟ فِى طُغْيَٰنِهِمْ يَعْمَهُونَ ", "translation": "And even if We gave them mercy and removed what was upon them of affliction, they would persist in their transgression, wandering blindly.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَوْ", "translit": "ْwَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَحِمْنَٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَٰnْmِḥَr", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَكَشَفْنَا", "translit": "āَnْfَhsَkَw", "root": "كشف"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِهِم", "translit": "mِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ضُرٍّ", "translit": "ٍّrُḍ", "root": "ضرر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَّلَجُّوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُّjgَlَّl", "root": "لجج"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "طُغْيَٰنِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِnَٰyْغُṭ", "root": "طغي"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يَعْمَهُونَ", "translit": "َnwُhَmْʿعَy", "root": "عمه"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 76, "text": "وَلَقَدْ أَخَذْنَٰهُم بِٱلْعَذَابِ فَمَا ٱسْتَكَانُوا۟ لِرَبِّهِمْ وَمَا يَتَضَرَّعُونَ ", "translation": "And We had gripped them with suffering [as a warning], but they did not yield to their Lord, nor did they humbly supplicate, [and will continue thus]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَخَذْنَٰهُم", "translit": "mُhَٰnْhdَhkَaʾ", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِٱلْعَذَابِ", "translit": "ِbāَhdَʿعْlٱِb", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَمَا", "translit": "āَmَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱسْتَكَانُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnāَkَtْsٱ", "root": "كين"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لِرَبِّهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِّbَrِl", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَتَضَرَّعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعَّrَḍَtَy", "root": "ضرع"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 77, "text": "حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا فَتَحْنَا عَلَيْهِم بَابًا ذَا عَذَابٍ شَدِيدٍ إِذَا هُمْ فِيهِ مُبْلِسُونَ ", "translation": "Until when We have opened before them a door of severe punishment, immediately they will be therein in despair.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "حَتَّىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَّtَḥ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَتَحْنَا", "translit": "āَnْḥَtَf", "root": "فتح"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِم", "translit": "mِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بَابًا", "translit": "āًbāَb", "root": "بوب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ذَا", "translit": "āَhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "عَذَابٍ", "translit": "ٍbāَhdَʿع", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "شَدِيدٍ", "translit": "ٍdyِdَhs", "root": "شدد"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "هُمْ", "translit": "ْmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "فِيهِ", "translit": "ِhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مُبْلِسُونَ", "translit": "َnwُsِlْbُm", "root": "بلس"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 78, "text": "وَهُوَ ٱلَّذِىٓ أَنشَأَ لَكُمُ ٱلسَّمْعَ وَٱلْأَبْصَٰرَ وَٱلْأَفْـِٔدَةَۚ قَلِيلًا مَّا تَشْكُرُونَ ", "translation": "And it is He who produced for you hearing and vision and hearts [i.e., intellect]; little are you grateful.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَنشَأَ", "translit": "َaʾَhsnَaʾ", "root": "نشا"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمْعَ", "translit": "َʿعْmَّslٱ", "root": "سمع"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَبْصَٰرَ", "translit": "َrَٰṣْbَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "بصر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَفْـِٔدَةَ", "translit": "َẗَdِٔـْfَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "فاد"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "قَلِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِlَq", "root": "قلل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَّا", "translit": "āَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "تَشْكُرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrُkْhsَt", "root": "شكر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 79, "text": "وَهُوَ ٱلَّذِى ذَرَأَكُمْ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ وَإِلَيْهِ تُحْشَرُونَ ", "translation": "And it is He who has multiplied you throughout the earth, and to Him you will be gathered.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ذَرَأَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَaʾَrَhd", "root": "ذرا"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَإِلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "تُحْشَرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrَhsْḥُt", "root": "حشر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 80, "text": "وَهُوَ ٱلَّذِى يُحْىِۦ وَيُمِيتُ وَلَهُ ٱخْتِلَٰفُ ٱلَّيْلِ وَٱلنَّهَارِۚ أَفَلَا تَعْقِلُونَ ", "translation": "And it is He who gives life and causes death, and His is the alternation of the night and the day. Then will you not reason?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يُحْىِۦ", "translit": "ۦِàْḥُy", "root": "حيي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَيُمِيتُ", "translit": "ُtyِmُyَw", "root": "موت"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَلَهُ", "translit": "ُhَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱخْتِلَٰفُ", "translit": "ُfَٰlِtْhkٱ", "root": "خلف"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلَّيْلِ", "translit": "ِlْyَّlٱ", "root": "ليل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَٱلنَّهَارِ", "translit": "ِrāَhَّnlٱَw", "root": "نهر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَفَلَا", "translit": "āَlَfَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "تَعْقِلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlِqْʿعَt", "root": "عقل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 81, "text": "بَلْ قَالُوا۟ مِثْلَ مَا قَالَ ٱلْأَوَّلُونَ ", "translation": "Rather,[1] they say like what the former peoples said.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "بَلْ", "translit": "ْlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قَالُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِثْلَ", "translit": "َlْhtِm", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْأَوَّلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlَّwَaʾْlٱ", "root": "اول"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 82, "text": "قَالُوٓا۟ أَءِذَا مِتْنَا وَكُنَّا تُرَابًا وَعِظَٰمًا أَءِنَّا لَمَبْعُوثُونَ ", "translation": "They said, \"When we have died and become dust and bones, are we indeed to be resurrected?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَءِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِءَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِتْنَا", "translit": "āَnْtِm", "root": "موت"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَكُنَّا", "translit": "āَّnُkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "تُرَابًا", "translit": "āًbāَrُt", "root": "ترب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَعِظَٰمًا", "translit": "āًmَٰẓِʿعَw", "root": "عظم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَءِنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِءَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَمَبْعُوثُونَ", "translit": "َnwُhtwُʿعْbَmَl", "root": "بعث"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 83, "text": "لَقَدْ وُعِدْنَا نَحْنُ وَءَابَآؤُنَا هَٰذَا مِن قَبْلُ إِنْ هَٰذَآ إِلَّآ أَسَٰطِيرُ ٱلْأَوَّلِينَ ", "translation": "We have been promised this, we and our forefathers, before; this is not but legends of the former peoples.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وُعِدْنَا", "translit": "āَnْdِʿعُw", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "نَحْنُ", "translit": "ُnْḥَn", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَءَابَآؤُنَا", "translit": "āَnُwʾٓāَbāَءَw", "root": "ابو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "قَبْلُ", "translit": "ُlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِنْ", "translit": "ْnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "هَٰذَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "إِلَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَسَٰطِيرُ", "translit": "ُryِṭَٰsَaʾ", "root": "سطر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْأَوَّلِينَ", "translit": "َnyِlَّwَaʾْlٱ", "root": "اول"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 84, "text": "قُل لِّمَنِ ٱلْأَرْضُ وَمَن فِيهَآ إِن كُنتُمْ تَعْلَمُونَ ", "translation": "Say, [O Muḥammad], \"To whom belongs the earth and whoever is in it, if you should know?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُل", "translit": "lُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لِّمَنِ", "translit": "ِnَmِّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضُ", "translit": "ُḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِيهَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "كُنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تَعْلَمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmَlْʿعَt", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 85, "text": "سَيَقُولُونَ لِلَّهِۚ قُلْ أَفَلَا تَذَكَّرُونَ ", "translation": "They will say, \"To Allāh.\" Say, \"Then will you not remember?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "سَيَقُولُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlwُqَyَs", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِl", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَفَلَا", "translit": "āَlَfَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "تَذَكَّرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrَّkَhdَt", "root": "ذكر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 86, "text": "قُلْ مَن رَّبُّ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ ٱلسَّبْعِ وَرَبُّ ٱلْعَرْشِ ٱلْعَظِيمِ ", "translation": "Say, \"Who is Lord of the seven heavens and Lord of the Great Throne?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "رَّبُّ", "translit": "ُّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلسَّبْعِ", "translit": "ِʿعْbَّslٱ", "root": "سبع"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَرَبُّ", "translit": "ُّbَrَw", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْعَرْشِ", "translit": "ِhsْrَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عرش"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْعَظِيمِ", "translit": "ِmyِẓَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عظم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 87, "text": "سَيَقُولُونَ لِلَّهِۚ قُلْ أَفَلَا تَتَّقُونَ ", "translation": "They will say, \"[They belong] to Allāh.\" Say, \"Then will you not fear Him?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "سَيَقُولُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlwُqَyَs", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِl", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَفَلَا", "translit": "āَlَfَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "تَتَّقُونَ", "translit": "َnwُqَّtَt", "root": "وقي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 88, "text": "قُلْ مَنۢ بِيَدِهِۦ مَلَكُوتُ كُلِّ شَىْءٍ وَهُوَ يُجِيرُ وَلَا يُجَارُ عَلَيْهِ إِن كُنتُمْ تَعْلَمُونَ ", "translation": "Say, \"In whose hand is the realm of all things - and He protects while none can protect against Him - if you should know?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَنۢ", "translit": "ۢnَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِيَدِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِdَyِb", "root": "يدي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَلَكُوتُ", "translit": "ُtwُkَlَm", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "كُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "شَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يُجِيرُ", "translit": "ُryِjgُy", "root": "جور"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "يُجَارُ", "translit": "ُrāَjgُy", "root": "جور"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "كُنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "تَعْلَمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmَlْʿعَt", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 89, "text": "سَيَقُولُونَ لِلَّهِۚ قُلْ فَأَنَّىٰ تُسْحَرُونَ ", "translation": "They will say, \"[All belongs] to Allāh.\" Say, \"Then how are you deluded?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "سَيَقُولُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlwُqَyَs", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِl", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَأَنَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّnَaʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "تُسْحَرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrَḥْsُt", "root": "سحر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 90, "text": "بَلْ أَتَيْنَٰهُم بِٱلْحَقِّ وَإِنَّهُمْ لَكَٰذِبُونَ ", "translation": "Rather, We have brought them the truth, and indeed they are liars.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "بَلْ", "translit": "ْlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَتَيْنَٰهُم", "translit": "mُhَٰnْyَtَaʾ", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِٱلْحَقِّ", "translit": "ِّqَḥْlٱِb", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَإِنَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَكَٰذِبُونَ", "translit": "َnwُbِhdَٰkَl", "root": "كذب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 91, "text": "مَا ٱتَّخَذَ ٱللَّهُ مِن وَلَدٍ وَمَا كَانَ مَعَهُۥ مِنْ إِلَٰهٍۚ إِذًا لَّذَهَبَ كُلُّ إِلَٰهٍۭ بِمَا خَلَقَ وَلَعَلَا بَعْضُهُمْ عَلَىٰ بَعْضٍۚ سُبْحَٰنَ ٱللَّهِ عَمَّا يَصِفُونَ ", "translation": "Allāh has not taken any son, nor has there ever been with Him any deity. [If there had been], then each deity would have taken what it created, and some of them would have [sought to] overcome others. Exalted is Allāh above what they describe [concerning Him].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱتَّخَذَ", "translit": "َhdَhkَّtٱ", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَلَدٍ", "translit": "ٍdَlَw", "root": "ولد"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مَعَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَʿعَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "إِلَٰهٍ", "translit": "ٍhَٰlِiʾ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "إِذًا", "translit": "āًhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَّذَهَبَ", "translit": "َbَhَhdَّl", "root": "ذهب"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "كُلُّ", "translit": "ُّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "إِلَٰهٍۭ", "translit": "ٍۭhَٰlِiʾ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "خَلَقَ", "translit": "َqَlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "وَلَعَلَا", "translit": "āَlَʿعَlَw", "root": "علو"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "بَعْضُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُḍْʿعَb", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "بَعْضٍ", "translit": "ٍḍْʿعَb", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "سُبْحَٰنَ", "translit": "َnَٰḥْbُs", "root": "سبح"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "عَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "يَصِفُونَ", "translit": "َnwُfِṣَy", "root": "وصف"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 92, "text": "عَٰلِمِ ٱلْغَيْبِ وَٱلشَّهَٰدَةِ فَتَعَٰلَىٰ عَمَّا يُشْرِكُونَ ", "translation": "[He is] Knower of the unseen and the witnessed, so high is He above what they associate [with Him].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "عَٰلِمِ", "translit": "ِmِlَٰʿع", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْغَيْبِ", "translit": "ِbْyَغْlٱ", "root": "غيب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَٱلشَّهَٰدَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَdَٰhَّhslٱَw", "root": "شهد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَتَعَٰلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَٰʿعَtَf", "root": "علو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يُشْرِكُونَ", "translit": "َnwُkِrْhsُy", "root": "شرك"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 93, "text": "قُل رَّبِّ إِمَّا تُرِيَنِّى مَا يُوعَدُونَ ", "translation": "Say, [O Muḥammad], \"My Lord, if You should show me that which they are promised,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُل", "translit": "lُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَّبِّ", "translit": "ِّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِمَّا", "translit": "āَّmِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تُرِيَنِّى", "translit": "àِّnَyِrُt", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يُوعَدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdَʿعwُy", "root": "وعد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 94, "text": "رَبِّ فَلَا تَجْعَلْنِى فِى ٱلْقَوْمِ ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ ", "translation": "My Lord, then do not place me among the wrongdoing people.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "رَبِّ", "translit": "ِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فَلَا", "translit": "āَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تَجْعَلْنِى", "translit": "àِnْlَʿعْjgَt", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْقَوْمِ", "translit": "ِmْwَqْlٱ", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِlَّٰẓlٱ", "root": "ظلم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 95, "text": "وَإِنَّا عَلَىٰٓ أَن نُّرِيَكَ مَا نَعِدُهُمْ لَقَٰدِرُونَ ", "translation": "And indeed, We are Able to show you what We have promised them.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَلَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "نُّرِيَكَ", "translit": "َkَyِrُّn", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "نَعِدُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُdِʿعَn", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَقَٰدِرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrِdَٰqَl", "root": "قدر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 96, "text": "ٱدْفَعْ بِٱلَّتِى هِىَ أَحْسَنُ ٱلسَّيِّئَةَۚ نَحْنُ أَعْلَمُ بِمَا يَصِفُونَ ", "translation": "Repel, by [means of] what is best, [their] evil. We are most knowing of what they describe.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱدْفَعْ", "translit": "ْʿعَfْdٱ", "root": "دفع"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِٱلَّتِى", "translit": "àِtَّlٱِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "هِىَ", "translit": "َàِh", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَحْسَنُ", "translit": "ُnَsْḥَaʾ", "root": "حسن"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلسَّيِّئَةَ", "translit": "َẗَyʾِّyَّslٱ", "root": "سوا"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "نَحْنُ", "translit": "ُnْḥَn", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَaʾ", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يَصِفُونَ", "translit": "َnwُfِṣَy", "root": "وصف"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 97, "text": "وَقُل رَّبِّ أَعُوذُ بِكَ مِنْ هَمَزَٰتِ ٱلشَّيَٰطِينِ ", "translation": "And say, \"My Lord, I seek refuge in You from the incitements of the devils,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقُل", "translit": "lُqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَّبِّ", "translit": "ِّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَعُوذُ", "translit": "ُhdwُʿعَaʾ", "root": "عوذ"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِكَ", "translit": "َkِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "هَمَزَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰzَmَh", "root": "همز"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلشَّيَٰطِينِ", "translit": "ِnyِṭَٰyَّhslٱ", "root": "شطن"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 98, "text": "وَأَعُوذُ بِكَ رَبِّ أَن يَحْضُرُونِ ", "translation": "And I seek refuge in You, my Lord, lest they be present with me.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَعُوذُ", "translit": "ُhdwُʿعَaʾَw", "root": "عوذ"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِكَ", "translit": "َkِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "رَبِّ", "translit": "ِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَحْضُرُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُrُḍْḥَy", "root": "حضر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 99, "text": "حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا جَآءَ أَحَدَهُمُ ٱلْمَوْتُ قَالَ رَبِّ ٱرْجِعُونِ ", "translation": "[For such is the state of the disbelievers] until, when death comes to one of them, he says, \"My Lord, send me back", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "حَتَّىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَّtَḥ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "جَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَحَدَهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhَdَḥَaʾ", "root": "احد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْمَوْتُ", "translit": "ُtْwَmْlٱ", "root": "موت"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "رَبِّ", "translit": "ِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱرْجِعُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُʿعِjgْrٱ", "root": "رجع"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 100, "text": "لَعَلِّىٓ أَعْمَلُ صَٰلِحًا فِيمَا تَرَكْتُۚ كَلَّآۚ إِنَّهَا كَلِمَةٌ هُوَ قَآئِلُهَاۖ وَمِن وَرَآئِهِم بَرْزَخٌ إِلَىٰ يَوْمِ يُبْعَثُونَ ", "translation": "That I might do righteousness in that which I left behind.\"[1] No! It is only a word he is saying; and behind them is a barrier until the Day they are resurrected.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَعَلِّىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِّlَʿعَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَعْمَلُ", "translit": "ُlَmْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "صَٰلِحًا", "translit": "āًḥِlَٰṣ", "root": "صلح"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِيمَا", "translit": "āَmyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "تَرَكْتُ", "translit": "ُtْkَrَt", "root": "ترك"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "كَلَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّlَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِنَّهَا", "translit": "āَhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كَلِمَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَmِlَk", "root": "كلم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "قَآئِلُهَا", "translit": "āَhُlِyʾٓāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَمِن", "translit": "nِmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَرَآئِهِم", "translit": "mِhِyʾٓāَrَw", "root": "وري"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "بَرْزَخٌ", "translit": "ٌhkَzْrَb", "root": "برزخ"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "يَوْمِ", "translit": "ِmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "يُبْعَثُونَ", "translit": "َnwُhtَʿعْbُy", "root": "بعث"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 101, "text": "فَإِذَا نُفِخَ فِى ٱلصُّورِ فَلَآ أَنسَابَ بَيْنَهُمْ يَوْمَئِذٍ وَلَا يَتَسَآءَلُونَ ", "translation": "So when the Horn is blown, no relationship will there be among them that Day, nor will they ask about one another.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "نُفِخَ", "translit": "َhkِfُn", "root": "نفخ"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلصُّورِ", "translit": "ِrwُّṣlٱ", "root": "صور"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَلَآ", "translit": "ٓāَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَنسَابَ", "translit": "َbāَsnَaʾ", "root": "نسب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بَيْنَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَوْمَئِذٍ", "translit": "ٍhdِyʾَmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "يَتَسَآءَلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlَءٓāَsَtَy", "root": "سال"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 102, "text": "فَمَن ثَقُلَتْ مَوَٰزِينُهُۥ فَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ هُمُ ٱلْمُفْلِحُونَ ", "translation": "And those whose scales are heavy [with good deeds] - it is they who are the successful.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَمَن", "translit": "nَmَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ثَقُلَتْ", "translit": "ْtَlُqَht", "root": "ثقل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَوَٰزِينُهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُnyِzَٰwَm", "root": "وزن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "هُمُ", "translit": "ُmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْمُفْلِحُونَ", "translit": "َnwُḥِlْfُmْlٱ", "root": "فلح"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 103, "text": "وَمَنْ خَفَّتْ مَوَٰزِينُهُۥ فَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ ٱلَّذِينَ خَسِرُوٓا۟ أَنفُسَهُمْ فِى جَهَنَّمَ خَٰلِدُونَ ", "translation": "But those whose scales are light - those are the ones who have lost their souls, [being] in Hell, abiding eternally.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَنْ", "translit": "ْnَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "خَفَّتْ", "translit": "ْtَّfَhk", "root": "خفف"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَوَٰزِينُهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُnyِzَٰwَm", "root": "وزن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "خَسِرُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُrِsَhk", "root": "خسر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَنفُسَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَsُfnَaʾ", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "جَهَنَّمَ", "translit": "َmَّnَhَjg", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "خَٰلِدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdِlَٰhk", "root": "خلد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 104, "text": "تَلْفَحُ وُجُوهَهُمُ ٱلنَّارُ وَهُمْ فِيهَا كَٰلِحُونَ ", "translation": "The Fire will sear their faces, and they therein will have taut smiles.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "تَلْفَحُ", "translit": "ُḥَfْlَt", "root": "لفح"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وُجُوهَهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhَhwُjgُw", "root": "وجه"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلنَّارُ", "translit": "ُrāَّnlٱ", "root": "نور"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "كَٰلِحُونَ", "translit": "َnwُḥِlَٰk", "root": "كلح"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 105, "text": "أَلَمْ تَكُنْ ءَايَٰتِى تُتْلَىٰ عَلَيْكُمْ فَكُنتُم بِهَا تُكَذِّبُونَ ", "translation": "[It will be said], \"Were not My verses recited to you and you used to deny them?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَكُنْ", "translit": "ْnُkَt", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَايَٰتِى", "translit": "àِtَٰyāَء", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تُتْلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlْtُt", "root": "تلو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَلَيْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَكُنتُم", "translit": "mُtnُkَf", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بِهَا", "translit": "āَhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تُكَذِّبُونَ", "translit": "َnwُbِّhdَkُt", "root": "كذب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 106, "text": "قَالُوا۟ رَبَّنَا غَلَبَتْ عَلَيْنَا شِقْوَتُنَا وَكُنَّا قَوْمًا ضَآلِّينَ ", "translation": "They will say, \"Our Lord, our wretchedness overcame us, and we were a people astray.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَبَّنَا", "translit": "āَnَّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "غَلَبَتْ", "translit": "ْtَbَlَغ", "root": "غلب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَلَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "شِقْوَتُنَا", "translit": "āَnُtَwْqِhs", "root": "شقو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَكُنَّا", "translit": "āَّnُkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "قَوْمًا", "translit": "āًmْwَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ضَآلِّينَ", "translit": "َnyِّlٓāَḍ", "root": "ضلل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 107, "text": "رَبَّنَآ أَخْرِجْنَا مِنْهَا فَإِنْ عُدْنَا فَإِنَّا ظَٰلِمُونَ ", "translation": "Our Lord, remove us from it, and if we were to return [to evil], we would indeed be wrongdoers.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "رَبَّنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnَّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَخْرِجْنَا", "translit": "āَnْjgِrْhkَaʾ", "root": "خرج"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِنْهَا", "translit": "āَhْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَإِنْ", "translit": "ْnِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عُدْنَا", "translit": "āَnْdُʿع", "root": "عود"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَإِنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ظَٰلِمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmِlَٰẓ", "root": "ظلم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 108, "text": "قَالَ ٱخْسَـُٔوا۟ فِيهَا وَلَا تُكَلِّمُونِ ", "translation": "He will say, \"Remain despised therein and do not speak to Me.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱخْسَـُٔوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُٔـَsْhkٱ", "root": "خسا"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "تُكَلِّمُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُmِّlَkُt", "root": "كلم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 109, "text": "إِنَّهُۥ كَانَ فَرِيقٌ مِّنْ عِبَادِى يَقُولُونَ رَبَّنَآ ءَامَنَّا فَٱغْفِرْ لَنَا وَٱرْحَمْنَا وَأَنتَ خَيْرُ ٱلرَّٰحِمِينَ ", "translation": "Indeed, there was a party of My servants who said, 'Our Lord, we have believed, so forgive us and have mercy upon us, and You are the best of the merciful.'", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَرِيقٌ", "translit": "ٌqyِrَf", "root": "فرق"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِّنْ", "translit": "ْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عِبَادِى", "translit": "àِdāَbِʿع", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَقُولُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlwُqَy", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "رَبَّنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnَّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ءَامَنَّا", "translit": "āَّnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فَٱغْفِرْ", "translit": "ْrِfْغٱَf", "root": "غفر"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لَنَا", "translit": "āَnَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَٱرْحَمْنَا", "translit": "āَnْmَḥْrٱَw", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَأَنتَ", "translit": "َtnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "خَيْرُ", "translit": "ُrْyَhk", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلرَّٰحِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِḥَّٰrlٱ", "root": "رحم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 110, "text": "فَٱتَّخَذْتُمُوهُمْ سِخْرِيًّا حَتَّىٰٓ أَنسَوْكُمْ ذِكْرِى وَكُنتُم مِّنْهُمْ تَضْحَكُونَ ", "translation": "But you took them in mockery to the point that they made you forget My remembrance, and you used to laugh at them.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَٱتَّخَذْتُمُوهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhwُmُtْhdَhkَّtٱَf", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "سِخْرِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِrْhkِs", "root": "سخر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "حَتَّىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَّtَḥ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَنسَوْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْwَsnَaʾ", "root": "نسي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ذِكْرِى", "translit": "àِrْkِhd", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَكُنتُم", "translit": "mُtnُkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِّنْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تَضْحَكُونَ", "translit": "َnwُkَḥْḍَt", "root": "ضحك"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 111, "text": "إِنِّى جَزَيْتُهُمُ ٱلْيَوْمَ بِمَا صَبَرُوٓا۟ أَنَّهُمْ هُمُ ٱلْفَآئِزُونَ ", "translation": "Indeed, I have rewarded them this Day for their patient endurance - that they are the attainers [of success].\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنِّى", "translit": "àِّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "جَزَيْتُهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhُtْyَzَjg", "root": "جزي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْيَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَyْlٱ", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "صَبَرُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُrَbَṣ", "root": "صبر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَنَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "هُمُ", "translit": "ُmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْفَآئِزُونَ", "translit": "َnwُzِyʾٓāَfْlٱ", "root": "فوز"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 112, "text": "قَٰلَ كَمْ لَبِثْتُمْ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ عَدَدَ سِنِينَ ", "translation": "[Allāh] will say, \"How long did you remain on earth in number of years?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَٰلَ", "translit": "َlَٰq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَمْ", "translit": "ْmَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَبِثْتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtْhtِbَl", "root": "لبث"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَدَدَ", "translit": "َdَdَʿع", "root": "عدد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "سِنِينَ", "translit": "َnyِnِs", "root": "سنو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 113, "text": "قَالُوا۟ لَبِثْنَا يَوْمًا أَوْ بَعْضَ يَوْمٍ فَسْـَٔلِ ٱلْعَآدِّينَ ", "translation": "They will say, \"We remained a day or part of a day; ask those who enumerate.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَبِثْنَا", "translit": "āَnْhtِbَl", "root": "لبث"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَوْمًا", "translit": "āًmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بَعْضَ", "translit": "َḍْʿعَb", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَوْمٍ", "translit": "ٍmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَسْـَٔلِ", "translit": "ِlَٔـْsَf", "root": "سال"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْعَآدِّينَ", "translit": "َnyِّdٓāَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عدد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 114, "text": "قَٰلَ إِن لَّبِثْتُمْ إِلَّا قَلِيلًاۖ لَّوْ أَنَّكُمْ كُنتُمْ تَعْلَمُونَ ", "translation": "He will say, \"You stayed not but a little - if only you had known.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَٰلَ", "translit": "َlَٰq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَّبِثْتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtْhtِbَّl", "root": "لبث"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَلِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِlَq", "root": "قلل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَّوْ", "translit": "ْwَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَنَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كُنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَعْلَمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmَlْʿعَt", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 115, "text": "أَفَحَسِبْتُمْ أَنَّمَا خَلَقْنَٰكُمْ عَبَثًا وَأَنَّكُمْ إِلَيْنَا لَا تُرْجَعُونَ ", "translation": "Then did you think that We created you uselessly and that to Us you would not be returned?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَفَحَسِبْتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtْbِsَḥَfَaʾ", "root": "حسب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَنَّمَا", "translit": "āَmَّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "خَلَقْنَٰكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَٰnْqَlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَبَثًا", "translit": "āًhtَbَʿع", "root": "عبث"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَأَنَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِلَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تُرْجَعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعَjgْrُt", "root": "رجع"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 116, "text": "فَتَعَٰلَى ٱللَّهُ ٱلْمَلِكُ ٱلْحَقُّۖ لَآ إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا هُوَ رَبُّ ٱلْعَرْشِ ٱلْكَرِيمِ ", "translation": "So exalted is Allāh, the Sovereign,[1] the Truth;[2] there is no deity except Him, Lord of the Noble Throne.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَتَعَٰلَى", "translit": "àَlَٰʿعَtَf", "root": "علو"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْمَلِكُ", "translit": "ُkِlَmْlٱ", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْحَقُّ", "translit": "ُّqَḥْlٱ", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَآ", "translit": "ٓāَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِلَٰهَ", "translit": "َhَٰlِiʾ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "رَبُّ", "translit": "ُّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْعَرْشِ", "translit": "ِhsْrَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عرش"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْكَرِيمِ", "translit": "ِmyِrَkْlٱ", "root": "كرم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 117, "text": "وَمَن يَدْعُ مَعَ ٱللَّهِ إِلَٰهًا ءَاخَرَ لَا بُرْهَٰنَ لَهُۥ بِهِۦ فَإِنَّمَا حِسَابُهُۥ عِندَ رَبِّهِۦٓۚ إِنَّهُۥ لَا يُفْلِحُ ٱلْكَٰفِرُونَ ", "translation": "And whoever invokes besides Allāh another deity for which he has no proof - then his account is only with his Lord. Indeed, the disbelievers will not succeed.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَدْعُ", "translit": "ُʿعْdَy", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَعَ", "translit": "َʿعَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِلَٰهًا", "translit": "āًhَٰlِiʾ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ءَاخَرَ", "translit": "َrَhkāَء", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بُرْهَٰنَ", "translit": "َnَٰhْrُb", "root": "برهن"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "فَإِنَّمَا", "translit": "āَmَّnِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "حِسَابُهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُbāَsِḥ", "root": "حسب"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "عِندَ", "translit": "َdnِʿع", "root": "عند"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "رَبِّهِۦٓ", "translit": "ٓۦِhِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "إِنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "يُفْلِحُ", "translit": "ُḥِlْfُy", "root": "فلح"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱلْكَٰفِرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrِfَٰkْlٱ", "root": "كفر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 118, "text": "وَقُل رَّبِّ ٱغْفِرْ وَٱرْحَمْ وَأَنتَ خَيْرُ ٱلرَّٰحِمِينَ ", "translation": "And, [O Muḥammad], say, \"My Lord, forgive and have mercy, and You are the best of the merciful.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقُل", "translit": "lُqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَّبِّ", "translit": "ِّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱغْفِرْ", "translit": "ْrِfْغٱ", "root": "غفر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَٱرْحَمْ", "translit": "ْmَḥْrٱَw", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَأَنتَ", "translit": "َtnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "خَيْرُ", "translit": "ُrْyَhk", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلرَّٰحِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِḥَّٰrlٱ", "root": "رحم"}], "hasIhya": true}], "tafsir": {"1": [{"arabic": "الله تعالى {قد أفلح المؤمنون الذين هم في صلاتهم خاشعون والذين هم عن اللغو معرضون} إلى قوله {أولئك هم الوارثون} وقال عز وجل {التائبون العابدون الحامدون} إلى قوله {وبشر المؤمنين} وقال عز وجل {إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم} إلى قوله {أولئك هم المؤمنون حقا} وقال تعالى {وعباد الرحمن الذين يمشون على الأرض هونا وإذا خاطبهم الجاهلون قالوا سلاما} إلى آخر السورة من أشكل عليه حاله فليعرض نفسه على هذه الآيات فوجود جميع هذه الصفات علامة حسن الخلق وفقد جميعها علامة سوء الخلق ووجود بعضها دون بعض يدل على البعض دون البعض فليشتغل بتحصيل", "english": "The foundational principle in this matter is that each person should abandon that which brings him joy from among the causes of this world. So whoever delights in wealth, or in status, or in acceptance during preaching, or in honor through judgeship and governance, or in the abundance of followers in teaching and benefitting others—he should first abandon that which brings him joy. For if he is prevented from something of that sort, and it is said to him: \"Your reward is in the Hereafter,\" and his reward is not diminished by the prevention, yet he dislikes that and is pained by it—then he is among those who rejoice in the life of this world and are content with it, and that is destructive for him. Then, when he has abandoned the causes of joy, let him withdraw from people, let him seclude himself with his own soul, and let him watch over his heart so that it is occupied with nothing but the remembrance of Allah, the Exalted, and reflection upon Him. And let him lie in wait for whatever desire or whisper (shahwah wa waswās) appears in his soul, so that he may crush its substance whenever it emerges. For every whisper has a cause, and it does not vanish except by severing that cause and attachment. And let him persist in this for the remainder of his life, for striving has no end except death.", "book_title": "Breaking the Two Desires", "section_title_ar": "Section 630", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 630: The Signs of Good Character and the Path of Spiritual Striving", "section_index": 630, "anchors": "- Root: جهد (j-h-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Exertion of one's utmost capacity; enduring hardship in pursuit of a goal.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-jihād is the soul's taking itself to the limit of its capacity while bearing difficulty; it is the means by which the lower self is subdued.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical toil and strain. Metaphorical: the striving of the soul against its passions, as in \"ijtahada ra'yahu\" (he exerted his judgment).\n- Root: حسن (ḥ-s-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Beauty, goodness, and excellence in form and conduct.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): That which is desirable and pleasing—whether by reason, inclination, or sense; ḥusn al-khuluq is the beauty of the soul's inward disposition.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical beauty and adornment. Metaphorical: moral excellence, as in \"ḥasuna khuluquhu\" (his character became good).\n- Root: خلق (kh-l-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Measuring, fashioning, and forming; also innate disposition and character.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Origination upon a just measure; for character, it denotes the soul's settled form and inward constitution.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: shaping and smoothing. Metaphorical: the created nature and moral temperament of a person.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Specification (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — The particle \"innamā\" restricts the predicate to the subject alone, as in the verse {«...»} \"Innamā al-muʾminūna...\" (The believers are only those...), which confines true faith to those possessing the enumerated attributes.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Breaking the Two Desires'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "قال الله عز وجل قد أفلح المؤمنون الذين هم في صلاتهم خاشعون فمدحهم بعد الإيمان بصلاة مخصوصة وهي المقرونة بالخشوع ثم ختم أوصاف المفلحين بالصلاة أيضا فقال تعالى والذين هم على صلواتهم يحافظون ثم قال تعالى في ثمرة تلك الصفات أولئك هم الوارثون الذين يرثون الفردوس هم فيها خالدون فوصفهم بالفلاح أولا وبوراثة الفردوس آخرا وما عندي أن هذرمة اللسان مع غفلة القلب تنتهي إلى هذا الحد ولذلك قال الله عز وجل في أضدادهم ما سلككم في سقر قالوا لم نك من المصلين فالمصلون هم ورثة الفردوس وهم المشاهدون لنور الله تعالى والمتمتعو", "english": "For the friends of God (awliyāʾ Allāh) who are granted unveiling of the dominion of the heavens and the earth (malakūt al-samāwāt wa-al-arḍ) and the secrets of lordship (asrār al-rubūbiyyah) — they are unveiled only in prayer, especially in prostration (sujūd), for the servant draws near to his Lord (Mighty and Majestic) through prostration. For this reason, He said, Most High: {«And prostrate and draw near»} (Q 96:19). And the unveiling of each worshiper is only to the measure of his purity from the turbidities of this world. It varies in strength and weakness, in abundance and scarcity, and in clarity and obscurity — until some are unveiled the very thing itself (al-shayʾ bi-ʿaynihi), while others are unveiled its likeness (al-shayʾ bi-mithālihi), just as some were shown this world in the form of a corpse, and Satan in the form of a dog crouching upon it, calling people to it.", "book_title": "Mysteries of Prayer", "section_title_ar": "Section 140", "section_title_en": "The Prayer of the Humble and the Unveiling of the Unveiled — Section 140", "section_index": 140, "anchors": "- Root: خ ش ع (kh-sh-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of humility, submissiveness, and lowering oneself; the physical posture of one who is abased before a greater power.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological terms, khushūʿ is the state of the heart's stillness and trembling before divine majesty, manifesting in the body's composure during ritual prayer (ṣalāh).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) refers to the physical lowering of the gaze and voice; metaphorical (majāz) extends to the soul's inner quietude and awe before God.\n- Root: ك ش ف (k-sh-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To remove a covering, unveil, disclose; the act of stripping away an obstruction to reveal what lies beneath.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In Quranic usage, kashf denotes divine removal of veils (kashf al-ḥijāb) — the uncovering of unseen realities (ghayb) to the heart, as in {«We have removed from you your covering»} (Q 50:22).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: uncovering a physical object; metaphorical: the disclosure of spiritual truths (mukāshafah) to the purified heart, a technical term in Sufi epistemology.\n- Root: ص ل و (ṣ-l-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes supplication, blessing, and the act of ritual prayer; also the burning of fire (iṣlāʾ), suggesting the consuming fervor of devotion.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-ṣalāh is the act of turning toward God in worship, encompassing both the physical rites and the inner orientation of the soul; its perfection lies in the presence of the heart.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the performance of the prescribed ritual; metaphorical: the soul's \"burning\" with longing and its purification through the fire of sincere devotion.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The use of restrictive particles to single out the specific quality (khushūʿ) as the defining attribute of the believers' prayer, distinguishing it from mere mechanical performance.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Mysteries of Prayer'", "badge": "Insight"}], "2": [{"arabic": "فهذا تفصيل صلاة الخاشعين الذين هم في صلاتهم خاشعون والذين هم على صلواتهم يحافظون والذين هم على صلاتهم دائمون والذين هم يناجون الله على قدر استطاعتهم في العبودية فليعرض الإنسان نفسه على هذه الصلاة فبالقدر الذي يسر له منه ينبغي أن يفرح وعلى ما يفوته ينبغي أن يتحسر وفي مداراة ذلك ينبغي أن يجتهد وأما صلاة الغافلين فهي مخطرة إلا أن يتغمده الله برحمته والرحمة واسعة والكرم فائض فنسأل الله أن يتغمدنا برحمته ويغمرنا بمغفرته إذ لا وسيلة لنا إلا الاعتراف بالعجز عن القيام بطاعته واعلم ان تخليص الصلاة عن الآ", "english": "For the friends of God (awliyāʾ Allāh) who are granted unveiling of the dominion of the heavens and the earth (malakūt al-samāwāt wa-al-arḍ) and the secrets of lordship (asrār al-rubūbiyyah) — they are unveiled only in prayer, especially in prostration (sujūd), for the servant draws near to his Lord (Mighty and Majestic) through prostration. For this reason, He said, Most High: {«And prostrate and draw near»} (Q 96:19). And the unveiling of each worshiper is only to the measure of his purity from the turbidities of this world. It varies in strength and weakness, in abundance and scarcity, and in clarity and obscurity — until some are unveiled the very thing itself (al-shayʾ bi-ʿaynihi), while others are unveiled its likeness (al-shayʾ bi-mithālihi), just as some were shown this world in the form of a corpse, and Satan in the form of a dog crouching upon it, calling people to it.", "book_title": "Mysteries of Prayer", "section_title_ar": "Section 140", "section_title_en": "The Prayer of the Humble and the Unveiling of the Unveiled — Section 140", "section_index": 140, "anchors": "- Root: خ ش ع (kh-sh-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of humility, submissiveness, and lowering oneself; the physical posture of one who is abased before a greater power.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological terms, khushūʿ is the state of the heart's stillness and trembling before divine majesty, manifesting in the body's composure during ritual prayer (ṣalāh).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) refers to the physical lowering of the gaze and voice; metaphorical (majāz) extends to the soul's inner quietude and awe before God.\n- Root: ك ش ف (k-sh-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To remove a covering, unveil, disclose; the act of stripping away an obstruction to reveal what lies beneath.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In Quranic usage, kashf denotes divine removal of veils (kashf al-ḥijāb) — the uncovering of unseen realities (ghayb) to the heart, as in {«We have removed from you your covering»} (Q 50:22).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: uncovering a physical object; metaphorical: the disclosure of spiritual truths (mukāshafah) to the purified heart, a technical term in Sufi epistemology.\n- Root: ص ل و (ṣ-l-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes supplication, blessing, and the act of ritual prayer; also the burning of fire (iṣlāʾ), suggesting the consuming fervor of devotion.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-ṣalāh is the act of turning toward God in worship, encompassing both the physical rites and the inner orientation of the soul; its perfection lies in the presence of the heart.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the performance of the prescribed ritual; metaphorical: the soul's \"burning\" with longing and its purification through the fire of sincere devotion.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The use of restrictive particles to single out the specific quality (khushūʿ) as the defining attribute of the believers' prayer, distinguishing it from mere mechanical performance.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Mysteries of Prayer'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "8": [{"arabic": "وبقوله تعالى {والذين هم لأماناتهم وعهدهم راعون}", "english": "I say: Every human being is never free from disobedience of the bodily members (jawāriḥ), for even the prophets were not free from it — as has come down in the Qurʾān and the reports concerning the sins of the prophets, their repentance, and their weeping over their misdeeds. And if one is free, in some states, from disobedience of the members, he is not free from the heart's inclination (hamm) toward sins. And if he is free, in some states, from the inclination, he is not free from the whispering of Satan (waswās al-shayṭān) by way of importing scattered thoughts that divert from the remembrance of God. And if he is free from that, he is not free from heedlessness (ghaflah) and deficiency in knowledge of God, His attributes, and His acts. All of that is deficiency (nuqṣān), and it has causes; and abandoning its causes by occupying oneself with their opposites is a reversion from one path to its contrary. The meaning of \"repentance\" (tawbah) is \"returning\" (rujūʿ), and it is inconceivable that a human being should be entirely free of this deficiency — rather, people differ only in degrees. As for the principle itself, it is unavoidable. For this reason, he (peace be upon him) said: \"Truly, a veil is cast over my heart, so that I seek forgiveness from God seventy times a day and night\" (1) — the hadith. And for this reason, God (exalted be He) honored him by saying: {«لِيَغْفِرَ لَكَ اللَّهُ مَا تَقَدَّمَ مِنْ ذَنْبِكَ وَمَا تَأَخَّرَ»} [That God may forgive you what has passed of your sin and what remains]. And if this was his state, how then is the state of anyone else?", "book_title": "Monitoring and Accounting", "section_title_ar": "Section 922", "section_title_en": "On the Universality of Repentance (Tawbah) and Its Obligation in Every Breath", "section_index": 922, "anchors": "- Root: توب (T-W-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of \"returning\" (rujūʿ) — turning back from a path to its opposite; the root denotes the act of revisiting and reversing a prior state.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Tawbah is \"abandoning the sin in the most excellent manner\" — the most complete form of excuse; in sacred law (sharʿ), it is abandoning the sin due to its ugliness, regretting what has passed, resolving not to return, and rectifying what can be rectified of past deeds through renewed acts.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"He repented to God from his sin\" (tāba al-ʿabdu ilā Allāhi min dhanbihi). Metaphorical (majāz): The extension of \"repentance\" to inner purification of the heart from accumulated rust (rayn), as the root's sense of \"returning\" is applied to the soul's reversion from negligence to vigilance.\n- Root: قلب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The heart as the organ of turning and overturning (taqlīb) — its state fluctuates between illumination and darkness, purity and rust.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Turning a thing\" is directing it from one face to another; the heart (qalb) is so named for its constant overturning between states — a key metaphysical anchor for Ghazali's doctrine of the heart's susceptibility to stains (akdār) and polish (ṣaqal).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"He turned the thing over\" (qalaba al-shayʾ). Metaphorical: \"The heart is rusted\" (qalb wajīb) — the inner organ's receptivity to impressions, whether dark (from sins) or luminous (from obedience).\n- Root: وجب (W-J-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Obligation as fixed establishment (thubūt); what is due and settled upon a person.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Wājib has two senses: (1) that whose non-existence entails a contradiction (metaphysical necessity), and (2) that whose neglect merits blame — subdivided into what reason deems obligatory (e.g., knowing divine unity) and what the Law deems obligatory (e.g., acts of worship). Ghazali here exploits this dual sense to distinguish legal obligation (fatwā) from the higher obligation of the path (ṭarīq).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"It became incumbent upon him\" (wajaba ʿalayhi). Metaphorical: The extension to \"the heart's palpitation\" (wajīb) — the inner agitation of one who realizes the urgency of repentance before death.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Focused Predication (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — governing the Quranic citations {«إِنَّمَا التَّوْبَةُ عَلَى اللَّهِ»} and {«وَلَيْسَتِ التَّوْبَةُ لِلَّذِينَ»}, where innamā restricts the predicate to a specific subject, delimiting the scope of accepted repentance to those who turn before death's arrival and before the rust hardens into a seal.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Monitoring and Accounting'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "9": [{"arabic": "فهذا تفصيل صلاة الخاشعين الذين هم في صلاتهم خاشعون والذين هم على صلواتهم يحافظون والذين هم على صلاتهم دائمون والذين هم يناجون الله على قدر استطاعتهم في العبودية فليعرض الإنسان نفسه على هذه الصلاة فبالقدر الذي يسر له منه ينبغي أن يفرح وعلى ما يفوته ينبغي أن يتحسر وفي مداراة ذلك ينبغي أن يجتهد وأما صلاة الغافلين فهي مخطرة إلا أن يتغمده الله برحمته والرحمة واسعة والكرم فائض فنسأل الله أن يتغمدنا برحمته ويغمرنا بمغفرته إذ لا وسيلة لنا إلا الاعتراف بالعجز عن القيام بطاعته واعلم ان تخليص الصلاة عن الآفات وإخلاصها لوجه الله عز وجل و", "english": "For the friends of God (awliyāʾ Allāh) who are granted unveiling of the dominion of the heavens and the earth (malakūt al-samāwāt wa-al-arḍ) and the secrets of lordship (asrār al-rubūbiyyah) — they are unveiled only in prayer, especially in prostration (sujūd), for the servant draws near to his Lord (Mighty and Majestic) through prostration. For this reason, He said, Most High: {«And prostrate and draw near»} (Q 96:19). And the unveiling of each worshiper is only to the measure of his purity from the turbidities of this world. It varies in strength and weakness, in abundance and scarcity, and in clarity and obscurity — until some are unveiled the very thing itself (al-shayʾ bi-ʿaynihi), while others are unveiled its likeness (al-shayʾ bi-mithālihi), just as some were shown this world in the form of a corpse, and Satan in the form of a dog crouching upon it, calling people to it.", "book_title": "Mysteries of Prayer", "section_title_ar": "Section 140", "section_title_en": "The Prayer of the Humble and the Unveiling of the Unveiled — Section 140", "section_index": 140, "anchors": "- Root: خ ش ع (kh-sh-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of humility, submissiveness, and lowering oneself; the physical posture of one who is abased before a greater power.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological terms, khushūʿ is the state of the heart's stillness and trembling before divine majesty, manifesting in the body's composure during ritual prayer (ṣalāh).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) refers to the physical lowering of the gaze and voice; metaphorical (majāz) extends to the soul's inner quietude and awe before God.\n- Root: ك ش ف (k-sh-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To remove a covering, unveil, disclose; the act of stripping away an obstruction to reveal what lies beneath.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In Quranic usage, kashf denotes divine removal of veils (kashf al-ḥijāb) — the uncovering of unseen realities (ghayb) to the heart, as in {«We have removed from you your covering»} (Q 50:22).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: uncovering a physical object; metaphorical: the disclosure of spiritual truths (mukāshafah) to the purified heart, a technical term in Sufi epistemology.\n- Root: ص ل و (ṣ-l-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes supplication, blessing, and the act of ritual prayer; also the burning of fire (iṣlāʾ), suggesting the consuming fervor of devotion.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-ṣalāh is the act of turning toward God in worship, encompassing both the physical rites and the inner orientation of the soul; its perfection lies in the presence of the heart.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the performance of the prescribed ritual; metaphorical: the soul's \"burning\" with longing and its purification through the fire of sincere devotion.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The use of restrictive particles to single out the specific quality (khushūʿ) as the defining attribute of the believers' prayer, distinguishing it from mere mechanical performance.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Mysteries of Prayer'", "badge": "Insight"}], "10": [{"arabic": "يحافظون ثم قال تعالى في ثمرة تلك الصفات أولئك هم الوارثون الذين يرثون الفردوس هم فيها خالدون فوصفهم بالفلاح أولا وبوراثة الفردوس آخرا وما عندي أن هذرمة اللسان مع غفلة القلب تنتهي إلى هذا الحد ولذلك قال الله عز وجل في أضدادهم ما سلككم في سقر قالوا لم نك من المصلين فالمصلون هم ورثة الفردوس وهم المشاهدون لنور الله تعالى والمتمتعون بقربه ودنوه من قلوبهم نسأل الله أن يجعلنا منهم وأن يعيذنا من عقوبة من تزينت أقواله وقبحت أفعاله إنه الكريم المنان القديم الإحسان وصلى الله على كل عبد مصطفى", "english": "For the friends of God (awliyāʾ Allāh) who are granted unveiling of the dominion of the heavens and the earth (malakūt al-samāwāt wa-al-arḍ) and the secrets of lordship (asrār al-rubūbiyyah) — they are unveiled only in prayer, especially in prostration (sujūd), for the servant draws near to his Lord (Mighty and Majestic) through prostration. For this reason, He said, Most High: {«And prostrate and draw near»} (Q 96:19). And the unveiling of each worshiper is only to the measure of his purity from the turbidities of this world. It varies in strength and weakness, in abundance and scarcity, and in clarity and obscurity — until some are unveiled the very thing itself (al-shayʾ bi-ʿaynihi), while others are unveiled its likeness (al-shayʾ bi-mithālihi), just as some were shown this world in the form of a corpse, and Satan in the form of a dog crouching upon it, calling people to it.", "book_title": "Mysteries of Prayer", "section_title_ar": "Section 140", "section_title_en": "The Prayer of the Humble and the Unveiling of the Unveiled — Section 140", "section_index": 140, "anchors": "- Root: خ ش ع (kh-sh-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of humility, submissiveness, and lowering oneself; the physical posture of one who is abased before a greater power.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological terms, khushūʿ is the state of the heart's stillness and trembling before divine majesty, manifesting in the body's composure during ritual prayer (ṣalāh).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) refers to the physical lowering of the gaze and voice; metaphorical (majāz) extends to the soul's inner quietude and awe before God.\n- Root: ك ش ف (k-sh-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To remove a covering, unveil, disclose; the act of stripping away an obstruction to reveal what lies beneath.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In Quranic usage, kashf denotes divine removal of veils (kashf al-ḥijāb) — the uncovering of unseen realities (ghayb) to the heart, as in {«We have removed from you your covering»} (Q 50:22).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: uncovering a physical object; metaphorical: the disclosure of spiritual truths (mukāshafah) to the purified heart, a technical term in Sufi epistemology.\n- Root: ص ل و (ṣ-l-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes supplication, blessing, and the act of ritual prayer; also the burning of fire (iṣlāʾ), suggesting the consuming fervor of devotion.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-ṣalāh is the act of turning toward God in worship, encompassing both the physical rites and the inner orientation of the soul; its perfection lies in the presence of the heart.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the performance of the prescribed ritual; metaphorical: the soul's \"burning\" with longing and its purification through the fire of sincere devotion.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The use of restrictive particles to single out the specific quality (khushūʿ) as the defining attribute of the believers' prayer, distinguishing it from mere mechanical performance.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Mysteries of Prayer'", "badge": "Insight"}], "12": [{"arabic": "ثم ذكر كيف جعل النطفة علقة والعلقة مضغة والمضغة عظاما فقال تعالى ولقد خلقنا الإنسان من سلالة من طين ثم جعلناه نطفة في قرار مكين ثم خلقنا النطفة علقة الآية فتكرير ذكر النطفة في الكتاب العزيز ليس ليسمع لفظه ويترك التفكر في معناه فانظر الآن إلى النطفة وهي قطرة من الماء قذرة لو تركت ساعة ليضربها الهواء فسدت وأنتنت كيف أخرجها رب الأرباب من الصلب والترائب وكيف جمع بين الذكر والأنثى وألقى الألفة والمحبة في قلوبهم وكيف قادهم بسلسلة المحبة والشهوة إلى الاجتماع وكيف استخرج النطفة من الرجل بحركة الوقاع وكيف استجلب دم الحيض من أعماق العروق وجمعه في الرحم ثم كيف خلق المولود من النطفة وسقاه بماء الحيض وغ", "english": "I say: The Exalted said: {أَلَمْ يَكُ نُطْفَةً مِنْ مَنِيٍّ يُمْنَىٰ ثُمَّ كَانَ عَلَقَةً فَخَلَقَ فَسَوَّىٰ} [Was he not a sperm-drop from semen emitted? Then he was a clinging clot, and He created and proportioned him] (Al-Qiyamah: 37-38). And the Exalted said: {أَلَمْ نَخْلُقْكُمْ مِنْ مَاءٍ مَهِينٍ فَجَعَلْنَاهُ فِي قَرَارٍ مَكِينٍ إِلَىٰ قَدَرٍ مَعْلُومٍ} [Did We not create you from a despicable fluid, then We placed it in a firm resting-place for a known term?] (Al-Mursalat: 20-22). And He said: {أَوَلَمْ يَرَ الْإِنْسَانُ أَنَّا خَلَقْنَاهُ مِنْ نُطْفَةٍ فَإِذَا هُوَ خَصِيمٌ مُبِينٌ} [Does man not see that We created him from a sperm-drop, yet behold, he is a manifest adversary?] (Ya-Sin: 77). And He said: {إِنَّا خَلَقْنَا الْإِنْسَانَ مِنْ نُطْفَةٍ أَمْشَاجٍ} [Indeed, We created man from a mixed sperm-drop] (Al-Insan: 2). Then He mentioned how He made the sperm-drop a clinging clot, the clot a chewed lump, and the lump bones, saying: {وَلَقَدْ خَلَقْنَا الْإِنْسَانَ مِنْ سُلَالَةٍ مِنْ طِينٍ ثُمَّ جَعَلْنَاهُ نُطْفَةً فِي قَرَارٍ مَكِينٍ ثُمَّ خَلَقْنَا النُّطْفَةَ عَلَقَةً} [And indeed We created man from an extract of clay, then We made him a sperm-drop in a firm resting-place, then We created the sperm-drop into a clinging clot] (Al-Mu'minun: 12-14), to the end of the verse.", "book_title": "Contemplation", "section_title_ar": "Section 1269", "section_title_en": "The Wonders of Human Creation from a Sperm-Drop — Section 1269", "section_index": 1269, "anchors": "- Root: نطف (n-ṭ-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Pure, clear water; a drop of fluid; the seminal fluid; also pearl.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النطفة: الماء الصافي\" — the sperm-drop as pure water, used Quranically for the seminal fluid; a technical term for the initial stage of human embryological creation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"الماء الصافي قل أو كثر\" — clear water, little or much. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"ليلة نطوف: مطرت حتى الصباح\" — a night of continuous rain, extending the root's sense of flowing/pouring.\n- Root: خلق (kh-l-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Measuring, estimating, fashioning precisely; creating from nothing or from a prior substance.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخلق أصله: التقدير المستقيم\" — origination upon a straight measure; used for both creation ex nihilo (إبداع) and formation from an existing thing (إيجاد من الشيء).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قدره قبل القطع\" — measuring before cutting (craftsman's act). Metaphorical: \"خلق الله الخلق: أوجده على تقدير أوجبته الحكمة\" — God's creation as bringing into existence upon a measure necessitated by wisdom.\n- Root: كيف (kayf)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: An interrogative particle asking about state or condition; also used for manner.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"كيف: لفظ يسأل به عما يصح أن يقال فيه: شبيه وغير شبيه\" — a word asking about what admits of likeness or unlikeness (qualities); not applicable to God's essence, but used here to provoke reflection on creaturely processes.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Interrogative of modality; in this context, a rhetorical summons to contemplate the \"how\" of divine craftsmanship.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The periodic structure here repeatedly employs \"ثم كيف\" (then how) as a coordinating interrogative chain, linking successive clauses without ellipsis, preserving the full syntactic weight of each stage of creation.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Contemplation'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "سبعة أطوار وتلا الآية الواردة في أطوار الخلقة وهي قوله تعالى ولقد خلقنا الإنسان من سلالة من طين ثم جعلناه نطفة في قرار مكين إلى قوله ثم أنشأناه خلقا آخر أي نفخنا فيه الروح ثم تلا قوله تعالى في الآية وإذا الموءودة سئلت وإذا نظرت إلى ما قدمناه في طريق القياس والاعتبار ظهر لك تفاوت منصب علي وابن عباس رضي الله عنهما في الغوص على المعاني ودرك العلوم كيف وفي المتفق عليه في الصحيحين على جابر أنه قال كنا نعزل على عهد رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم والقرآن ينزل وفي لفظ آخر كنا نعزل فبلغ ذلك نبي الله صلى الله عليه وسلم فلم ينهنا (1) وفيه أيضا عن جابر أنه قال إن رجلا أتى رسول الله صل", "english": "**The third:** Fear of excessive hardship (ḥaraj) due to the multitude of children, and guarding against the need for toil in earning a livelihood and entering into evil means of acquisition. This also is not prohibited, for the paucity of hardship assists one in religion. Indeed, perfection and excellence lie in trust in God (tawakkul) and confidence in God's guarantee, where He said: {«وما من دابة في الأرض إلا على الله رزقها»} \"And there is no creature on earth but that upon Allah is its provision\" [Hūd: 6]. Without doubt, in this [trust] there is a falling from the summit of perfection and an abandonment of what is more excellent — but looking to consequences and preserving wealth and storing it up, despite its contradicting tawakkul, we do not say it is prohibited.", "book_title": "Etiquette of Marriage", "section_title_ar": "Section 307", "section_title_en": "** On Coitus Interruptus ('Azl), Its Intentions, and the Etiquettes of Childbirth and Divorce", "section_index": 307, "anchors": "**\n- Root: عزل (ʿazl)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Separation, removal, setting aside\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Withdrawal to prevent conception\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical separation; Metaphorical: preventing pregnancy\n- Root: وأد (waʾad)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Burying a female infant alive (pre-Islamic practice)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The killing of daughters by burial\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: burial alive; Metaphorical: any prevention of life\n- Root: طلق (ṭalaq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Divorce; dissolution of marriage bond\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Legal dissolution of the marriage contract\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: releasing/loosing; Metaphorical: dissolution of marriage\n- Sibawayh Rule: Conditional clauses with \"فإن قلت\" (objection-response dialectic structure)\n\n---\n\n**", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Etiquette of Marriage'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "التراب وأخرج ذريته من سلالة من طين ومن ماء دافق فأخرجهم من الأصلاب إلى الأرحام ومنها إلى الدنيا ثم إلى القبر ثم إلى العرض ثم إلى الجنة أو إلى النار فهذا مبدؤهم وهذا غايتهم وهذه منازلهم وخلق الدنيا زادا للمعاد ليتناول منها ما يصلح للتزود فلو تناولوها بالعدل لانقطعت الخصومات وتعطل الفقهاء ولكنهم تناولوها بالشهوات فتولدت منها الخصومات فمست الحاجة إلى سلطان يسوسهم واحتاج السلطان إلى قانون يسوسهم به فالفقيه هو العالم بقانون السياسة وطريق التوسط بين الخلق إذا تنازعوا بحكم الشهوات فكان", "english": "Know that the obligatory (al-farḍ) is not distinguished from other things except by mentioning the divisions of the sciences. The sciences, in relation to the purpose with which we are concerned, divide into religious (sharʿiyyah) and non-religious. By \"religious\" I mean that which is derived from the prophets — may the blessings of God be upon them and His peace — and to which the intellect does not guide, such as arithmetic (al-ḥisāb); nor does experience guide to it, such as medicine (al-ṭibb); nor does hearing/linguistic convention guide to it, such as language (al-lughah). For the sciences that are not religious divide into: that which is praiseworthy (maḥmūd), that which is blameworthy (madhmūm), and that which is permissible (mubāḥ).", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 13", "section_title_en": "Section 13 — The Distinction of Sciences: Religious and Non-Religious, Their Divisions and Rulings", "section_index": 13, "anchors": "- Root: علم (ʿalima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning: \"knowledge, perception, cognition of a thing by its true reality.\" Al-Farahidi: \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" (knowledge is the apprehension of a thing by its true reality).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — knowledge is of two kinds: (1) apprehension of the essence of a thing, and (2) judgment upon a thing by the existence or negation of something pertaining to it. This is the theological (Kalam) sense: knowledge as veridical cognition of the real.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqiqah): \"علمت الشيء: عرفته\" — to know a thing is to recognize it. Metaphorical (majaz): \"ما علمت بخبرك: ما شعرت به\" — \"I did not know of your news\" means \"I had no awareness of it,\" indicating that knowledge can be used for latent perception.\n- Root: شرع (sharaʿa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Al-Farahidi: \"الشرع: نهج الطريق الواضح\" — the clear, open path. The root denotes making a path clear and accessible.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الشرع: نهج الطريق الواضح... ثم استعير ذلك للطريقة الإلهية\" — the term is metaphorically transferred to the Divine Law (al-sharīʿah), the clear path laid down by God through His prophets.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"شرع في الماء شروعا\" — to enter into water (immersion). Metaphorical: the religious law is that which one enters into and follows as a clear way, like entering a watering-place (al-mashraʿ).\n- Root: دنا (danā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Al-Farahidi: \"دنا: قرب\" — to be near, to approach. The root denotes proximity in place, time, or rank.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الدنو: القرب بالذات، أو بالحكم\" — nearness by essence or by judgment. Used for spatial, temporal, and station proximity. The \"nearer\" (al-adnā) can mean the lesser or the meaner, as opposed to the greater or the better.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"دنا منه: قرب\" — to draw near. The root is distinct from \"danu'a\" (دناءة) meaning baseness or lowness, though the two are sometimes confused by the masses.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the permissibility of interposing elements between a governing word and its governed prepositional phrase, particularly when the interposed element is essential to the completion of meaning. This applies to the periodic sentence structure of the text, where long conditional and relative clauses are interposed between the subject (al-farḍ) and its predicate (lā yamtāzu).", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "14": [{"arabic": "أسلمت سجد وجهي للذي خلقه وصوره وشق سمعه وبصره فتبارك الله أحسن الخالقين اللهم سجد لك سوادي وخيالي وآمن بك فؤادي أبوء بنعمتك علي وأبوء بذنبي وهذا ما جنيت على نفسي فاغفر لي فإنه لا يغفر الذنوب إلا أنت (5) أو تقول سبحان ربي الأعلى ثلاث مرات (6) فإذا فرغت من الصلاة فقل اللهم أنت السلام ومنك السلام تباركت يا ذا الجلال والإكرام (7) وتدعو بسائر الأدعية التي ذكرناها فإذا قمت من المجلس وأردت دعاء يكفر لغو المجلس فقل سبحانك اللهم وبحمدك أشهد أن لا إله إلا أنت أستغفرك وأتوب إليك عملت سوءا وظلمت نفسي فاغفر لي فإنه لا يغفر الذنوب إل", "english": "When you rise in the morning and hear the call to prayer (adhān), it is recommended for you to respond to the caller of prayer (mu'adhdhin) — and we have already mentioned this, along with the supplications for entering and leaving the privy, and the supplications of ablution (wuḍū'), in the Book of Purification. When you set out toward the mosque, say: \"O Allah, place light (nūr) in my heart, and light in my tongue, and place light in my hearing, and place light in my sight, and place light behind me, and light before me, and place light above me. O Allah, grant me light.\" And also say: \"O Allah, I ask You by the right of those who ask of You, and by the right of this my walking unto You — for I have not gone out in insolence, nor in vanity, nor for show, nor for reputation. I have gone out seeking protection from Your wrath and seeking Your good pleasure. So I ask You to deliver me from the Fire and to forgive me my sins — verily, none forgives sins but You.\"", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "section_title_ar": "Section 254", "section_title_en": "Section 254 — Supplications for the Hours of Day and Night: From Dawn to Sleep", "section_index": 254, "anchors": "- Root: ن و ر (n-w-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: النور: الضوء المنتشر الذي يُبصر به، وهو ضد الظلمة\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): النور في القرآن يقع على ضروب: منها المعقول كالإيمان والحكمة، قال تعالى: {قد جاءكم من الله نور وكتاب مبين}، ومنها المحسوس كضوء الشمس والقمر\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): النور حقيقة في الضوء المحسوس، ومجاز في الهدى والإيمان والعلم — فدعاء النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم بطلب النور يشمل المعنيين جميعاً\n- Root: س ج د (s-j-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: السجود: وضع الجبهة على الأرض تعظيماً، وأصله التطامن والانخفاض\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): السجود ضربان: سجود عبادة وهو وضع الجبهة على الأرض تقرباً، وسجود طاعة وهو الانقياد والخضوع — قال تعالى: {ولله يسجد من في السموات والأرض طوعاً وكرهاً}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): سجد حقيقة في وضع الجبهة، ومجاز في الخضوع والانقياد — وقوله \"سجد لك سوادي وخيالي\" من المجاز المرسل\n- Root: ذ ك ر (dh-k-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: الذكر: نقيض النسيان، والذكر: الصوت الذي يُذكر به الشيء، والذكر: الشرف والصيت\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الذكر ضربان: ذكر بالقلب وهو التفكر في عظمة الله، وذكر باللسان وهو التلفظ بأسمائه — وأعلى مراتبه ما اجتمع فيه القلب واللسان\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): الذكر حقيقة في استحضار الشيء بالقلب أو اللسان، ومجاز في الصلاة والدعاء والثناء\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — جواز تقديم المعمول وتأخيره مع بقاء العامل عاملاً في المعنى، كقوله \"لك الحمد\" حيث تقدم الجار والمجرور على المبتدأ للاختصاص والاهتمام", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'", "badge": "Du'a"}, {"arabic": "الإنسان من سلالة من طين ثم جعلناه نطفة في قرار مكين إلى قوله ثم أنشأناه خلقا آخر أي نفخنا فيه الروح ثم تلا قوله تعالى في الآية وإذا الموءودة سئلت وإذا نظرت إلى ما قدمناه في طريق القياس والاعتبار ظهر لك تفاوت منصب علي وابن عباس رضي الله عنهما في الغوص على المعاني ودرك العلوم كيف وفي المتفق عليه في الصحيحين على جابر أنه قال كنا نعزل على عهد رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم والقرآن ينزل وفي لفظ آخر كنا نعزل فبلغ ذلك نبي الله صلى الله عليه وسلم فلم ينهنا (1) وفيه أيضا عن جابر أنه قال إن رجلا أتى رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم فقال إن لي", "english": "**The third:** Fear of excessive hardship (ḥaraj) due to the multitude of children, and guarding against the need for toil in earning a livelihood and entering into evil means of acquisition. This also is not prohibited, for the paucity of hardship assists one in religion. Indeed, perfection and excellence lie in trust in God (tawakkul) and confidence in God's guarantee, where He said: {«وما من دابة في الأرض إلا على الله رزقها»} \"And there is no creature on earth but that upon Allah is its provision\" [Hūd: 6]. Without doubt, in this [trust] there is a falling from the summit of perfection and an abandonment of what is more excellent — but looking to consequences and preserving wealth and storing it up, despite its contradicting tawakkul, we do not say it is prohibited.", "book_title": "Etiquette of Marriage", "section_title_ar": "Section 307", "section_title_en": "** On Coitus Interruptus ('Azl), Its Intentions, and the Etiquettes of Childbirth and Divorce", "section_index": 307, "anchors": "**\n- Root: عزل (ʿazl)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Separation, removal, setting aside\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Withdrawal to prevent conception\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical separation; Metaphorical: preventing pregnancy\n- Root: وأد (waʾad)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Burying a female infant alive (pre-Islamic practice)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The killing of daughters by burial\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: burial alive; Metaphorical: any prevention of life\n- Root: طلق (ṭalaq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Divorce; dissolution of marriage bond\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Legal dissolution of the marriage contract\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: releasing/loosing; Metaphorical: dissolution of marriage\n- Sibawayh Rule: Conditional clauses with \"فإن قلت\" (objection-response dialectic structure)\n\n---\n\n**", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Etiquette of Marriage'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "23": [{"arabic": "إلى قومه فقال يا قوم أسلموا فإن محمدا يعطي عطاء من لا يخاف الفاقة (1) وقال ابن عمر قال صلى الله عليه وسلم إن لله عبادا يخصهم بالنعم لمنافع العباد فمن بخل بتلك المنافع على العباد نقلها الله تعالى عنه وحولها إلى غيره (2) حديث الهلالي أتى النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم بأسرى من بني العنبر فأمر بقتلهم وأفرد منهم رجلا الحديث وفيه فإن الله شكر له سخاء فيه لم أجد له أصلا (3) حديث إن لكل شيء ثمرة وثمرة المعروف تعجيل السراح لم أقف له على أصل (4) حديث نافع عن ابن عمر طعام الجواد دوا", "english": "I say: Know that the Prophet (peace be upon him) was asked by a man, and he gave him and bestowed upon him. Then another man came and asked him, and he ordered that he be given abundant sheep filling the space between two mountains, from the sheep of the alms-collection. The man returned to his people and said: \"O my people, embrace Islam, for Muhammad gives the gift of one who does not fear poverty.\" (1)", "book_title": "Condemnation of Miserliness and Love of Wealth", "section_title_ar": "Section 764", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 764: On Generosity and the Bounty of God", "section_index": 764, "anchors": "- Root: ع ط و (ʿ-ṭ-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of giving, bestowing, and extending; al-ʿaṭāʾ is the gift itself, and the verb ʿaṭā signifies the act of reaching out with something to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ʿaṭāʾ in the divine context is the emanation of bounty (al-jūd) from the Creator to creation, unconstrained by need or requital; it is an act of pure beneficence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): physical extension of the hand in giving. Metaphorical (majāz): the bestowal of favor, rank, or mercy, as in \"the king gave to the petitioner.\"\n- Root: س خ و (s-kh-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of liberality and open-handedness; al-sakhāʾ is the natural disposition of giving freely without reluctance.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-sakhāʾ is the virtue opposed to al-bukhl (miserliness); it is the soul's readiness to part with what it possesses for the sake of another, mirroring divine generosity.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: spending wealth readily. Metaphorical: the heart's expansiveness and freedom from attachment to possessions.\n- Root: ب خ ل (b-kh-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of withholding and hoarding; al-bukhl is the restraint of what one owns from those who have a claim upon it.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-bukhl is of two kinds—withholding one's own goods, and withholding what belongs to others (e.g., knowledge or authority); the latter is more blameworthy, as in {«Who are miserly and command people to miserliness»} (4:37).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: refusal to give wealth. Metaphorical: the sky \"withholding\" rain, as in the verse of Abū Najm: \"when the sky is miserly with its rain.\"\n- Root: ن ع م (n-ʿ-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of ease, well-being, and bounty; al-niʿmah is the state of being in a favorable condition, and al-naʿīm is the abundance of such states.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-niʿmah is the goodly state (al-ḥālah al-ḥasanah) bestowed by God; it is a genus term covering both the little and the much, as in {«If you count the blessings of God, you cannot number them»} (16:18).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: pleasant living and comfort. Metaphorical: \"their ostriches have departed\" (khafat naʿāmatuhum)—i.e., their prosperity has vanished, using the ostrich as a symbol of swift departure.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Prepositional Interposition (bāb al-faṣl bayna al-jār wa-al-majrūr) — The permissibility of separating the preposition from its object for rhetorical emphasis, as in the prophetic idiom \"giving the gift of one who does not fear poverty,\" where the syntactic structure underscores the absolute nature of the act.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Condemnation of Miserliness and Love of Wealth'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "27": [{"arabic": "وفاحشا فقد روي أن نوحا عليه السلام لما ركب السفينة حمل فيها من كل زوجين اثنين كما أمره الله تعالى فرأى في السفينة شيخا لم يعرفه فقال له نوح ما أدخلك فقال دخلت لأصيب قلوب أصحابك فتكون قلوبهم معي وأبدانهم معك فقال له نوح أخرج منها يا عدو الله فإنك لعين فقال له إبليس خمس أهلك بهن الناس وسأحدثك منهم بثلاث ولا أحدثك باثنتين فأوحى الله تعالى إلى نوح أنه لا حاجة لك بالثلاث فليحدثك بالاثنين فقال له نوح", "english": "Among his great gates are anger (ghaḍab) and lustful desire (shahwah). For anger is the ghoul of the intellect — when the soldier of the intellect weakens, the soldier of Satan attacks. Whenever a man becomes angry, Satan plays with him as a boy plays with a ball. It has been narrated that Moses, peace be upon him, was met by Iblis, who said to him: \"O Moses, you are the one whom God chose for His message and to whom He spoke directly (taklīman). I am a creature among God's creatures. I sinned, and I wish to repent. So intercede for me to my Lord that He might accept my repentance.\" Moses said: \"Yes.\" When Moses ascended the mountain and spoke with his Lord, Mighty and Majestic, and wished to descend, his Lord said to him: \"Deliver the trust.\" Moses said: \"O Lord, Your servant Iblis wishes that You accept his repentance.\" So God, exalted be He, revealed to Moses: \"O Moses, I have already granted your request — that he prostrate before the grave of Adam so that his repentance might be accepted.\" Moses then met Iblis and said to him: \"I have secured your request — you have been commanded to prostrate before the grave of Adam so that your repentance might be accepted.\" But he became angry and arrogant, saying: \"I did not prostrate to him while he was alive — shall I prostrate to him dead?\" Then he said to him: \"O Moses, you have a right upon me because you interceded for me to your Lord. So remember me at three occasions, and I shall not destroy you in them: Remember me when you become angry, for my spirit is in your heart and my eye is in your eye, and I flow through you like the flow of blood. Remember me when you become angry — for when a man becomes angry, I blow into his nostrils, and he does not know what he does. And remember me when you encounter the battle-line (zaḥf), for I come to the son of Adam when he encounters the battle-line and remind him of his wife, his children, and his family, until he turns and flees. And beware of sitting with a woman who is not a close relative (dhāt maḥram), for I am her messenger to you and your messenger to her — I shall not cease until I tempt you with her and tempt her with you.\"", "book_title": "Disciplining the Soul", "section_title_ar": "Section 600", "section_title_en": "The Detailed Exposition of the Entrances of Satan into the Heart", "section_index": 600, "anchors": "- Root: شيط (Shayṭ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning relates to burning, scorching, or being consumed by fire — that which is burnt and spoiled.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الشيطان refers to the rebellious one among jinn and mankind; the term denotes one who has deviated from truth through pride and disobedience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"شاط اللحم\" — meat that is burnt and spoiled. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"شاط دمه\" — his blood was spilled in vain; \"أشاط السلطان دمه\" — the ruler declared his blood lawful to shed. Thus the Shayṭān is so named because he is consumed by fire, or because he causes others to be consumed/destroyed.\n\n- Root: غضب (Ghaḍab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The boiling and agitation of the blood of the heart, seeking vengeance.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الغضب: ثوران دم القلب إرادة الانتقام\" — anger is the surging of the blood of the heart desiring vengeance. When attributed to God, it means only retribution and punishment, not an emotional state.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: anger as a human emotional state. Metaphorical: \"غضبت له قوائم عوج\" — even inanimate things are described metaphorically. The Prophet said: \"اتقوا الغضب فإنه جمرة توقد في قلب ابن آدم\" — anger is a burning ember kindled in the heart of the son of Adam.\n\n- Root: قلب (Qalb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The heart; also the turning and revolving of a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the turning of a thing and its conversion from one aspect to another. The heart is so named because of its constant turning and fluctuation of states.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to turn something over, to flip it. Metaphorical: \"قلب المعلم الصبيان\" — the teacher dismissed the boys (turned them back to their homes); \"رجل قلب حول\" — a man who turns matters over and devises stratagems. The heart is the seat of understanding and discernment, and its protection is the fortress against Satan.\n\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of interposing elements between a preposition and its object when the meaning remains clear, analogous to how the heart's defenses may be interposed between Satan and his objectives.\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Disciplining the Soul'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "34": [{"arabic": "أنتم إلا بشر مثلنا وقالوا أنؤمن لبشرين مثلنا ولئن أطعتم بشرا مثلكم إنكم إذا لخاسرون فتعجبوا من أن يفوز برتبة الرسالة والوحي والقرب من الله تعالى بشر مثلهم فحسدوهم وأحبوا زوال النبوة عنهم جزعا أن يفضل عليهم من هو مثلهم في الخلقة لا عن قصد تكبر وطلب رياسة وتقدم عداوة أو سبب آخر من سائر الأسباب وقالوا متعجبين أبعث الله بشرا رسولا {وقالوا} لولا أنزل علينا الملائكة وقال تعالى أو عجبتم أن جاءكم ذكر من ربكم على رجل منكم الآية السبب الخامس الخوف من فوت المقاصد وذلك يختص بمتزاحمين على مقصود واحد فإن كان واحد يحسد صاحبه في كل نعمة تكون عونا", "english": "I say: God is Knower of what is in the breasts. {«إِنْ تَمْسَسْكُمْ حَسَنَةٌ تَسُؤْهُمْ»} — \"If good touches you, it grieves them\" {«وَدُّوا مَا عَنِتُّمْ قَدْ بَدَتِ الْبَغْضَاءُ مِنْ أَفْوَاهِهِمْ وَمَا تُخْفِي صُدُورُهُمْ أَكْبَرُ»} — \"They wish for your hardship; hatred has already appeared from their mouths, and what their breasts conceal is greater.\" And envy (ḥasad), arising from hatred, may lead to contention, mutual fighting, and the spending of one's entire life in removing another's blessing through stratagems, slander, the tearing of veils, and whatever runs in that course.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Envy", "section_title_ar": "Section 722", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Book on the Rebuke of the Self: The Seven Causes of Envy (Section 722)", "section_index": 722, "anchors": "- Root: سبب (sabab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The rope by which one climbs; every means by which one reaches a thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Every means by which one arrives at something is called a sabab\" — thus the causes enumerated here are the psychological \"ropes\" by which envy ascends in the soul.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a physical cord. Metaphorical: any linkage or occasion — here, the inward occasions (asbāb) that give rise to ḥasad.\n- Root: بغض (bughḍ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Hatred, aversion of the heart.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The opposite of love (maḥabbah); a disposition of the heart toward another's harm.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: inner aversion. Metaphorical: its \"appearance from the mouths\" (Quranic) as outward speech betraying the concealed hatred.\n- Root: كبر (kibr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Greatness; pride; disdain.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-kabīr and al-ṣaghīr are relative terms\" — pride is the claim to greatness relative to another, deeming oneself above him.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: bigness in stature or age. Metaphorical: \"takabbara 'alayhi\" — to deem oneself greater than another and refuse submission to him.\n- Root: قصد (qaṣd)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Straightness of path; intending a goal.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-qaṣd: istiqāmat al-ṭarīq\" — the straight road; also moderation between extremes.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to head toward a place. Metaphorical: \"maqāṣid\" — the aims and objectives toward which one directs oneself; here, the worldly goals whose pursuit breeds rivalry and envy.\n- Root: نزل (nazal)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To descend from a height; to alight.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-nuzūl fī al-aṣl huwa inḥiṭāṭ min 'uluww\" — descent from above; the sending down of revelation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: descending from a high place. Metaphorical: \"anzala Allāhu al-kitāb\" — God's sending down of the Quran; and \"mā unzila\" — what was revealed, as in the Quranic citation regarding the Qurashites' objection to revelation upon a mere human.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Causal Coordination (al-'aṭf al-sababī) — The enumeration of the seven causes proceeds by coordinated clauses (fa-... thumma ...), each introduced as an independent proposition linked by causation (sabab), requiring the translator to preserve the logical sequence of psychological motivations without collapsing them into one another.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Condemnation of Envy'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "38": [{"arabic": "الله تعالى بين من افترى على الله كذبا وبين من كذب بالحق فقال تعالى ومن أظلم ممن افترى على الله كذبا أو كذب بالحق لما جاءه وقال تعالى فمن أظلم ممن كذب على الله وكذب بالصدق إذ جاءه ومنها الرياء وملاحظة الخلق والجهد في استمالة قلوبهم وصرف وجوههم والرياء هو الداء العضال الذي يدعو إلى أكبر الكبائر كما سيأتي في كتاب الرياء والمناظر لا يقصد إلا الظهور عند الخلق وانطلاق ألسنتهم بالثناء عليه فهذه عشر خصال من أمهات الفواحش الباطنة سوى ما يتفق لغير المتماسكين منهم من الخصام المؤدي إلى الضرب", "english": "**QUAD_ANCHORS:**\n- Root: نظر (naẓara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to look, to examine, to consider; al-munāẓarah is mutual examination/argumentation between two parties.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: naẓar as intellectual contemplation (taʾammul bi-l-baṣīrah); munāẓarah as dialectical exchange.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sitting face-to-face in debate; Metaphorical: disputation for victory.\n- Root: غلب (ghalaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to overcome, to conquer, to prevail.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghalabah as seeking to dominate another in argument.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: victory in combat; Metaphorical: intellectual supremacy.\n- Root: حسد (ḥasada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to envy, to wish removal of blessings from another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: envy as wishing deprivation of blessings from the deserving — among the basest traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: envy of another's blessing; Metaphorical: consuming fire of the soul.\n- Root: كبر (kabura)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be great, to be haughty.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-kibr as self-elevation above others; condemned by God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: greatness; Metaphorical: arrogance and pride.\n- Root: غيب (ghāba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be absent, to be hidden.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghībah as mentioning another with what he dislikes in his absence — likened to eating carrion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: backbiting; Metaphorical: consuming the flesh of one's brother.\n- Root: راءى (rāʾā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to show off, to display.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-riyāʾ as performing acts for the sake of being seen — hidden shirk.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: showing good deeds; Metaphorical: seeking reputation.\n- Root: نفس (nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: self, soul, spirit.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: tazkiyat al-nafs as self-praise and self-purification — forbidden by God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: self; Metaphorical: self-commendation.\n- Root: حقd (ḥaqida)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to bear malice, to harbor resentment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ḥiqd as nursing enmity in the heart — negated from the believer.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: bearing grudge; Metaphorical: hidden rancor.\n- Root: نفاق (nifāq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: hypocrisy, showing contrary to what one conceals.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: hypocrisy in deeds — outward display contrary to inward state.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: hypocrisy in faith; Metaphorical: hypocrisy in character.\n- Root: علم (ʿalima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to know, to perceive.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: knowledge as apprehension of reality — its pursuit for other than God is destructive.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: knowledge; Metaphorical: the dangerous burden of the scholar.\n- Root: جدل (jadala)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to argue, to dispute.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-jidāl as contention; al-mirāʾ as disputation with falsehood — condemned.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: argumentation; Metaphorical: obstinate disputation.\n- Root: شرك (sharaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to associate, to share.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-riyāʾ as hidden shirk — associating others with God in intention.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: polytheism; Metaphorical: hidden association in deeds.\n- Root: موت (māta)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to die.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: death as separation of soul from body; spiritual death as ruin of the soul.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical death; Metaphorical: spiritual destruction.\n- Root: حيى (ḥayiya)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to live.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: life as the power of perception; eternal life as the reward of the righteous.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical life; Metaphorical: spiritual vitality.\n- Root: هلك (halaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to perish, to be destroyed.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-halāk as ruin; eternal destruction as everlasting punishment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: destruction; Metaphorical: eternal ruin.\n- Root: رياء (riyāʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: showing off, ostentation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: riyāʾ as the incurable disease calling to the greatest sins.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: ostentation; Metaphorical: spiritual disease.\n- Root: جاه (jāh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: rank, status, prestige.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: love of status (ḥubb al-jāh) among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: position; Metaphorical: seeking prestige.\n- Root: مباهاة (mubāhāh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: mutual boasting, vying in splendor.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: mubāhāh as each seeking to display superiority over the other.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: mutual boasting; Metaphorical: rivalry in honor.\n- Root: مماراة (mumārāh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: disputation, wrangling.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-mirāʾ as contentious argument — condemned by the Prophet.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: disputation; Metaphorical: obstinate wrangling.\n- Root: فخر (fakhara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to boast, to pride oneself.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-fakhr as self-glorification — among the blameworthy traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: boasting; Metaphorical: vainglory.\n- Root: عجب (ʿajiba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to wonder, to be amazed.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ʿujb as self-admiration — among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder; Metaphorical: self-conceit.\n- Root: بغض (baghiḍa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to hate, to detest.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-baghdāʾ as mutual hatred — contrary to brotherhood.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: hatred; Metaphorical: mutual enmity.\n- Root: غضب (ghaḍiba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be angry.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghaḍab as anger — among the blameworthy traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: anger; Metaphorical: rage.\n- Root: طمع (ṭamiʿa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to covet, to desire.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ṭamaʿ as covetousness — among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: desire; Metaphorical: greed.\n- Root: أشر (ashira)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be exultant, to be insolent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ashar as excessive joy and insolence — blameworthy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: exultation; Metaphorical: insolence.\n- Root: بطر (baṭira)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be arrogant, to be ungrateful.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-baṭar as arrogance in prosperity — among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: arrogance; Metaphorical: ingratitude for blessings.\n- Root: خيلاء (khuyalāʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: pride, haughtiness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-khuyalāʾ as pride and conceit — blameworthy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: pride; Metaphorical: haughty bearing.\n- Root: خشية (khashyah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: fear, awe.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-khashyah as reverential fear — among the traits of the believers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: fear; Metaphorical: reverential awe.\n- Root: رحمة (raḥmah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: mercy, compassion.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-raḥmah as tenderness of heart — among the traits of the believers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: mercy; Metaphorical: compassion.\n- Root: غفلة (ghaflah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: heedlessness, inattention.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghaflah as heedlessness — contrary to vigilance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: inattention; Metaphorical: spiritual heedlessness.\n- Root: خشوع (khushūʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: humility, submissiveness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-khushūʿ as humility — among the traits of the believers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: humility; Metaphorical: devotional submission.\n- Root: تذكير (tadhkīr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: reminding, admonishing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-tadhkīr as reminding of God — a righteous act.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: reminding; Metaphorical: preaching.\n- Root: وعظ (waʿaẓa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to admonish, to exhort.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-waʿẓ as exhortation to obedience and warning against disobedience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: admonition; Metaphorical: sermonizing.\n- Root: فتوى (fatwā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: legal ruling, formal opinion.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-fatwā as clarification of legal rulings.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: legal opinion; Metaphorical: religious authority.\n- Root: قضاء (qaḍāʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: judgment, decree.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-qaḍāʾ as judgment between people.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: judgment; Metaphorical: judicial authority.\n- Root: وقف (waqafa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to stop, to stand.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-waqf as endowment of property for charitable purposes.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: standing; Metaphorical: religious endowment.\n- Root: ثواب (thawāb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: reward, recompense.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-thawāb as divine recompense for righteous deeds.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: reward; Metaphorical: divine recompense.\n- Root: عذاب (ʿadhāb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: punishment, torment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ʿadhāb as divine punishment for the disobedient.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: punishment; Metaphorical: divine chastisement.\n- Root: ملك (malaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to possess, to rule.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-mulk as sovereignty; al-mulk al-muʾabbad as eternal sovereignty in Paradise.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: dominion; Metaphorical: eternal kingdom.\n- Root: نعيم (naʿīm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: bliss, delight.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-naʿīm as bliss; al-naʿīm al-sarmad as everlasting bliss.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: delight; Metaphorical: eternal bliss.\n- Root: رخصة (rukhaṣah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: concession, dispensation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-rukhaṣah as legal concession.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: concession; Metaphorical: legal dispensation.\n- Root: رياسة (riyāsah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: leadership, headship.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: love of leadership among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: leadership; Metaphorical: seeking authority.\n- Root: اندراس (indirās)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: effacement, obliteration.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: indirās al-ʿulūm as the obliteration of the sciences.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: effacement; Metaphorical: extinction of knowledge.\n- Root: صبيان (ṣibyān)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: boys, children.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: children's motivation for learning through play.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: boys; Metaphorical: pupils.\n- Root: مكتب (maktab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn- Root: مكتب (maktab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: place of instruction, school for boys.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: the elementary school where children are taught.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: school; Metaphorical: place of elementary learning.\n- Root: خلاق (khilāq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: portion, share, lot.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: \"no portion\" (lā khilāq) in the Hereafter — utter deprivation of divine reward.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: share; Metaphorical: spiritual allotment in the afterlife.\n- Root: فاجر (fājir)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: wicked, immoral, profligate.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: the dissolute one who commits sins openly.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sinner; Metaphorical: one through whom God nonetheless supports religion.\n- Root: شمع (shamʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: candle, wax taper.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: the candle as parable — consuming itself while giving light to others.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: candle; Metaphorical: one who destroys himself but benefits others.\n- Root: نار (nār)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: fire.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: fire as parable — consuming itself and all around it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: fire; Metaphorical: one who destroys himself and others.\n- Root: سعد (saʿida)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be fortunate, to prosper.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: saʿādah as felicity — success in the Hereafter.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: good fortune; Metaphorical: eternal felicity.\n- Root: شقي (shaqiya)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be wretched, to be miserable.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: shaqāwah as wretchedness — ruin in the Hereafter.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: misery; Metaphorical: eternal ruin.\n- Root: أخلص (akhlaṣa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to purify, to be sincere.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: ikhlāṣ as purifying intention for God alone — the condition of acceptance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sincerity; Metaphorical: pure devotion.\n- Root: ريب (rāba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to doubt, to suspect.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-rayb as doubt and uncertainty — to be dispelled by evidence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: doubt; Metaphorical: skepticism.", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 39", "section_title_en": "Section 39", "section_index": 39, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "43": [{"arabic": "الباعث في حقه ويبقى ما سبق من أمر الولد فإن ذلك عام إلا للممسوح وهو نادر ومن الطباع ما تغلب عليها الشهوة بحيث لا تحصنه المرأة الواحدة فيستحب لصاحبها الزيادة على الواحدة إلى الأربع فإن يسر الله له مودة ورحمة واطمأن قلبه بهن وإلا فيستحب له الاستبدال فقد نكح علي رضي الله عنه بعد وفاة فاطمة عليها السلام بسبع ليال ويقال إن الحسن بن علي كان منكاحا حتى نكح زيادة على مائتي امرأة وكان ربما عقد على أربع في وقت واحد وربما طلق أربعا في وقت واحد واستبدل بهن وقد قال صلى الله عليه وسلم للح", "english": "وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم: \"لا يلقى الله أحد بذنب أعظم من جهالة أهله.\"", "book_title": "Etiquette of Marriage", "section_title_ar": "Section 296", "section_title_en": "Section 296", "section_index": 296, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Etiquette of Marriage'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "51": [{"arabic": "الأكل من الدين وعليه نبه رب العالمين بقوله وهو أصدق القائلين كلوا من الطيبات واعملوا صالحا فمن يقدم على الأكل ليستعين به على العلم والعمل ويقوي به على التقوى فلا ينبغي أن يترك نفسه مهملا سدى يسترسل في الأكل استرسال البهائم في المرعى فإن ما هو ذريعة إلى الدين ووسيلة إليه ينبغي أن تظهر أنوار الدين عليه وإنما أنوار الدين آدابه وسننه التي يزم العبد بزمامها ويلجم المتقي بلجامها حتى يتزن بميزان الشرع شهوة الطعام في إقدامها وإحجامها فيصير بسببها مدفعة للوزر ومجلبة للأجر وإن كان فيها أوفى حظ للنفس قال صلى الله عليه وسلم إن الرجل ليؤجر حتى في ا", "english": "To proceed: The aspiration of those possessed of intellects (ulū al-albāb) is the meeting with Allah, the Exalted, in the Abode of Recompense. And there is no path to reaching the meeting with Allah except through knowledge (ʿilm) and action (ʿamal). Nor is perseverance upon both of them possible except through the soundness of the body; nor is the soundness of the body unclouded except through foods and provisions, partaking of them according to the measure of need, repeated over the succession of times. It is from this aspect that some of the righteous predecessors (al-salaf al-ṣāliḥīn) said: \"Eating is part of the religion.\" Upon this the Lord of the Worlds alerted us by His saying — and He is the most truthful of speakers: {«كلوا من الطيبات واعملوا صالحا»} (Eat of the wholesome things and work righteousness) {«المؤمنون/51»}. So whoever undertakes eating in order to seek aid thereby upon knowledge and action, and to strengthen himself thereby upon godfearingness (taqwā), then he ought not to leave his soul neglected and aimless, letting himself loose in eating as cattle loose themselves in the pasture. For that which is a means to the religion and a pathway unto it — it is fitting that the lights of the religion shine upon it.", "book_title": "Manners of Eating", "section_title_ar": "Section 281", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Etiquettes of Eating — The First Book of the Quarter of Customs from The Revival of the Religious Sciences", "section_index": 281, "anchors": "- Root: اكل (akl)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of eating, consuming, and taking nourishment; the act of ingesting food.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأكل: تناول المطعم\" — eating is the taking of nourishment; used metaphorically for fire consuming wood, and for the daily sustenance (rizq) provided by Allah.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqiqah) refers to physical ingestion; metaphorical (majaz) extends to consuming wealth, property, or even the effects of time upon objects.\n- Root: دين (din)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of debt, obligation, and recompense; the structured way of obedience owed to Allah.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"دنت الرجل: أخذت منه دينا\" — the root carries the sense of indebtedness and reciprocal obligation, framing religion as the binding covenant of servitude.\n- Root: ربع (rubʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of four, a quarter, and a dwelling place.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أربعة، وأربعون، وربع، ورباع كلها من أصل واحد\" — all derived from one origin; the quarter (rubʿ) as a structural division.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"ربع بالمكان: أقام به\" — to dwell in a place; metaphorical: \"اللهم اربعني من دين علي أي انعشني\" — to raise up, to revive, as the quarter of the book revives the religion.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the exclusive framing of purpose statements, as in \"إنما أنوار الدين آدابه\" (the lights of religion are only its etiquettes).", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Manners of Eating'", "badge": "Insight"}], "53": [{"arabic": "وبذلك هلكت الأمم السالفة إذ افترقت فرقا فكل معجب برأيه وكل حزب بما لديهم فرحون وجميع أهل البدع والضلال إنما أصروا عليها لعجبهم بآرائهم والعجب بالبدعة هو استحسان ما يسوق إليه الهوى والشهوة مع ظن كونه حقا وعلاج هذا العجب أشد من علاج غيره لأن صاحب الرأي الخطأ جاهل بخطئه ولو عرفه لتركه ولا يعالج الداء الذي لا يعرف والجهل داء لا يعرف فتعسر مداواته جدا لأن العارف يقدر على أن يبين للجاهل جهله ويزيله عنه إلا إذا كان معجبا برأيه وجهله فإنه لا يصغي إلى العارف ويتهمه فقد سلط الله عله بلية تهلكه وهو يظنها نعمة فكيف يمك", "english": "If you say: \"He, peace and blessings be upon him, said after his words to Fāṭimah and Ṣafiyyah: 'I cannot avail you against God in anything, except that you have a tie of kinship which I will moisten with its moisture'\" — a ḥadīth [reported as]: \"Do you hope for my intercession, while the Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib do not hope for it?\" — narrated by al-Ṭabarānī in al-Awsaṭ from the narration of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar, and in it is Uṣayrim ibn Ḥawshab from Isḥāq ibn Wāṣil, and both are extremely weak — so that indicates that he will single out his relatives for intercession.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Pride and Conceit", "section_title_ar": "Section 882", "section_title_en": "Section 882 — On the Limits of Intercession, the Vices of Pride in Lineage, Numbers, Wealth, and Opinion, and Their Treatment", "section_index": 882, "anchors": "- Root: شفع (Sh-F-')\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is pairing or joining; intercession (shafā'ah) is the joining of one's plea to another's cause.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Intercession is the seeking of benefit for another; its acceptance is contingent upon divine permission and good pleasure, as restricted by the Qur'anic qualifiers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah) is the act of making a number even (from watr, odd); metaphorical (majāz) is the mediator's plea before authority.\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-J-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Astonishment arising from an unknown cause; self-admiration (ʿujb) is a blameworthy state of being pleased with oneself.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العجب والتعجب: حالة تعرض للإنسان عند الجهل بسبب الشيء\" — a state that overtakes a person due to ignorance of the cause; hence, it is impossible regarding God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a wondrous thing; metaphorical: the one who performs marvels (taʿjābah). Self-admiration is the root of every error and innovation.\n- Root: رأي (R-'-Y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Opinion, view, or judgment formed through reflection.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ra'y is the considered opinion that may err or hit the mark; it is blameworthy when it becomes an object of ʿujb (self-admiration).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sight/vision; metaphorical: intellectual insight. The ḥadīth \"إعجاب كل ذي رأي برأيه\" (the admiration of every man of opinion for his own view) is cited as a sign of the age's corruption.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction and Qualification) — The conditional clauses (in-sharṭ) governing intercession, e.g., {إلا لمن ارتضى} and {إلا بإذنه}, impose necessary restrictions that negate any absolute reliance on intercession.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Condemnation of Pride and Conceit'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "55": [{"arabic": "رسوله وقد قال تعالى أيحسبون أن ما نمدهم به من مال وبنين نسارع لهم في الخيرات بل لا يشعرون وقال تعالى سنستدرجهم من حيث لا يعلمون وقال تعالى فتحنا عليهم أبواب كل شيء حتى إذا فرحوا بما أوتوا أخذناهم بغتة فإذا هم مبلسون وفي تفسير قوله تعالى سنستدرجهم من حيث لا يعلمون أنهم كلما أحدثوا ذنبا أحدثنا لهم نعمة ليزيد غرورهم وقال تعالى إنما نملي لهم ليزدادوا إثما وقال تعالى ولا تحسبن الله غافلا عما يعمل الظالمون إنما يؤخرهم ليوم تشخص فيه الأبصار إلى غير ذلك مما ورد في كتاب الله تعالى وسنة رسوله فمن آمن به تخلص من هذا الغرور فإن منشأ هذا الغرور الجهل ب", "english": "As for knowing it by way of authoritative transmission and affirmation (taṣdīq): it is that one believes in the Book of God — exalted is He — and affirms His Messenger. For God — exalted is He — has said: {«أيحسبون أن ما نمدهم به من مال وبنين * نسارع لهم في الخيرات بل لا يشعرون»} — \"Do they reckon that what We extend to them of wealth and sons — [that] We hasten for them good things? Nay, but they do not perceive\" (Q. 23:55-56). And He said — exalted is He: {«سنستدرجهم من حيث لا يعلمون»} — \"We shall draw them on by degrees from whence they do not know\" (Q. 7:182). And He said — exalted is He: {«فتحنا عليهم أبواب كل شيء حتى إذا فرحوا بما أوتوا أخذناهم بغتة فإذا هم مبلسون»} — \"We opened upon them the gates of everything, until when they rejoiced in what they were given, We seized them suddenly, and behold, they were in despair\" (Q. 6:44). And in the interpretation of His saying — exalted is He: {«سنستدرجهم من حيث لا يعلمون»} — it is said: \"Whenever they innovated a sin, We innovated for them a blessing, to increase their self-deception.\" And He said — exalted is He: {«إنما نملي لهم ليزدادوا إثما»} — \"We only grant them respite that they may increase in sin\" (Q. 3:178). And He said — exalted is He: {«ولا تحسبن الله غافلا عما يعمل الظالمون إنما يؤخرهم ليوم تشخص فيه الأبصار»} — \"And do not reckon God heedless of what the wrongdoers do; He only delays them to a Day when eyes will stare fixedly\" (Q. 14:42). And [there is] more of that kind which has come in the Book of God — exalted is He — and in the Sunnah of His Messenger. So whoever believes in it is delivered from this self-deception. For the origin of this self-deception is ignorance of God and of His attributes; for whoever knows Him does not feel secure from His stratagem (makr) and is not deceived by such corrupt fancies.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Delusion", "section_title_ar": "Section 888", "section_title_en": "Section 888 — On the Remedy for Self-Deception (Ghurūr): Recognizing the Signs of Divine Honor (Karāmah) and Humiliation (Hawān)", "section_index": 888, "anchors": "- Root: غرر (gh-r-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of an outward trace or mark; heedlessness in wakefulness; the blaze on a horse's forehead.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Ghurūr\" is delusion stemming from heedlessness — the soul's being overtaken while imagining security, as in {«ما غرك بربك الكريم»} (Q. 82:6). It is the state of one who is deceived by apparent favors while blind to their underlying test.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): heedlessness and exposure to predation, like a gazelle deceived by moonlight. Metaphorical (Majaz): being \"deceived by God\" (ightirār billāh) — trusting in His mercy while persisting in disobedience.\n- Root: لهم (l-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Swallowing whole; the gullet.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Ilhām\" — God's casting a meaning into the heart (al-raw'), distinct from demonic whispering; the channel of inner vision for the knowers (ʿārifūn).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to swallow. Metaphorical: the heart's receptive \"swallowing\" of divine inspiration.\n- Root: حدث (ḥ-d-th)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Origination after non-existence; newness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Ḥudūth\" — a thing's coming to be after it was not; \"muḥdith\" — the originator, exclusively God for substances (jawāhir). \"Iḥdāth\" — bringing about a novel state or act.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: temporal newness. Metaphorical: \"muḥaddath\" — one inspired with correct discernment, as if newly spoken-to.\n- Root: نعم (n-ʿ-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Softness, ease, well-being; a state of comfort.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Niʿmah\" — the good state one inhabits; \"naʿīm\" — the act of living pleasantly. Blessings are both a test and a sign, per {«وإن تعدوا نعمة الله لا تحصوها»} (Q. 16:18).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: bounty and comfort. Metaphorical: \"khafat naʿāmatuhum\" — their prosperity vanished; blessings may signal either favor or gradual ruin (istidrāj).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction and Focused Predication with \"innamā\")\n  * In {«إنما نملي لهم ليزدادوا إثما»} (Q. 3:178) and {«إنما يؤخرهم ليوم تشخص فيه الأبصار»} (Q. 14:42), \"innamā\" restricts the purpose of divine respite exclusively to increase of sin and deferred reckoning — negating any reading of it as benevolent indulgence.", "content_type": "dhikr", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on constant remembrance of God and contemplative invocations, from 'Condemnation of Delusion'", "badge": "Dhikr"}], "57": [{"arabic": "وقال تعالى {إن الذين هم من خشية ربهم مشفقون} وقال تعالى {إنا كنا قبل في أهلنا مشفقين} وقد وصف الله تعالى الملائكة عليهم السلام مع تقدسهم عن الذنوب ومواظبتهم على العبادات على الدءوب بالإشفاق فقال تعالى مخبرا عنهم يسبحون الليل والنهار لا يفترون وهم من خشيته مشفقون فمتى زال الإشفاق والحذر مما سبق به القضاء في الأزل وينكشف عند خاتمة الأجل غلب الأمن من مكر الله وذلك يوجب الكبر وهو سبب الهلاك فالكبر دليل الأمن والأمن مهلك والتواضع دليل الخوف وهو مسعد فإذن ما يفسده العابد بإضمار الكبر واحتقار ال", "english": "...so he gave some of it in charity and fed his family with some of it. He then returned, saying: \"This is good, but it is not like devoting oneself entirely to obedience of God.\" He came again in a dream a second time, and it was said to him: \"Go to so-and-so, the cobbler, and say to him: 'What is this yellowness on your face?'\" So he went to him and asked him. He replied: \"I have not seen anyone among mankind except that it occurred to me that he would be saved — but you, I [saw] that you would perish.\" The worshiper said: \"By this [very sign]...\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of Pride and Conceit", "section_title_ar": "Section 872", "section_title_en": "The Cure for Conceit (Kibr): Trials of the Soul and the Path to Humility", "section_index": 872, "anchors": "- Root: وجل (wajila)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of fear, trepidation, and trembling of the heart.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance of reverential awe before divine majesty, distinct from mere terror; it is the state of the heart that accompanies acts of obedience when mindful of their acceptance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes inward trembling; metaphorical (Majaz) extension applies to the soul's anxious state regarding its ultimate destiny.\n- Root: شفق (shafaqa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The thin, delicate edge of twilight; also compassion mixed with fear.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الإشفاق: عناية مختلطة بخوف\" — solicitous care blended with fear; when followed by (من) the sense of fear predominates, as in the cited verses.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal root refers to the redness of dusk; metaphorical usage denotes the soul's anxious solicitude, as in \"مشفقون\" (those who are apprehensive).\n- Root: كبر (kabira)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of greatness, magnitude, and seniority.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الكبير والصغير من الأسماء المتضايفة\" — relative terms; in moral theology, kibr (conceit) is the soul's self-inflation before others and before God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) denotes physical or quantitative greatness; metaphorical (Majaz) usage — \"كبر علي ذلك\" (it weighed heavily upon him) — extends to the heart's refusal to submit to truth, which is the essence of blameworthy pride.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The particle \"إنما\" restricts and confines the predicate, as in \"فإذن ما يفسده العابد...\" where the structure emphasizes that the corruption caused by hidden pride exceeds any apparent rectification.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Pride and Conceit'", "badge": "Insight"}], "60": [{"arabic": "والذي يدل على فضيلة هذه الخصلة قوله تعالى {يؤتون ما آتوا وقلوبهم وجلة أنهم إلى ربهم راجعون} أي أنهم يؤتون الطاعات وهم على وجل عظيم من قبولها وقال تعالى {إن الذين هم من خشية ربهم مشفقون} وقال تعالى {إنا كنا قبل في أهلنا مشفقين} وقد وصف الله تعالى الملائكة عليهم السلام مع تقدسهم عن الذنوب ومواظبتهم على العبادات على الدءوب بالإشفاق فقال تعالى مخبرا عنهم يسبحون الليل والنهار لا يفترون وهم من خشيته مشفقون فمتى زال الإشفاق والحذر مما سبق به القضاء في الأزل وينكشف عند خاتمة الأجل غلب الأمن من مكر الله وذلك يوجب الكبر وه", "english": "...so he gave some of it in charity and fed his family with some of it. He then returned, saying: \"This is good, but it is not like devoting oneself entirely to obedience of God.\" He came again in a dream a second time, and it was said to him: \"Go to so-and-so, the cobbler, and say to him: 'What is this yellowness on your face?'\" So he went to him and asked him. He replied: \"I have not seen anyone among mankind except that it occurred to me that he would be saved — but you, I [saw] that you would perish.\" The worshiper said: \"By this [very sign]...\"", "book_title": "Fear and Hope", "section_title_ar": "Section 872", "section_title_en": "The Cure for Conceit (Kibr): Trials of the Soul and the Path to Humility", "section_index": 872, "anchors": "- Root: وجل (wajila)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of fear, trepidation, and trembling of the heart.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance of reverential awe before divine majesty, distinct from mere terror; it is the state of the heart that accompanies acts of obedience when mindful of their acceptance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes inward trembling; metaphorical (Majaz) extension applies to the soul's anxious state regarding its ultimate destiny.\n- Root: شفق (shafaqa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The thin, delicate edge of twilight; also compassion mixed with fear.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الإشفاق: عناية مختلطة بخوف\" — solicitous care blended with fear; when followed by (من) the sense of fear predominates, as in the cited verses.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal root refers to the redness of dusk; metaphorical usage denotes the soul's anxious solicitude, as in \"مشفقون\" (those who are apprehensive).\n- Root: كبر (kabira)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of greatness, magnitude, and seniority.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الكبير والصغير من الأسماء المتضايفة\" — relative terms; in moral theology, kibr (conceit) is the soul's self-inflation before others and before God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) denotes physical or quantitative greatness; metaphorical (Majaz) usage — \"كبر علي ذلك\" (it weighed heavily upon him) — extends to the heart's refusal to submit to truth, which is the essence of blameworthy pride.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The particle \"إنما\" restricts and confines the predicate, as in \"فإذن ما يفسده العابد...\" where the structure emphasizes that the corruption caused by hidden pride exceeds any apparent rectification.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Fear and Hope'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "يواظبون على العبادات ويؤتون ما آتوا وقلوبهم وجلة أنهم إلى ربهم راجعون يخافون على أنفسهم وهم طول الليل والنهار في طاعة الله يبالغون في التقوى والحذر من الشبهات والشهوات ويبكون على أنفسهم في الخلوات وأما الآن فترى الخلق آمنين مسرورين مطمئنين غير خائفين مع إكبابهم على المعاصي وانهماكهم في الدنيا وإعراضهم عن الله تعالى زاعمين أنهم واثقون بكرم الله تعالى وفضله راجون", "english": "Every soul shall taste what it has earned. And {«وَأَنَّ كُلَّ نَفْسٍ بِمَا كَسَبَتْ رَهِينَةٌ»} — \"And that every soul is a pledge for what it has earned.\" So what has deluded you concerning God, after you have heard and comprehended? They will say: \"Had we but listened or reasoned, we would not be among the companions of the Blaze.\" Thus they will confess their sin. So away with the companions of the Blaze!", "book_title": "Condemnation of Delusion", "section_title_ar": "Section 890", "section_title_en": "The Anatomy of Hope (Rajā') and Delusion (Ghurūr): A Kalamic Dissection of the Soul's Two States", "section_index": 890, "anchors": "- Root: نفس (Nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, or spirit; that which desires proximity and discourse; the locus of breath and life.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Nafs denotes the rūḥ (spirit) and, in divine attribution, the very Dhāt (Essence) of God—as in {«وَيُحَذِّرُكُمُ اللَّهُ نَفْسَهُ»}—entailing no real multiplicity despite grammatical annexation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage refers to the living, breathing self; metaphorical (Majaz) usage extends to \"blood\" (nafs sā'ilah) and the \"evil eye\" (nafs as 'ayn). Here, it signifies the moral agent bearing responsibility.\n- Root: توب (Tawb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Return, repentance; the act of turning back from error.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Tawbah is abandoning the sin in the most excellent manner—the third and highest form of excuse (after denial and pretext). In Sacred Law: forsaking the sin due to its ugliness, remorse for the past, resolve against recurrence, and restitution of what can be restored.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"The servant repents to God from his sin; God turns in mercy to the servant.\" The reciprocal dynamic—God's acceptance (tawbah) meeting the servant's return (inābah)—is operative in this text.\n- Root: ذنب (Dhanb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The tail of an animal; what trails behind; by extension, a fault or crime as the \"consequence\" one drags after oneself.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In origin, \"taking hold of a thing's tail\"—i.e., seizing the outcome. Sin is that which trails the soul and binds it as a pledge (rahīnah).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Metaphorical: \"He is among the tails (adhnāb) of the people\"—the laggards. The sinner is one who follows his lower tail, and the verse {«كُلُّ نَفْسٍ بِمَا كَسَبَتْ رَهِينَةٌ»} makes the soul a hostage to its own trailing deeds.\n- Root: غفر (Ghafr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To cover, shield, or protect; the Ghifār tribe's name; a helmet (mighfar) that shields the head.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ghufrān from God is His shielding the servant from the touch of chastisement—a protective covering, not mere erasure. It may also mean outward forbearance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"O God, forgive (ghufrānan)!\"—a covering over the stain. Metaphorical: Zuhayr's beasts \"did not forgive\" their negligence—i.e., did not shield the offspring from destruction. Hope of forgiveness without repentance is thus not ghufrān but ghurūr (delusion).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين العامل والمعمول (Interposition between Operator and Operand) — The periodic structure of verses like {«إِنَّ اللَّهَ يَغْفِرُ الذُّنُوبَ جَمِيعًا»} delays the predicate to heighten rhetorical impact; the conditional \"if you say... know that\" (fa-in qulta... fa-'lam) governs the entire dialectical sequence, requiring the apodosis to resolve the protasis with logical necessity.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Delusion'", "badge": "Insight"}], "86": [{"arabic": "العرش العظيم لا إله إلا الله رب السموات السبع ورب العرش الكريم (10) فإن أردت النوم فتوضأ أولا ثم توسد على يمينك مستقبل القبلة ثم كبر الله تعالى أربعا وثلاثين وسبحه ثلاثا وثلاثين واحمده ثلاثا وثلاثين (11) ثم قل اللهم إني أعوذ برضاك من سخطك وبمعافاتك من عقوبتك وأعوذ بك منك اللهم إني لا أستطيع أن أبلغ ثناء عليك ولو حرصت ولكن أنت كما أثنيت على نفسك (12) اللهم باسمك أحيا وأموت (13) اللهم رب السموات ورب الأرض ورب كل شيء ومليكه فالق الحب والنوى ومنزل التوراة والإنجيل والقرآن أعوذ بك من شر كل ذي شر و", "english": "When you rise in the morning and hear the call to prayer (adhān), it is recommended for you to respond to the caller of prayer (mu'adhdhin) — and we have already mentioned this, along with the supplications for entering and leaving the privy, and the supplications of ablution (wuḍū'), in the Book of Purification. When you set out toward the mosque, say: \"O Allah, place light (nūr) in my heart, and light in my tongue, and place light in my hearing, and place light in my sight, and place light behind me, and light before me, and place light above me. O Allah, grant me light.\" And also say: \"O Allah, I ask You by the right of those who ask of You, and by the right of this my walking unto You — for I have not gone out in insolence, nor in vanity, nor for show, nor for reputation. I have gone out seeking protection from Your wrath and seeking Your good pleasure. So I ask You to deliver me from the Fire and to forgive me my sins — verily, none forgives sins but You.\"", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "section_title_ar": "Section 254", "section_title_en": "Section 254 — Supplications for the Hours of Day and Night: From Dawn to Sleep", "section_index": 254, "anchors": "- Root: ن و ر (n-w-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: النور: الضوء المنتشر الذي يُبصر به، وهو ضد الظلمة\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): النور في القرآن يقع على ضروب: منها المعقول كالإيمان والحكمة، قال تعالى: {قد جاءكم من الله نور وكتاب مبين}، ومنها المحسوس كضوء الشمس والقمر\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): النور حقيقة في الضوء المحسوس، ومجاز في الهدى والإيمان والعلم — فدعاء النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم بطلب النور يشمل المعنيين جميعاً\n- Root: س ج د (s-j-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: السجود: وضع الجبهة على الأرض تعظيماً، وأصله التطامن والانخفاض\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): السجود ضربان: سجود عبادة وهو وضع الجبهة على الأرض تقرباً، وسجود طاعة وهو الانقياد والخضوع — قال تعالى: {ولله يسجد من في السموات والأرض طوعاً وكرهاً}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): سجد حقيقة في وضع الجبهة، ومجاز في الخضوع والانقياد — وقوله \"سجد لك سوادي وخيالي\" من المجاز المرسل\n- Root: ذ ك ر (dh-k-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: الذكر: نقيض النسيان، والذكر: الصوت الذي يُذكر به الشيء، والذكر: الشرف والصيت\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الذكر ضربان: ذكر بالقلب وهو التفكر في عظمة الله، وذكر باللسان وهو التلفظ بأسمائه — وأعلى مراتبه ما اجتمع فيه القلب واللسان\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): الذكر حقيقة في استحضار الشيء بالقلب أو اللسان، ومجاز في الصلاة والدعاء والثناء\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — جواز تقديم المعمول وتأخيره مع بقاء العامل عاملاً في المعنى، كقوله \"لك الحمد\" حيث تقدم الجار والمجرور على المبتدأ للاختصاص والاهتمام", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'", "badge": "Du'a"}, {"arabic": "حين ينام وهو واضع خده على يده اليمنى وهو يرى أنه ميت في ليلته تلك اللهم رب السموات السبع ورب العرش العظيم ربنا ورب كل شيء ومليكه (2) الدعاء إلى آخره كما ذكرناه في كتاب الدعوات فحق على العبد أن يفتش عن ثلاثة عند نومه أنه على ماذا ينام وما الغالب عليه حب الله تعالى وحب لقائه أو حب الدنيا وليتحقق أنه يتوفى على ما هو الغالب عليه ويحشر على ما يتوفى عليه فإن المرء مع من أحب ومع ما أحب العاشر الدعاء عن التنبه فليقل في تيقظاته وتقلباته مهما تنبه ما كان يقوله رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم لا إله إلا الله الواحد القهار رب السموات والأرض وما بينهما العزيز الغف", "english": "Then it is recommended, after the salutation of the witr, that he say: \"Glory be to the King, the Most Holy, the Lord of the angels and the Spirit. You have enveloped the heavens and the earth with majesty and dominion. You have manifested Your might through power, and You have subdued the servants through death.\" It is related that he (peace and blessings be upon him) — until he died — most of his prayer was seated, except for the obligatory prayers (1). And he said to the one who prays seated: \"Half the reward of the one who prays standing,\" and to the one who sleeps: \"Half the reward of the one who prays seated\" (2). This indicates the validity of the supererogatory prayer in all states.", "book_title": "Arrangement of Litanies", "section_title_ar": "Section 264", "section_title_en": "The Fourth and Fifth Wirds (Portions of Night Devotion): On Sleep and the Final Sixth of the Night", "section_index": 264, "anchors": "- Root: ليل (Layl)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Night; the period of darkness from sunset to dawn; its plural forms include layālī and layalāt.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Night is a divinely ordained sign (āyah) of Allah's power; sleep is described as a form of death (tawaffā) in the Qur'an, linking the nightly cycle to resurrection.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah) for the astronomical night; metaphorical (majāz) for spiritual darkness or heedlessness.\n- Root: نوم (Nawm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sleep; slumber; a state of rest.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Sleep is \"death in a light form\" (al-mawt al-khafīf), and death is \"sleep in a heavy form\" (al-nawm al-thaqīl). Allah says: {«Allah takes the souls at the time of their death, and those that die not during their sleep»} (Q. 39:42).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: bodily rest. Metaphorical: heedlessness (ghaflah) or spiritual inertia; \"the market slept\" means it stagnated.\n- Root: عله (Alaha/Ilah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To worship; to seek refuge; the root for ilāh (deity) and Allāh (the Supreme Deity).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The root denotes the act of devotion and the object of ultimate veneration; al-ulūhiyyah is the state of being worshipped.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: deity. Metaphorical: that which one is utterly devoted to or enslaved by (e.g., one's desires).\n- Root: الي (Alā/Yalū)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be occupied with; to manage; related to al-ālah (instrument) and ma'ālah (consequence).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The root conveys the idea of returning to a state or being entrusted with a matter; al-ālah is that by which one accomplishes an affair.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: instrumentality. Metaphorical: the means by which one draws near to Allah.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Focused Predication (al-Ḥaṣr wa al-Taqyīd bi-innamā) — Used to distinguish the specific legal scope of actions (e.g., the witr prayer's status) and to restrict general statements to their precise conditions.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Arrangement of Litanies'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "91": [{"arabic": "كان النخعي إذا مر بمثل قوله عز وجل {ما اتخذ الله من ولد وما كان معه من إله} يخفض صوته كالمستحيي عن أن يذكره بكل شيء لا يليق به وروي أنه يقال لقارىء القرآن اقرأ وارق ورتل كما كنت ترتل في الدنيا (2) وأما دوام القيام فإنه تنبيه على إقامة القلب مع الله عز وجل على نعت واحد من الحضور قال صلى الله عليه وسلم إن الله عز وجل مقبل على المصلي ما لم يلتفت (3) وكما تجب حراسة الرأس والعين عن الالتفات إلى الجهات فكذلك تجب حراسة السر عن الالتفات إلى غير الصلاة فإذا التفت إلى غيره فذكره باطلاع الله عليه وبقبح التهاون بالمناجى عند غفلة ا", "english": "For this reason, he, peace be upon him, said: \"Give us rest, O Bilāl\" (1) — meaning, give us rest through it (the prayer) and through the call to it, for his delight, peace be upon him, was in it.", "book_title": "Mysteries of Prayer", "section_title_ar": "Section 138", "section_title_en": "The Inner Works of Prayer: Unveiling the Heart's Secrets in the Ritual Prayer (Asrār al-Ṣalāh)", "section_index": 138, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn a thing over, to revolve it, to divert it from one state to another; the heart as that which is constantly turning and fluctuating.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — The heart is that which is turned and diverted; its rectitude lies in its orientation towards God, its ruin in its turning towards other than Him.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical (Majaz): \"قلب المعلم الصبيان: صرفهم إلى بيوتهم\" (the teacher turned the boys towards their homes) — thus the heart's turning is its direction and intent.\n- Root: وجه (W-J-H)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The face; the foremost, noblest, and most apparent part of a thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"ولما كان الوجه أول ما يستقبلك، وأشرف ما في ظاهر البدن استعمل في مستقبل كل شيء، وفي أشرفه ومبدئه\" — The face is used metaphorically for the essence and the direction of a thing; the \"face of the heart\" is its intention and aspiration.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"واجهته مواجهة ووجاها\" (to face one another). Metaphorical: \"هذا وجه الثوب. ووجه القوم، وهؤلاء وجوه البلد\" (the face of a garment, the chiefs of a people) — thus the \"face\" of the heart is its foremost orientation.\n- Root: وجل (W-J-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A trembling of the heart from fear or awe.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — The sensing of fear; the heart's tremor before the majesty of the Divine.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال، وقد وجل وجلا\" — A man trembling with fear.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The particle \"innamā\" restricts and confines the predicate to the subject alone, negating it from all else. This is crucial for understanding \"إنما هذه الظواهر تحريكات للبواطن\" — the outward acts are *only* movements to stir the inward realities, nothing more.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Mysteries of Prayer'", "badge": "Insight"}], "96": [{"arabic": "المداراة وهي مع من يخاف شره قال الله تعالى {ادفع بالتي هي أحسن السيئة} قال ابن عباس في معنى قوله {ويدرؤون بالحسنة السيئة} أي الفحش والأذى بالسلام والمداراة وقال في قوله تعالى {ولولا دفع الله الناس بعضهم ببعض} قال بالرغبة والرهبة والحياء والمداراة وقالت عائشة رضي الله عنها استأذن رجل على رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم قال ائذنوا له فبئس رجل العشيرة هو فلما دخل ألان له القول حتى أن له عنده منزلة فلما خرج قلت له لما دخل قلت الذي قلت ثم ألنت له القول فقال يا عائشة إن شر الناس منزلة عند الله يوم القيامة من ترك", "english": "A man defended him, and the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: \"Whoever defends the honor of his brother shall have it as a shield for him against the Fire.\" (1)", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 435", "section_title_en": "Section 435", "section_index": 435, "anchors": "- Root: رد (r-d-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To return, repel, defend, avert; al-radd ʿan al-ʿirḍ is to repel defamation from one's honor\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Defense against false accusation; the theological nuance of divine recompense (al-mukāfaʾah) for defending a brother's honor\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal repulsion of physical harm extended metaphorically to verbal defense of honor (ḥaqīqah vs. majāz)\n- Root: عطس (ʿ-t-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sneezing; al-tashmīt is the invocation of mercy upon the sneezer\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The sneeze is attributed to God (min Allāh) as a sign of divine favor, while yawning is attributed to Satan\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The response \"yarḥamukallāh\" is a supplication, not an assertion — distinguishing literal invocation from mere congratulation\n- Root: عاد (ʿ-w-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To visit repeatedly; al-ʿiyādah is the visitation of the sick\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Returning to someone in his affliction; the reward is measured by the intensity of compassion shown\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The \"return\" (ʿawd) is literal in visitation, but the reward is spiritual — a metaphorical extension of recompense\n- Root: شيع (sh-y-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To accompany, follow; al-tashyīʿ is the funeral procession\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Following the bier with the intention of honoring the deceased and taking admonition (al-iʿtibār)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The qīrāṭ is a measure of reward — metaphorical weight, not literal coin\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Conditional Protasis (Bāb al-sharṭ) — The construction \"man ... fa-...\" (whoever does X, then Y) establishes a causal nexus requiring the apodosis to be a new sentence, often carrying the force of divine decree.\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "97": [{"arabic": "ويقول عند ذلك أعوذ بك من همزات الشياطين وأعوذ بك رب أن يحضرون ثم يغسل يديه ثلاثا قبل أن يدخلهما الإناء ويقول اللهم إني أسألك اليمن والبركة وأعوذ بك من الشؤم والهلكة ثم ينوي رفع الحدث أو استباحة الصلاة ويستديم النية إلى غسل الوجه فإن نسيها عند الوجه لم يجزه ثم يأخذ غرفة لفيه بيمينه فيتمضمض بها ثلاثا ويغرغر بأن يرد الماء إلى الغلصمة إلا أن يكون صائما فيرفق ويقول اللهم أعني على تلاوة كتابك وكثرة الذكر لك ثم يأخذ غرفة لأنفه ويستنشق ثلاثا ويصعد الماء بالنفس إلى خياشيمه ويستنثر ما فيها ويقول في الاستنشا", "english": "Know that the use of the tooth-stick is a confirmed Sunnah. It is reported concerning its virtue from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUmar through a weak chain of transmission, and Abū Dāwūd and al-Ḥākim narrated it, and the latter declared it sound (ṣaḥīḥ). Al-Bayhaqī, however, deemed it weak from the ḥadīth of ʿĀʾishah, and he weakened the version containing the wording \"from seventy prayers.\" There is the ḥadīth: \"Were it not that I would impose hardship upon my community, I would have commanded them to use the tooth-stick at every prayer\" — agreed upon (muttafaq ʿalayh) from the ḥadīth of Abū Hurayrah. There is the ḥadīth: \"Why do I see you entering upon me with yellowed teeth? Use the tooth-stick!\" — al-Bazzār and al-Bayhaqī narrated it from the ḥadīth of al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib; Abū Dāwūd and al-Baghawī from the ḥadīth of Tammām ibn al-ʿAbbās; and al-Bayhaqī from the ḥadīth of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās — and it is disordered (muḍṭarib). There is the ḥadīth: \"He used the tooth-stick multiple times during the night\" — Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿAbbās. And the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿAbbās: \"The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, did not cease commanding us to use the tooth-stick until we thought that something concerning it would be revealed to him\" — Aḥmad narrated it.", "book_title": "Arrangement of Litanies", "section_title_ar": "Section 112", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Tooth-Stick (Siwāk) and the Etiquette of Ablution (Wuḍūʾ)", "section_index": 112, "anchors": "- Root: س و ك (s-w-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes rubbing or cleansing the teeth with a stick (siwāk); al-Farāhīdī notes its use for the act of friction against a surface to remove residue.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the religious lexicon, siwāk is a purifying act (taṭhīr) of the mouth, tied to devotional hygiene and the Sunnah's emphasis on preparing the body for prayer.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage refers to the physical stick (ʿūd) and the mechanical action; metaphorical (majāz) extension applies to spiritual purification, as the mouth's cleanliness mirrors the heart's purity.\n- Root: و ض ء (w-ḍ-ʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Wuḍūʾ signifies washing specific bodily parts for ritual purity; al-Khalīl links it to brightness and radiance (ḍawʾ), as the washed limbs shine on the Day of Resurrection.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): It is a legislated act of cleansing (ṭahārah) that removes minor ritual impurity (ḥadath), preconditioning the soul for communion with God in prayer.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: washing with water; metaphorical: the inner purification of the heart from vices, as the outward washing symbolizes inward renewal.\n- Root: غ ر ر (gh-r-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Ghurrah denotes a blaze or white mark on a horse's forehead; al-Ayn records its use for a prominent, shining feature.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the ḥadīth, \"ghurrah\" refers to the luminous mark on the forehead of the believer from washing in ablution, a sign of honor on the Day of Judgment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the white star on a horse's brow; metaphorical: the radiant trace of worship on the believer's body, visible in the Hereafter.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-ʿAṭf ʿalā al-Sabab (Coordination of Causes) — When linking actions or supplications (duʿāʾ) with fa- or thumma, the syntactic sequence mirrors the chronological order of ritual acts, ensuring that each clause is semantically complete and temporally precise.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Arrangement of Litanies'", "badge": "Du'a"}, {"arabic": "الرجيم {رب أعوذ بك من همزات الشياطين وأعوذ بك رب أن يحضرون} وليقرأ قل أعوذ برب الناس وسورة الحمد لله وليقل عند فراغه من القراءة صدق الله تعالى وبلغ رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم اللهم انفعنا به وبارك لنا فيه الحمد لله رب العالمين وأستغفر الله الحي القيوم وفي أثناء القراءة إذا مر بآية تسبيح سبح وكبر وإذا مر بآية دعاء واستغفار دعا واستغفر وإن مر بمرجو سأل وإن مر بمخوف استعاذ يفعل ذلك بلسانه أو بقلبه فيقول سبحان الله نعوذ بالله اللهم ارزقنا اللهم ارحمنا قال حذيفة صليت مع رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم فاب", "english": "If he recites without ritual purity, reclining upon his bed, he also has merit, but it is below that [of the one who is pure and seated properly]. Allah Most High said: {الَّذِينَ يَذْكُرُونَ اللَّهَ قِيَامًا وَقُعُودًا وَعَلَىٰ جُنُوبِهِمْ وَيَتَفَكَّرُونَ فِي خَلْقِ السَّمَاوَاتِ وَالْأَرْضِ} (\"Those who remember Allah standing, sitting, and upon their sides, and reflect upon the creation of the heavens and the earth\") — and He praised all of them, but He mentioned standing first, then sitting, then remembrance while reclining.", "book_title": "Arrangement of Litanies", "section_title_ar": "Section 223", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Etiquette of Qur'an Recitation — Section 223: On the Manners of the Reciter, the Measure of Recitation, Division into Portions, Orthography, Tartīl, Weeping, Rights of the Verses, Invocations, and Vocalization", "section_index": 223, "anchors": "- Root: قرا (Q-R-A)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To read, recite, gather together; the core act of assembling letters into articulated speech.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Recitation (tilāwah) is the following of the Qur'an in obedience and belief; qirā'ah is the articulation of the letters. The term carries both the physical act of vocalization and the spiritual act of devotion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah): to read from a written text or from memory. Metaphorical (majāz): \"qara'a al-rajul\" — to become religious, devoted, as the reciter is characterized by the state of worship.\n- Root: سجد (S-J-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To bow, prostrate, lower oneself; the physical posture of placing the forehead on the ground.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Prostration is of two kinds: voluntary (ikhtiyārī) — exclusive to humans, by which reward is deserved; and subjugated (taskhīrī) — universal to all creation, as in {وَلِلَّهِ يَسْجُدُ مَنْ فِي السَّمَاوَاتِ وَالْأَرْضِ}.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the physical act of prostration. Metaphorical: \"shajarah sājidah\" — a tree bending over; \"ʿayn sājidah\" — a languid, drooping eye. The prostration of recitation is the literal act performed in obedience to the command of the verse.\n- Root: تلو (T-L-W)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To follow, to recite; the reciter \"follows\" the letters in sequence and \"follows\" the commands contained therein.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Tilāwah is following the Qur'an in deed as well as in word; the true reciter is he whose recitation is a following of the divine command.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The conditional and restrictive structures in the prophetic sayings (e.g., \"من قرأ القرآن في أقل من ثلاث لم يفقهه\") establish a causal link between the act and its consequence, requiring precise parsing of the conditional clause to extract the legal ruling.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Arrangement of Litanies'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "99": [{"arabic": "القرظي أنه كان يقرأ قوله تعالى حتى إذا جاء أحدهم الموت قال ربي أرجعون لعلي أعمل صالحا فيما تركت قال أي شيء تريد في أي شيء ترغب أتريد أن ترجع لتجمع المال وتغرس الغراس وتبني البنيان وتشق الأنهار قال لا لعلي أعمل صالحا فيما تركت قال فيقول الجبار {كلا إنها كلمة هو قائلها} أي ليقولنها عند الموت وقال أبو هريرة قال النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم المؤمن في قبره في روضة خضراء ويرحب له في قبره سبعون ذراعا ويضىء حتى يكون كالقمر ليلة البدر هل تدرون فيماذا أنزلت فإن له معيشة ضنكا قالوا الله ورسوله أعلم قال عذاب الكافر في قبره يسلط عليه تسعة وتسعون تنينا هل", "english": "Al-Barā' ibn ʿĀzib said: We went out with the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, in the funeral procession of a man from the Anṣār. The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, sat at his grave with his head bowed, then said: \"O God, I seek refuge in You from the torment of the grave\" — three times. Then he said: \"Verily, the believer, when he is in the final stage of the Hereafter and the severance from this world, God sends down to him angels whose faces are like the sun, bearing with them his embalming perfume and his shroud. They sit before him as far as his eye can reach. When his soul departs, every angel between heaven and earth prays for him, and every angel in heaven [prays for him], and the gates of heaven are opened — there is no gate but that it yearns for his soul to enter through it. When his soul ascends, it is said: 'O Lord, Your servant so-and-so.' He says: 'Return him, and show him what I have prepared for him of honor, for I promised him: {From it We created you, and into it We shall return you, and from it We shall bring you forth another time} [Ta-Ha/ 55].' And he indeed hears the shuffling of their sandals when they turn away departing, until it is said to him: 'O this one, who is your Lord, and what is your religion, and who is your Prophet?' He says: 'My Lord is God, my religion is Islam, and my Prophet is Muhammad, may God bless him and grant him peace.' He said: Then he is rebuked with a severe rebuke — and this is the last opportunity presented to the dead — and when he says that, a caller calls out: 'He has spoken the truth' — and this is the meaning of His saying, the Exalted: {God establishes those who believe with the firm word} [Ibrahim/ 27]. Then there comes to him a visitor of beautiful countenance, pleasant fragrance, and fine garments, who says: 'Rejoice in the mercy of your Lord and gardens of enduring delight.' He says: 'And you — may God give you good news of good. Who are you?' He says: 'I am your righteous deed. By God, I knew not but that you were swift to the obedience of God and slow to the disobedience of God — so may God reward you with good.' He said: Then a caller calls out: 'Spread for him from the carpets of Paradise, and open for him a gate to Paradise.' So carpets of Paradise are spread for him, and a gate to Paradise is opened for him. He says: 'O God, hasten the establishment of the Hour, so that I may return to my family and my wealth.' He said: And as for the disbeliever — when he is in the final stage of the Hereafter and the severance from this world, there descend to him angels, harsh and severe, bearing with them garments of fire and tunics of molten pitch. They surround him completely. When his soul departs, every angel between heaven and earth curses him, and every angel in heaven [curses him], and the gates of heaven are shut — there is no gate but that it abhors his soul to enter through it. When his soul ascends, it is cast away, and it is said: 'O Lord, Your servant so-and-so — neither heaven nor earth accepted him.' So God, Mighty and Majestic, says: 'Return him, and show him what I have prepared for him of evil, for I promised him: {From it We created you, and into it We shall return you, and from it We shall bring you forth another time}.' And he indeed hears the shuffling of their sandals when they turn away departing, until it is said to him: 'O this one, who is your Lord, and who is your Prophet, and what is your religion?' He says: 'I do not know.' It is said: 'You did not know!' Then there comes to him a visitor of ugly countenance, foul stench, and vile garments, who says: 'Rejoice in the wrath of God and a painful enduring torment.' He says: 'May God give you evil news. Who are you?' He says: 'I am your wicked deed. By God, I knew not but that you were swift in the disobedience of God and slow to the obedience of God — so may God reward you with evil.' He says: 'And you — may God reward you with evil.' Then there is appointed for him a blind, deaf, and mute one, bearing with him an iron mace — were the two heavy species (thaqalān, i.e., jinn and men) to gather upon it to lift it, they could not — and were he to strike a mountain with it, it would become dust. He strikes him with it a blow, and he becomes dust. Then the soul is returned to him, and he strikes him with it between his eyes a blow whose sound is heard by all upon the earths, save the two heavy species. He said: Then a caller calls out: 'Spread for him two planks of fire, and open for him a gate to the Fire.' So two planks of fire are spread for him, and a gate to the Fire is opened for him.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1323", "section_title_en": "The Torments of the Grave and the Visions of the Soul at Death — Section 1323", "section_index": 1323, "anchors": "- Root: قبر (q-b-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The grave as the abode of the dead; the act of interment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The grave is the place of repose for the dead, and the term extends metaphorically to the state of the barzakh (intermediate realm). The Quranic phrase {حتى زرتم المقابر} [التكاثر/ 2] is a metonymy for death itself.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) — the physical grave. Metaphorical (Majaz) — the spiritual state of the soul after death, as in \"رفع قبراه\" (raising one's grave-mound), i.e., arrogance, indicating that the grave's true reality transcends mere earth.\n- Root: ملك (m-l-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Dominion and authority; the angels as emissaries of divine command.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الملك: هو المتصرف بالأمر والنهي\" — the one who disposes by command and prohibition. Angels are termed \"رسل\" (messengers) of God, as in {الله يصطفي من الملائكة رسلا} [الحج/ 75], and their acts are entirely subordinate to divine will.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — angels as corporeal agents of punishment or mercy. Metaphorical — their true nature is immaterial (laṭīfah rabbāniyyah), imperceptible to the physical senses of the living.\n- Root: روح (r-w-ḥ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Breath, spirit, the animating principle.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The soul (rūḥ) is a subtle divine reality (laṭīfah rabbāniyyah) that departs the body at death; its extraction is described in degrees of ease or violence according to faith or disbelief.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — the breath of life. Metaphorical — the spiritual essence that perceives the malakūt (invisible dominion) after death, a realm beyond the reach of the corporeal eye.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The periodic structure of the hadith, e.g., \"خرجنا مع رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم في جنازة رجل من الأنصار,\" demonstrates how prepositional phrases (مع، في، من) interlock to build a narrative sequence, each clause grammatically complete yet subordinate to the overarching report, mirroring the unbroken chain of transmission (isnād).", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Afterlife"}, {"arabic": "دخل فيه فاضطجع فيه ومكث ساعة ثم قال {رب أرجعون لعلي أعمل صالحا فيما تركت} ثم يقول يا ربيع قد أرجعت فاعمل الآن قبل أن لا ترجع وقال ميمون بن مهران خرجت مع عمر بن عبد العزيز إلى المقبرة فلما نظر إلى القبور بكى وقال يا ميمون هذه قبور آبائي بني أمية كأنهم لم يشاركوا أهل الدنيا في لذاتهم أما تراهم صرعى قد خلت بهم المثلات وأصاب الهوام من أبدانهم ثم بكى وقال والله ما أعلم أحدا أنعم ممن صار إلى هذه القبور وقد أمن من عذاب الله وآداب المعزي خفض الجناح وإظهار الحزن وقلة الحديث وترك التبسم وآداب تشييع الجنا", "english": "A man defended him, and the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: \"Whoever defends the honor of his brother shall have it as a shield for him against the Fire.\" (1)", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 435", "section_title_en": "Section 435", "section_index": 435, "anchors": "- Root: رد (r-d-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To return, repel, defend, avert; al-radd ʿan al-ʿirḍ is to repel defamation from one's honor\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Defense against false accusation; the theological nuance of divine recompense (al-mukāfaʾah) for defending a brother's honor\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal repulsion of physical harm extended metaphorically to verbal defense of honor (ḥaqīqah vs. majāz)\n- Root: عطس (ʿ-t-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sneezing; al-tashmīt is the invocation of mercy upon the sneezer\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The sneeze is attributed to God (min Allāh) as a sign of divine favor, while yawning is attributed to Satan\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The response \"yarḥamukallāh\" is a supplication, not an assertion — distinguishing literal invocation from mere congratulation\n- Root: عاد (ʿ-w-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To visit repeatedly; al-ʿiyādah is the visitation of the sick\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Returning to someone in his affliction; the reward is measured by the intensity of compassion shown\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The \"return\" (ʿawd) is literal in visitation, but the reward is spiritual — a metaphorical extension of recompense\n- Root: شيع (sh-y-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To accompany, follow; al-tashyīʿ is the funeral procession\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Following the bier with the intention of honoring the deceased and taking admonition (al-iʿtibār)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The qīrāṭ is a measure of reward — metaphorical weight, not literal coin\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Conditional Protasis (Bāb al-sharṭ) — The construction \"man ... fa-...\" (whoever does X, then Y) establishes a causal nexus requiring the apodosis to be a new sentence, often carrying the force of divine decree.\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "100": [{"arabic": "ظعنوا فليس لهم إياب هيهات هيهات {كلا إنها كلمة هو قائلها ومن ورائهم برزخ إلى يوم يبعثون} فكأن قد صرتم إلى ما صاروا إليه من البلاء والوحدة في دار المثوى وارتهنتم في ذلك المضجع وضمكم ذلك المستودع فكيف بكم لو عاينتم الأمور وبعثرت القبور وحصل ما في الصدور وأوقفتم للتحصيل بين يدي الملك الجليل فطارت القلوب لإشفاقها من سالف الذنوب وهتكت عنكم الحجب والأستار وظهرت منكم العيوب والأسرار هنالك تجزى كل نفس بما كسبت إن الله عز وجل يقول ليجزى الذي أساءوا بما عملوا ويجزى الذي أحسنوا بالحسنى وقال تعالى {ووضع الكتاب ف", "english": "And indeed there has come the narration from him, from his Lord, Mighty and Glorious, that He said to Moses, peace be upon him: \"When you see the rich man approaching, say: 'A sin whose punishment has been hastened.' And when you see the poor man approaching, say: 'Welcome to the badge of the righteous.'\" And if you wish, you may follow the possessor of the Spirit and the Word, Jesus son of Mary, peace be upon him — for he used to say: \"My condiment is hunger, my badge is fear, my garment is wool, my warmth in winter is the rising places of the sun, my lamp is the moon, my mount is my two feet, and my food and my fruit is what the earth brings forth. I pass the night and I possess nothing, and I rise in the morning and I possess nothing — and there is no one upon the earth richer than I.\"", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 737", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 737: On the Censure of This World and the Description of Its Perils", "section_index": 737, "anchors": "- Root: دنو (danuww)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"دنا فهو دانٍ\" — proximity, nearness; the world (al-dunyā) is so named because it is nearer and lower, deferred from the Hereafter.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الدنو: القرب بالذات، أو بالحكم\" — proximity by essence or by decree; used of place, time, and station. The \"nearer\" (al-adnā) is contrasted with the greater and the better.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical nearness. Metaphorical: the lower, contemptible station — \"أهل الدناءة هم أهل الشناءة\" (the people of baseness are the people of abhorrence).\n- Root: يوم (yawm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"اليوم\" — a day; also any period of time.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اليوم يعبر به عن وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها. وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — the day is the time from sunrise to sunset, or any duration whatsoever. \"وذكرهم بأيام الله\" — the days of God are His mighty deeds and calamities upon the disbelievers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the solar day. Metaphorical: \"أيام العرب\" — their battles and vicissitudes; \"أيام الله\" — His punitive visitations.\n- Root: وقد (waqada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"وقدت النار تقد وقودا ووقدا\" — the fire blazed; al-wuqūd is the fuel.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"وقودها الناس والحجارة\" — the fuel of the Fire is men and stones; the one who kindles it for himself (istawqada) is he who seeks its light.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: kindling a fire. Metaphorical: \"قلب وقاد\" — a blazing, keen heart; \"وقد الحصى\" — the pebbles became scorching hot (i.e., the intensity of the sun's heat).\n- Root: ألك (alaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"المألكة: الرسالة، لأنها تؤلك في الفم\" — the message (risālah) is called ma'lakah because it is chewed/worked in the mouth.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الملائكة تقع على الواحد والجمع\" — angels (malā'ikah) are so named as bearers of messages; \"الله يصطفي من الملائكة رسلا\" {«الله يصطفي من الملائكة رسلا»} — God chooses messengers from the angels.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: chewing (as a horse chews its bit). Metaphorical: delivering a message — the angel is the one who delivers the divine communication.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — \"إنما\" restricts the predicate to the subject alone, negating it of all else. Applied in the sermon: \"إنما يتزين لي أوليائي بالذل والخوف\" — My friends adorn themselves for Me ONLY with humility, fear, and submission — restricting their adornment to these alone, excluding all worldly ornament.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "قوله عز وجل رب أرجعون لعلي أعمل صالحا فيما تركت {قال}", "english": "And whoever dies without having performed Hajj despite affluence — his matter is severe with God — Exalted is He. ʿUmar — may God be pleased with him — said: \"I nearly resolved to write to the provinces imposing the jizyah upon whoever did not perform Hajj among those who could find a way to it.\"", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 197", "section_title_en": "The Second Chapter on the Conditions for the Obligation of Hajj, the Validity of Its Pillars, Its Duties, and Its Prohibitions", "section_index": 197, "anchors": "- Root: شرط (sh-r-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A mark, sign, or condition; that which is stipulated and by which something is bound.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): A condition (sharṭ) is that upon which the existence or validity of another thing depends, whether by divine decree or human stipulation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): a sign or token; metaphorical (majāz): a stipulated prerequisite that renders an act contingent upon it.\n- Root: أحرم (a-ḥ-r-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To enter into the sacred state (iḥrām); from ḥarām, that which is protected and forbidden during the pilgrimage.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Iḥrām is the intentional entry into a state wherein certain otherwise permissible acts become prohibited, marking the commencement of pilgrimage rites.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to make something forbidden; metaphorical: the pilgrim's self-consecration by intention and talbiyah, rendering ordinary acts unlawful.\n- Root: استطاع (i-s-t-ṭ-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To have power, capacity, or means over something; istiṭāʿah is the ability to accomplish an act.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Istiṭāʿah is of two kinds: capacity of the self (bodily ability) and capacity of one's wealth (financial means); both are requisite for the obligation of Hajj.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical or financial power; metaphorical: the legal capacity that renders worship obligatory, as in the verse {وَلِلَّهِ عَلَى النَّاسِ حِجُّ الْبَيْتِ مَنِ اسْتَطَاعَ إِلَيْهِ سَبِيلًا}.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Conditional Clauses (Bāb al-sharṭ) — The particle \"fa\" (then) in apodosis clauses marks the consequent as dependent upon the fulfillment of the protasis, as in \"man istatāʿa lazimahu al-ḥajj\" (whoever has the means, Hajj becomes obligatory upon him).", "content_type": "general", "topic": "Classical commentary from Al-Ghazali's Ihya Ulum al-Din, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Commentary"}], "102": [{"arabic": "القيامة وقال تعالى فمن ثقلت موازينه فأولئك هم المفلحون ومن خفت موازينه الآية ووجهها أن الله تعالى يحدث في صحائف الأعمال وزنا بحسب درجات الأعمال عند الله تعالى فتصير مقادير أعمال العباد معلومة للعباد حتى يظهر لهم العدل في العقاب أو الفضل في العفو وتضعيف الثواب الأصل الخامس الصراط وهو جسر ممدود على متن جهنم أرق من الشعرة وأحد من السيف قال الله تعالى فاهدوهم إلى صراط الجحيم وقفوهم إنهم مسئولون وهذا ممكن فيجب التصديق به فإن القادر على أن يطير الطير في الهواء قادر على أن يسير الإنسان على الصراط الأصل السا", "english": "For they, despite their distinction in eloquence and rhetorical perfection, aimed at reviling him, forbidding him, killing him, and expelling him — as God, Mighty and Majestic, informed us about them — yet they were unable to produce anything to rival the Qur'an. For it was not within the capacity of human beings to combine the grandeur of the Qur'an's diction with its harmonious order, especially given what it contains of the accounts of the ancients, while he was illiterate, never having practiced the study of books — and [given] its announcement of the unseen in matters whose truthfulness was verified in the future, such as His saying, Exalted is He: {«لَتَدْخُلُنَّ الْمَسْجِدَ الْحَرَامَ إِنْ شَاءَ اللَّهُ آمِنِينَ مُحَلِّقِينَ رُءُوسَكُمْ وَمُقَصِّرِينَ»} (You shall surely enter the Sacred Mosque, if God wills, secure, with your heads shaved and cut short) [Al-Fatḥ 48:27], and such as His saying: {«الم غُلِبَتِ الرُّومُ فِي أَدْنَى الْأَرْضِ وَهُمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ غَلَبِهِمْ سَيَغْلِبُونَ فِي بِضْعِ سِنِينَ»} (Alif Lām Mīm. The Romans have been defeated in the nearest land, and they, after their defeat, shall be victorious within a few years) [Al-Rūm 30:1-3].", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 96", "section_title_en": "The Fourth Pillar: On Matters of Hearing (al-Samʿiyyāt) and the Verification of the Prophet ﷺ in What He Reported — Comprising Ten Fundamental Principles", "section_index": 96, "anchors": "- Root: حشر (ḥ-sh-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To gather, assemble, drive together; al-ḥashr is the gathering of creation on the Day of Resurrection.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-ḥashr is the bringing together of a people from their various places of dispersal; it is used specifically for the resurrection gathering.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) for driving cattle together; metaphorical (majāz) for the ingathering of souls for judgment.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Conditional Protasis (al-sharṭ) — \"If you are truthful, then rise...\" (fa-in kunta ṣādiqan fa-qum...) — the apodosis (jawāb al-sharṭ) functions as the proof of the protasis, establishing necessary knowledge (ʿilm ḍarūrī).\n\n- Root: نشر (n-sh-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To spread, unfold, revive after death; al-nushūr is the revivification of the dead.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-nashr is the spreading of the soul back into the body after death; it is the opposite of al-ṭayy (folding up).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for unfolding a garment; metaphorical for the resurrection when the earth \"spreads forth\" what it contains.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Coordination (al-ʿaṭf) — \"al-ḥashr wa-l-nashr\" (the gathering and the spreading) — two coordinated nouns forming a single compound concept of resurrection.\n\n- Root: مكن (m-k-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To establish, empower, give firm footing; makkana means to make something firm and stable.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-makān is the place that contains a thing; tamakkun is the firm establishment of a thing in its place.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for physical placement; metaphorical for divine power — \"God is able\" (qādir) means He has full capacity and authority over all things.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Possibility (al-imkān) — \"that is possible\" (dhālika mumkin) — the predicate of possibility is established by the absence of logical contradiction, not by empirical observation.\n\n- Root: عذب (ʿ-dh-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sweet, pleasant (of water); al-ʿadhāb is the punishment that is \"sweet\" to the inflictor but bitter to the recipient — used predominantly for divine chastisement.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-ʿadhāb is the infliction of pain that deters; it is specifically the painful consequence of disobedience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for sweet water; metaphorical (majāz) for punishment that is \"appointed\" as a deterrent.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Exception (al-istithnāʾ) — \"except as God wills\" (illā mā shāʾa) — the exception clause restricts the scope of human perception, not divine action.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "106": [{"arabic": "من ربكم فيقولون ربنا غلبت علينا شقوتنا وكنا قوما ضالين ربنا أخرجنا منها فإن عدنا فإنا ظالمون قال فيجيبهم اخسئوا فيها ولا تكلمون {قال}", "english": "And ʿAlī, may God honor his face, said: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: \"Seek refuge in God from the Pit of Sorrow, or the Valley of Sorrow.\" It was said: \"O Messenger of God, what is the Valley or Pit of Sorrow?\" He said: \"A valley in Hell from which Hell itself seeks refuge with God seventy times each day. God, the Exalted, has prepared it for the reciters who show off.\" (2)", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1349", "section_title_en": "The Vastness of Hellfire, Its Depths, and the Torments of Its Inhabitants", "section_index": 1349, "anchors": "- Root: و د ي (w-d-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A valley; a place where torrents flow. In the lexicon, it denotes a lowland or watercourse between mountains.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Used for the valleys of Hell as places of confinement and passage, each containing further branching ravines (شُعَب), indicating graded descent.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for earthly valleys; here transferred (majāz) to the topographical reality of Hell, affirming its corporeal vastness.\n- Root: ع م ق (ʿ-m-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Depth; the far bottom of a well or pit. Al-Farahidi: \"العمق: قعر البئر ونحوها.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Depth as a measure of descent; in the Hereafter, it is unbounded, paralleling the unbounded depth of worldly desire.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for physical depth; here extended to show the infinite regress of punishment corresponding to infinite craving.\n- Root: غ س ق (gh-s-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Stinking, foul pus or ichor that flows from the skin of the damned; also intense darkness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-Ghassāq is the putrid effusion of Hell, a punishment for the senses, specifically for drinking when the damned cry out in thirst.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for what is loathsome and fetid; in the Quranic context, it is the designated drink of the inhabitants.\n- Root: ز ق م (z-q-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To swallow with difficulty; the tree of Zaqqūm, which grows in the depths of Hell.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): A specific tree in Hell whose fruit is like the heads of devils, eaten by the damned to fill their bellies, causing choking and agony.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for a real tree; its description is sensory, not metaphorical, affirming the physical reality of punishment.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The periodic structure of \"في جهنم سبعين ألف واد\" allows the prepositional phrase to precede the subject for emphasis, establishing the spatial setting before detailing its contents.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "108": [{"arabic": "ولا يغاثون بل يقال لهم اخسئوا فيها ولا تكلمون أولئك الذي اشتروا الحياة الدنيا بالآخرة فلا يخفف عنهم العذاب ولا هم ينصرون وإذا عظمت غوائل الدنيا وشرورها فلا بد أولا من معرفة حقيقة الدنيا وما هي وما الحكمة في خلقها مع عداوتها وما مدخل غرورها وشرورها فإن من لا يعرف الشر لا يتقيه ويوشك أن يقع فيه ونحن نذكر ذم الدنيا وأمثلتها وحقيقتها وتفصيل معانيها وأصناف الأشغال المتعلقة بها ووجه الحاجة إلى أصولها وسبب انصراف الخلق عن الله بسبب التشاغل بفضولها إن شاء الله تعالى وهو المعين على ما يرت", "english": "Praise be to God, who made His friends acquainted with the calamitous snares of this world and its afflictions, and uncovered for them its defects and its shameful parts, until they gazed upon its evidences and its signs, and weighed its evil deeds against its good deeds. Thus they came to know that its objectionable aspects exceed its commendable ones, that what is hoped from it does not fulfill what is feared from it, and that its rising is not safe from its eclipse. Yet it is in the likeness of a comely woman who draws people to herself by her beauty — while within her are hidden secrets of evil, ugly things that destroy those who yearn for union with her. Then she is a fleer from her seekers, niggardly in her turning toward them; and when she does turn, one is not secure from her evil and her pestilence. If she does good for an hour, she does evil for a year; and if she does evil once, she makes it her perpetual habit. So the cycles of her turning are ever more tightly drawn, and the commerce of her sons is losing and ruined, and her afflictions in succession strike the breasts of her seekers, and the courses of her states proclaim the humiliation of those who pursue her. Thus every one deluded by her is destined to abasement, and every one who waxes proud through her proceeds to regret. Her very nature is to flee from her seeker and to be sought by the one who flees from her. Whoever serves her, she eludes; whoever turns away from her, she comes to. Her clarity is never free of the admixture of turbidity, nor is her joy ever disentangled from what embitters it. Her soundness bequeaths sickness, her youth drives on to decrepitude, and her bliss bears no fruit but remorse and regret. She is deceitful, guileful, fleeting, evasive. She never ceases adorning herself for her seekers until, when they have become her lovers, she bares her fangs at them, disorders the ordered strands of their affairs, and uncovers for them the hidden store of her wonders — then she makes them taste the lethal poisons and strikes them with the piercing shafts of her arrows. While her companions are in joy and bounty from her, suddenly she turns away from them as though she were but scattered dreams; then she rushes upon them with her calamities, grinds them as grain is ground, and shrouds them in their winding-sheets beneath the dust. If she has possessed one of them with all that the sun has risen upon, she makes him as reaped harvest, as though he had not dwelt in plenty the day before. Her companions desire joy from her, and she promises them delusion — until they hope for much and build palaces, and then their palaces become graves, their gatherings become ruin, their striving becomes scattered dust, and their cry becomes \"Woe and destruction!\" Such is her description — and the command of God is a decree determined. And the prayer and peace be upon Muhammad, His servant and His Messenger, sent to the worlds as a bringer of glad tidings and a warner, and as an illuminating lamp; and upon those of his family and companions who were for him supporters in the religion, and against the wrongdoers helpers; and abundant peace.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 729", "section_title_en": "The Book of Condemnation of This World — Being the Sixth Book of the Quarter of the Destructive Things from The Revival of the Religious Sciences", "section_index": 729, "anchors": "- Root: لهم (l-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of swallowing, devouring, or ingesting wholly\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الإلهام: casting something into the heart (al-rawʿ), specifically from God or the higher assembly; the root denotes the soul's complete reception, as one who swallows entirely\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: ألهمه الله الخير — God cast good into his heart. Metaphorical: جيش لهم — an army that engulfs and swallows whomever enters it\n- Root: دنا (d-n-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Nearness in place, time, or rank; the world (al-dunyā) is so named because it is nearer and lower, deferred from the Hereafter\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الدنو: proximity by essence or by judgment, used of place, time, and station; al-dunyā is the nearer, lower life\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: هو دانٍ — he is near. Metaphorical: الأدنى — the inferior, the baser, contrasted with what is better\n- Root: حسر (ḥ-s-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Uncovering, stripping away a covering; exhaustion from exposure of one's strength\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الحسر: uncovering what veils; al-ḥasīr: one whose strength is laid bare and spent; al-ḥāsir: the weary whose powers are uncovered as exhausted\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: حسر عن ذراعيه — he bared his forearms. Metaphorical: فلان كريم المحسر — so-and-so is noble when tested/examined (his hidden quality is uncovered)\n- Root: سرر (s-r-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Concealment, the secret kept hidden in the breast; the innermost\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الإسرار: opposite of declaration; al-sirr: the speech concealed in the soul\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: أسر الحديث — he concealed the speech. Metaphorical: أعطيتك سره — I gave you its choicest essence; هو في سر النسب — he is in the pure core of lineage\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of interposing elements between a governing word and its governed prepositional phrase when the interposed element is complete in itself, as in the rhetorical suspensions of this oration", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Afterlife"}, {"arabic": "الزبانية هيهات لات حين أمان ولا خروج لكم من دار الهوان فاخسئوا فيها ولا تكلمون ولو أخرجتم منها لكنتم إلى ما نهيتم عنه تعودون فعند ذلك يقنطون وعلى ما فرطوا في جنب الله يتأسفون ولا ينجيهم الندم ولا يغنيهم الأسف بل يكبون على وجوههم مغلولين النار من فوقهم والنار من تحتهم والنار عن أيمانهم والنار عن شمائلهم فهم غرقى في النار طعامهم نار وشرابهم نار ولباسهم نار ومهادهم نار فهم بين مقطعات النيران وسرابيل القطران وضرب المقامع وثقل السلاسل فهم يتجلجلون في مضايقها ويتحطمون في دركاتها ويضطربون بين غواشيها تغلى بهم النار كغلي ا", "english": "O you who are heedless of your own soul, deluded by what occupies you of the distractions of this world that is on the verge of passing away and perishing — abandon reflection upon that from which you are departing, and turn your thought to your place of arrival (mawrid), for you have been informed that the Fire is a place of arrival for all, since it was said: {«وإن منكم إلا واردها كان على ربك حتما مقضيا ثم ننجي الذين اتقوا ونذر الظالمين فيها جثيا»} [And there is none of you but shall arrive at it — an inevitability decreed upon your Lord. Then We shall save those who were God-fearing, and leave the wrongdoers in it, kneeling]. You are thus in certainty regarding the arrival (wurūd), and in doubt regarding deliverance. So instill within your heart the terror of that place of arrival — perhaps you will thereby prepare yourself for deliverance from it.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1348", "section_title_en": "Discourse on the Attributes of Hellfire, Its Terrors, and Its Fetters", "section_index": 1348, "anchors": "- Root: ورد (w-r-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to arrive at water\" — the primordial act of approaching a watering-place; from this derives the sense of arriving at any destination, whether desired or dreaded.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الورود أصله: قصد الماء\" — its origin is the intent to reach water; then applied generally, and used of the Fire \"على سبيل الفظاعة\" (by way of horrification), as in {وإن منكم إلا واردها} — the universal \"arrival\" at Hell is a divinely decreed certainty.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): ورد الماء — arriving at water to drink. Metaphorical (Majaz): وردت البلد — arriving at a land; ورد عليه أمر لم يطقه — an overwhelming matter descended upon him. Here, \"al-wurūd\" is majaz extended to the eschatological \"arrival\" at the Fire, a passage all must undergo.\n- Root: جلد (j-l-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The skin or hide covering the body; جلد الكتاب — to cover a book with leather; جلدته بالسياط — to flog.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الجلد: قشر البدن\" — the skin is the body's integument; in the Quranic verse {كلما نضجت جلودهم بدلناهم جلودا غيرها} [النساء/56], the renewal of skins is the renewal of the seat of pain, ensuring perpetual sensation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: flaying, covering, stripping. Metaphorical: جلدته على هذا الأمر — compelled him. In this text, the literal sense governs: skins roasted, then replaced, so that torment never ceases.\n- Root: ظلم (z-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Darkness, absence of light; also wrongdoing. The lexicographers note the moral-ontological link: injustice is a kind of darkness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الظلمة: عدم النور\" — darkness is the privation of light; used metaphorically for ignorance, polytheism, and transgression, as light is used for their opposites.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"الظلم ظلمات يوم القيامة\" — wrongdoing is darknesses on the Day of Resurrection. Metaphorical: أرض مظلومة — land never before dug. Here, \"zulumāt\" (ظلمات) is used literally of Hell's physical darknesses, yet these are also the reified shadows of the inmates' own wrongs.\n- Root: نار (n-w-r / n-w-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Fire; the root also yields نار — to kindle, to blaze up. The lexicons record the proverb: ورَيْتُ بك زنادي — \"I struck fire from your flint,\" i.e., I perceived from you good counsel.\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): Fire is the elemental reality of Hell, but its Quranic usage spans both physical flame and the \"Fire of God kindled\" which mounts over hearts [الهمزة/6-7] — the inward fire of heedlessness made outward.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: flame, burning. Metaphorical: نار الحرب — the fire of war. In this passage, the literal fire of Jahannam is described with maximal sensory concreteness, yet it remains the outward manifestation of the soul's own kindled crimes.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the permissibility of interposing elements between a preposition and its object when the intervening phrase is semantically complete. Applied here in the periodic constructions where long descriptive clauses intervene between the initial vocative (يا أيها الغافل) and its eventual predicate, sustaining rhetorical tension across the entire passage.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}], "109": [{"arabic": "غفارا أستغفر الله إنه كان توابا رب اغفر وارحم وأنت خير الراحمين فاغفر لنا وارحمنا وأنت خير الراحمين فاغفر لنا وارحمنا وأنت خير الغافرين ويستحب أن يقرأ قبل غروب الشمس والشمس وضحاها والليل إذا يغشى والمعوذتين ولتغرب الشمس عليه وهو في الاستغفار فإذا سمع الأذان قال اللهم هذا إقبال ليلك وإدبار نهارك وأصوات دعاتك كما سبق ثم يجيب المؤذن ويشتغل بصلاة المغرب وبالغروب قد انتهت أوراد النهار فينبغي أن يلاحظ العبد أحواله ويحاسب نفسه فقد انقضى من طريقه مرحلة فإن ساوى يومه أمسه فيكون مغبونا وإن كان شرا منه فيكون معلونا فقد قال صلى الله عليه وسلم لا بورك لي", "english": "I say: And just as knowledge and remembrance are the nourishment of the heart, which can never be cut off from it, so too the measure of moderation is this; and deficiency from it may lead to the disturbance of the body — except for one who accustoms himself to wakefulness gradually, for he may train himself upon it without disturbance. And this litany (wird) is among the longest of the litanies and the most delightful for the worshippers, and it is one of the \"evenings and mornings\" (al-āṣāl) which God Most High mentioned when He said: {«ولله يسجد من في السموات والأرض طوعا وكرها وظلالهم بالغدو والآصال»} \"And to God prostrates whoever is in the heavens and the earth, willingly or unwillingly, and their shadows, in the mornings and the evenings\" (Q. 13:15). And when inanimate objects prostrate to God, Mighty and Majestic, how then can the intelligent servant be heedless of the varieties of worship?", "book_title": "Arrangement of Litanies", "section_title_ar": "Section 261", "section_title_en": "The Seventh Daily Litany (Al-Wird al-Sābiʿ): The Time of Sunset — The Completion of the Day's Litanies and Self-Accounting", "section_index": 261, "anchors": "- Root: ورد (warada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes arriving at water, seeking it, and drinking from it; also the portion or share of water allotted for drinking at a specific time.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الورود أصله: قصد الماء\" — the core meaning is the intentional approach to water; the litany (wird) is the appointed portion of devotional recitation, likened to the daily share of water that a person must take to sustain life.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): the act of arriving at water. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"ورد علي كتاب سرني مورده\" — a book arrived upon me whose arrival pleased me; likewise, the wird is the arrival of the appointed time for worship, and the soul's approach to the fountain of divine remembrance.\n- Root: عصر (ʿaṣr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes pressing, squeezing, and extracting; also the time of afternoon, so called because it is the pressing of the day's end.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The ʿaṣr is the latter part of the day; God swore by it in {«والعصر»} (Q. 103:1), indicating its great merit.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the afternoon time. Metaphorical: the pressing of one's life — as the day is pressed at its end, so the life of man is pressed at its term.\n- Root: غرب (gharaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes disappearance, absence, and setting; the sun's setting (ghurūb) is its concealment from the eye.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The setting of the sun marks the boundary between the day's works and the night's; it is the terminus of the visible world's diurnal cycle.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the sun's descent below the horizon. Metaphorical: the setting of the sun of life — the death of man, after which there is no rising.\n- Root: حاسب (ḥāsaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes reckoning, counting, and taking account; muḥāsabah is the mutual reckoning between two parties.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-accounting (muḥāsabat al-nafs) is the soul's reckoning with itself over its deeds before the Day of Reckoning, for \"one who reckons himself before he is reckoned\" is among the wise.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the arithmetic counting of sums. Metaphorical: the soul's audit of its own works, weighing the day's deeds against the standard of the Sacred Law.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the separation of the prepositional phrase from its governing verb, as in the construction \"فسبحان الله حين تمسون\" where the adverbial clause is interposed to emphasize the temporal specification of the act of glorification.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Arrangement of Litanies'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "113": [{"arabic": "شيء يسير فقال له عمر حسبك بصحبته يوما أو بعض يوم شؤما وشرا وقال الفضيل ما ازداد رجل من ذي سلطان قربا إلا ازداد من الله بعدا وكان سعيد بن المسيب يتجر في الزيت ويقول إن في هذا لغنى عن هؤلاء السلاطين وقال وهيب هؤلاء الذين يدخلون على الملوك لهم أضر على الأمة من المقامرين وقال محمد بن سلمة الذباب على العذرة أحسن من قارئ على باب هؤلاء ولما خالط الزهري السلطان كتب أخ له في الدين إليه عافانا الله وإياك أبا بكر من الفتن فقد أصبحت بحال ينبغي لمن عرفك أن يدعوا لك الله ويرحمك أصبحت شيخا كبيرا قد أثقلتك", "english": "As for the reports: When the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, described the tyrannical rulers, he said: \"Whoever opposes them is saved, whoever withdraws from them is safe — or nearly safe — and whoever falls in with them in their worldly affairs is of them.\" (1)", "book_title": "The Lawful and Prohibited", "section_title_ar": "Section 389", "section_title_en": "The Sixth Chapter: On What is Lawful and Unlawful in Associating with Tyrannical Rulers, and the Ruling on Frequent Their Assemblies, Entering Upon Them, and Showing Them Honor", "section_index": 389, "anchors": "- Root: ظلم (Ẓ-L-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is placing something in other than its proper place; darkness as the absence of light.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الظلمة: عدم النور\" — darkness is the absence of light; used metaphorically for ignorance, polytheism, and transgression, as opposed to light (نور) which represents knowledge and justice.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"الظلم ظلمة كما أن العدل نور\" — injustice is darkness just as justice is light. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"أرض مظلومة\" — land never dug or watered, indicating the deprivation of benefit.\n- Root: وهي (W-H-Y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Weakness, fissure, or slackening in a structure; a crack in leather or a wall.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوهي: شق في الأديم والثوب\" — a rent in hide or cloth; \"وكل شيء استرخى رباطه فقد وهي\" — anything whose binding slackens has become weak.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"وهى الحائط\" — the wall cracked. Metaphorical: \"واهي العزالي\" — clouds pouring forth rain, i.e., their water-skins have burst open.\n- Root: خبر (KHB-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Knowledge of a thing from report or direct experience; testing and ascertaining.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخبر: العلم بالأشياء المعلومة من جهة الخبر\" — knowledge of things from the channel of report; \"الخبرة المعرفة ببواطن الأمر\" — experiential knowledge of the inner realities of affairs.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"خبرت الرجل واختبرته خبرا وخبرة\" — I tested the man and gained knowledge of him. Metaphorical: \"تخبر عن مجهوله مرآته\" — one's mirror reveals his unknown state.\n- Root: وفي (W-F-Y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Completeness, fulfillment, and reaching full measure.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوافي: الذي بلغ التمام\" — that which has attained completion; \"أوفى: إذا تمم العهد ولم ينقض حفظه\" — fulfilled the covenant and did not breach its preservation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"درهم واف\" — a full-weight dirham. Metaphorical: \"أوفى على المائة\" — he surpassed a hundred; \"توفاه الله\" — God took him in full, i.e., completed his term.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Governing the exclusive restriction of predication, as in \"فليس مني ولست منه\" (he is not of me, nor am I of him), where the negation establishes complete disassociation.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'The Lawful and Prohibited'", "badge": "Insight"}], "116": [{"arabic": "في الجنة ولا أبالي وهؤلاء في النار ولا أبالي (1) فتعالى الله الملك الحق لا يسئل عما يفعل وهم يسئلون ولنقتصر على هذا القدر اليسير من ذكر عجائب القلب فإن استقصاءه لا يليق بعلم المعاملة وإنما ذكرنا منه ما يحتاج إليه لمعرفة أغوار علوم المعاملة وأسرارها لينتفع بها من لا يقنع بالظواهر ولا يجتزئ بالقشر عن اللباب بل يتشوق إلى معرفة دقائق حقائق الأسباب وفيما ذكرناه كفاية له ومقنع إن شاء الله تعالى والله ولي التوفيق تم كتاب عجائب القلب ولله الحمد والمنة ويتلوه كتاب رياضة النفس وتهذيب الأخلاق والحمد لله وحده وصلى الله على ك", "english": "I say: Know that when the Shayṭān prevails and the heart inclines to its own kind among the parties of the Shayṭān, turning away from the party of God—Exalted is He—and His saints, and aiding the party of the Shayṭān and His enemies, then there flows upon its bodily members, by virtue of the prior decree, that which is the cause of its distance from God—Exalted is He. But if the angelic attributes are predominant over the heart, the heart does not lend its ear to the Shayṭān's seduction, nor to his incitement toward the immediate world, nor to his belittling of the matter of the Hereafter. Rather, it inclines to the party of God—Exalted is He—and obedience appears upon its members in accordance with what has preceded in the decree. Thus, the heart of the believer is between two fingers of the fingers of the All-Merciful—that is, between the mutual tugging of these two hosts—and this is the meaning of \"turning\" (taqlīb): the transition from one party to another. As for perpetual constancy, whether with the party of the angels or with the party of the Shayṭān, that is rare on both sides. These acts of obedience and disobedience emerge from the treasuries of the Unseen into the world of witness by way of the treasury of the heart, for it is among the treasuries of the Malakūt. And when they do emerge, they become signs by which the masters of hearts recognize the prior decree. For whomever is created for Paradise, the means of obedience are made easy for him; and whomever is created for the Fire, the means of disobedience are made easy for him, and evil companions are given sway over him, and the rule of the Shayṭān is cast into his heart. For by various kinds of deceptions he beguiles the foolish, saying: \"God is Merciful, so do not care\"; and \"All people do not fear God, so do not oppose them\"; and \"Life is long, so be patient until you repent tomorrow.\" He promises them and fills them with desires—{يَعِدُهُمْ وَيُمَنِّيهِمْ وَمَا يَعِدُهُمُ الشَّيْطَانُ إِلَّا غُرُورًا} \"He promises them and fills them with desires, and the Shayṭān promises them nothing but delusion\" {النساء/120}. He promises them repentance and fills them with hopes of forgiveness, and thus destroys them, by God's permission, through these ruses and what runs in their course. So he expands their hearts to accept delusion and constricts them from accepting the truth. And all of that is by God's decree and destiny: {فَمَن يُرِدِ اللَّهُ أَن يَهْدِيَهُ يَشْرَحْ صَدْرَهُ لِلْإِسْلَامِ وَمَن يُرِدْ أَن يُضِلَّهُ يَجْعَلْ صَدْرَهُ ضَيِّقًا حَرَجًا كَأَنَّمَا يَصَّعَّدُ فِي السَّمَاءِ} \"Whomsoever God wishes to guide, He expands his breast to Islam; and whomsoever He wishes to lead astray, He makes his breast narrow and constricted, as though he were climbing into the sky\" {الأنعام/125}. {إِن يَنصُرْكُمُ اللَّهُ فَلَا غَالِبَ لَكُمْ وَإِن يَخْذُلْكُمْ فَمَن ذَا الَّذِي يَنصُرُكُم مِّن بَعْدِهِ} \"If God helps you, none can overcome you; and if He forsakes you, who is there to help you after Him?\" {آل عمران/160}. He is the Guide and the Misleader; He does what He wills and decrees what He desires. There is no repeller of His judgment and no reverser of His decree. He created Paradise and created for it its people, and employed them in obedience; and He created the Fire and created for it its people, and employed them in disobedience. And He made known to creation the mark of the people of Paradise and the people of the Fire, saying: {إِنَّ الْأَبْرَارَ لَفِي نَعِيمٍ وَإِنَّ الْفُجَّارَ لَفِي جَحِيمٍ} \"Verily, the righteous shall be in bliss, and verily, the wicked shall be in the Blazing Fire\" {الانفطار/13-14}. Then He—Exalted is He—said, as related from His Prophet, peace be upon him: \"These are in Paradise, and I do not care; and these are in the Fire, and I do not care.\" Exalted is God, the King, the Truth! {لَا يُسْأَلُ عَمَّا يَفْعَلُ وَهُمْ يُسْأَلُونَ} \"He is not questioned about what He does, but they shall be questioned\" {الأنبياء/23}.", "book_title": "Disciplining the Soul", "section_title_ar": "Section 611", "section_title_en": "The Explanation of the Heart's Rapid Fluctuation and the Division of Hearts Between Change and Constancy", "section_index": 611, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of turning, overturning, and revolving a thing from one face to another; the heart is so named for its constant oscillation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart's essence is its perpetual turning and redirection; its fluctuation is not accidental but constitutive of its nature.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): physical overturning. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — the heart's movement between the two hosts (jundān) is a real, dynamic turnover of allegiance.\n- Root: شيط (sh-y-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Burning, scorching, or spoiling meat by over-smoking; blood going to waste.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The Shayṭān is so named for his being burned and banished from mercy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Metaphorical: \"شاط دمه إذا بطل\" — the Shayṭān's work is to render the servant's spiritual life wasted and nullified, as blood spilled in vain.\n- Root: حزب (ḥ-z-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A group or party with cohesion and strength; al-Ḥayzabūn (the old woman) shows the root's extension to a gathered mass.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحزب: جماعة فيها غلظ\" — the party of God (ḥizb Allāh) versus the party of the Shayṭān (ḥizb al-Shayṭān) are two opposing, organized camps in cosmic conflict.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a band of partisans. Metaphorical: \"قرأ حزبه من القرآن\" — a portion assigned; here, the heart is assigned to one of the two parties by divine decree.\n- Root: خلق (kh-l-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Measuring and smoothing; creating as precise estimation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخلق أصله: التقدير المستقيم\" — creation is origination upon a just measure; God's creative act entails the pre-ordainment of ends.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: shaping leather before cutting. Metaphorical: \"خلق الله الخلق: أوجده على تقدير أوجبته الحكمة\" — God's creation of Paradise and Hellfire and their inhabitants is an act of wise, pre-eternal determination.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The phrase \"إنما ذكرنا منه ما يحتاج إليه\" restricts the author's purpose: only the necessary measure of the heart's wonders is expounded, not exhaustive treatment, signaling a deliberate boundary of the science of practical religion (ʿilm al-muʿāmalah).", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Disciplining the Soul'", "badge": "Insight"}], "117": [{"arabic": "الله ولقوله ولا تجعلوا مع الله إلها آخر فمن لم يره في كل شيء فقد رأى غيره وكل ما التفت إليه العبد سوى الله تعالى تضمن التفاته شيئا من الشرك الخفي بل التوحيد الخالص أن لا يرى في كل شيء إلا الله عز وجل العاشر التبري وأعني به أن يتبرأ من حوله وقوته والالتفات إلى نفسه بين الرضا والتزكية فإذا تلا بآيات الوعد والمدح للصالحين فلا يشهد نفسه عند ذلك بل يشهد الموقنين والصديقين فيها ويتشوف إلى أن يلحقه الله عز وجل بهم وإذا تلا آيات المقت وذم العصاة والمقصرين شهد على نفسه هناك وقدر أنه المخا", "english": "For this reason, Yūsuf ibn Asbāṭ said: \"I intend to recite the Qur'an, but when I recall what is in it, I fear abhorrence (al-maqt), so I turn instead to glorification (al-tasbīḥ) and seeking forgiveness (al-istighfār).\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of Pride and Conceit", "section_title_ar": "Section 231", "section_title_en": "** The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 231: On the Inward Conditions of Qur'anic Recitation (Continued)", "section_index": 231, "anchors": "**\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the stirring of fear/apprehension within\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف. يقال: وجل يوجل وجلا، فهو وجل. قال تعالى: {إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم}\" — the theological state of the heart's trembling upon remembrance of God\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال، وقد وجل وجلا\" — the physical/emotional state of fear. Metaphorical (Majaz): The heart's trembling as the seat of spiritual perception\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — turning and transforming\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه، كقلب الثوب، وقلب الإنسان\" — the heart as that which is constantly turning/transforming between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" — physical turning. Metaphorical: \"قلب المعلم الصبيان: صرفهم إلى بيوتهم\" — the heart's turning toward or away from spiritual realities\n- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, spirit — that which is breathed\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح\" — the soul as the animating principle; also the self as the locus of blameworthy attributes\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"شيء نفيس ومنفس\" — preciousness. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — the self as that which must be transcended in spiritual realization\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise delimitation of spiritual states (أحوال) and their hierarchical gradation (درجات)\n\n---\n\n#", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Pride and Conceit'", "badge": "Insight"}], "118": [{"arabic": "غفارا أستغفر الله إنه كان توابا رب اغفر وارحم وأنت خير الراحمين فاغفر لنا وارحمنا وأنت خير الراحمين فاغفر لنا وارحمنا وأنت خير الغافرين ويستحب أن يقرأ قبل غروب الشمس والشمس وضحاها والليل إذا يغشى والمعوذتين ولتغرب الشمس عليه وهو في الاستغفار فإذا سمع الأذان قال اللهم هذا إقبال ليلك وإدبار نهارك وأصوات دعاتك كما سبق ثم يجيب المؤذن ويشتغل بصلاة المغرب وبالغروب قد انتهت أوراد النهار فينبغي أن يلاحظ العبد أحواله ويحاسب نفسه فقد انقضى من طريقه مرحلة فإن ساوى يومه أمسه فيكون مغبونا وإن كان شرا منه فيكون معلونا", "english": "I say: And just as knowledge and remembrance are the nourishment of the heart, which can never be cut off from it, so too the measure of moderation is this; and deficiency from it may lead to the disturbance of the body — except for one who accustoms himself to wakefulness gradually, for he may train himself upon it without disturbance. And this litany (wird) is among the longest of the litanies and the most delightful for the worshippers, and it is one of the \"evenings and mornings\" (al-āṣāl) which God Most High mentioned when He said: {«ولله يسجد من في السموات والأرض طوعا وكرها وظلالهم بالغدو والآصال»} \"And to God prostrates whoever is in the heavens and the earth, willingly or unwillingly, and their shadows, in the mornings and the evenings\" (Q. 13:15). And when inanimate objects prostrate to God, Mighty and Majestic, how then can the intelligent servant be heedless of the varieties of worship?", "book_title": "Arrangement of Litanies", "section_title_ar": "Section 261", "section_title_en": "The Seventh Daily Litany (Al-Wird al-Sābiʿ): The Time of Sunset — The Completion of the Day's Litanies and Self-Accounting", "section_index": 261, "anchors": "- Root: ورد (warada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes arriving at water, seeking it, and drinking from it; also the portion or share of water allotted for drinking at a specific time.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الورود أصله: قصد الماء\" — the core meaning is the intentional approach to water; the litany (wird) is the appointed portion of devotional recitation, likened to the daily share of water that a person must take to sustain life.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): the act of arriving at water. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"ورد علي كتاب سرني مورده\" — a book arrived upon me whose arrival pleased me; likewise, the wird is the arrival of the appointed time for worship, and the soul's approach to the fountain of divine remembrance.\n- Root: عصر (ʿaṣr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes pressing, squeezing, and extracting; also the time of afternoon, so called because it is the pressing of the day's end.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The ʿaṣr is the latter part of the day; God swore by it in {«والعصر»} (Q. 103:1), indicating its great merit.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the afternoon time. Metaphorical: the pressing of one's life — as the day is pressed at its end, so the life of man is pressed at its term.\n- Root: غرب (gharaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes disappearance, absence, and setting; the sun's setting (ghurūb) is its concealment from the eye.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The setting of the sun marks the boundary between the day's works and the night's; it is the terminus of the visible world's diurnal cycle.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the sun's descent below the horizon. Metaphorical: the setting of the sun of life — the death of man, after which there is no rising.\n- Root: حاسب (ḥāsaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes reckoning, counting, and taking account; muḥāsabah is the mutual reckoning between two parties.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-accounting (muḥāsabat al-nafs) is the soul's reckoning with itself over its deeds before the Day of Reckoning, for \"one who reckons himself before he is reckoned\" is among the wise.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the arithmetic counting of sums. Metaphorical: the soul's audit of its own works, weighing the day's deeds against the standard of the Sacred Law.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the separation of the prepositional phrase from its governing verb, as in the construction \"فسبحان الله حين تمسون\" where the adverbial clause is interposed to emphasize the temporal specification of the act of glorification.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Arrangement of Litanies'", "badge": "Du'a"}]}, "wordTiming": {"abdulbasit": {"1": [[1, 350, 900], [2, 900, 1866], [3, 1866, 3685]], "2": [[1, 350, 1723], [2, 1723, 2185], [3, 2185, 2792], [4, 2792, 4444], [5, 4444, 6062]], "3": [[1, 350, 2012], [2, 2012, 2454], [3, 2454, 2965], [4, 2965, 4278], [5, 4278, 5905]], "4": [[1, 350, 2023], [2, 